/* Chapter 4 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":4,"title":"The Philosophy and Features of Islamic Finance","part":"part-i","partRoman":"I","pages":[73,98],"minutes":65,"estimates":{"f1":65,"f2":80},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"intermediate","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch4-1","text":"Explain why Islamic banking is wider than “interest-free banking”.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-2","text":"Distinguish a loan from an investment in the Islamic context.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-3","text":"Classify Islamic financing tools by liquidity, guarantee and rate of return (Table 4.1).","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-4","text":"Explain why not all pre-fixed returns are Riba, and the role of benchmarks.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-5","text":"Apply the maxim Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman / Al Ghunm bil Ghurm.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-6","text":"Explain exchange rules, the time value of money and the status of paper money in Islamic finance.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-7","text":"Discuss money creation, indexation and settlement of debts under currency fluctuation.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-8","text":"Explain why 'interest-free banking' is a narrower concept than 'Islamic banking', and state what makes a transaction a genuine 'investment' in the Islamic framework","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-9","text":"Explain how Islamic banks operationally avoid Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar in their products","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-10","text":"List the major Islamic financing modes (Mudarabah, Musharakah, Murabaha-Mu'ajjal, Salam, Ijarah, Istisna'a) and classify them by liquidity, guarantee and rate-of-return predictability","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-11","text":"State the Shari'ah maxim 'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' / 'Al Ghunm bil Ghurm' and explain its role as the criterion for legality of return on capital","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-12","text":"Explain why Islamic banks are said to 'deal in goods, not money' and what this implies for their risk profile versus conventional banks","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-13","text":"Explain the debt-versus-equity issue in Islamic finance and why debt-creating modes remain legitimate, not just equity-based ones","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-14","text":"State the Shari'ah exchange rules for currencies and commodities, and explain the concept of time value of money in Islamic finance as distinct from conventional opportunity cost/interest","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch4-15","text":"Explain the Islamic perspective on the status of paper money, money creation, and inflation-linked debt settlement","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"This chapter translates the prohibitions into operating principles for Islamic banks: asset-backed transactions, profit only with risk, dealing in goods rather than money, stricter transparency and extra risks. It also resolves common myths — fixed returns, time value of money, debt vs equity and ‘benevolent’ banking — that recur in exams and in Chapter 17.","whyExpanded":"This chapter translates the prohibitions and ethics from Chapter 3 into the operating philosophy of actual Islamic financial institutions — what it means, in practice, for a bank to avoid Riba/Gharar/Maisir while still being a real business (not a charity), how profit entitlement is tied to risk-bearing, and how money, pricing and time value work differently than in conventional finance. It is the direct conceptual bridge to Part II (contract law) and Part III (specific financing products).","overview":"Islamic finance avoids interest, Gharar and gambling and uses alternative principles — sharing, deferred trading and leasing. Returns must be linked to risk and real assets; Islamic banks deal in goods, not money; they face additional risks. The chapter then discusses debt versus equity, business versus benevolence, exchange rules, time value of money, and money and monetary policy, including paper money, currency trading, money creation and indexation.","summary":"Islamic finance requires institutions and clients to follow Islamic jurisprudence, avoiding Riba, Gharar and Qimar. Not all gains on capital are prohibited — only increases stipulated over the principal of loans or debts. Profit is the reward of capital, but capital bears the risk of loss; transactions must be associated with goods, services or benefits. Islamic banks deal in goods, share risk and handle physical assets, facing unique risks. Currency exchange must follow the Sarf rules. Time value is recognised only in the pricing of sale and lease contracts: a credit price can exceed the cash price if fixed before finalisation, but a created debt cannot increase. Lending is a virtuous act, not a business; Islamic banking is a business, and while Shirkah modes are preferable, trade and lease modes are also permissible.","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 4 translates the prohibitions of Chapter 3 into the operating philosophy of Islamic finance: all transactions must be asset-backed, with a precise distinction between genuine 'investment' (tied to real activity, risk-contingent) and mere 'financial transactions' like loans (guaranteed, no return). It walks through how Islamic banks operationally avoid Riba, Gharar and Maisir; surveys the major financing modes (Mudarabah, Musharakah, Murabaha, Salam, Ijarah, Istisna'a) and their liquidity/risk profiles; and grounds profit entitlement in the maxim 'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' (no gain without bearing loss liability). It corrects two persistent myths — that Islamic finance must be equity-only, and that Islamic banks should function as charities — and details the Shari'ah exchange rules, the concept of time value of money as expressed only through genuine trade/lease pricing (never interest), and the Islamic perspective on paper money, money creation, and the strict rule against indexing debts to inflation.","summarySection":"4.8","takeaways":["Loan ≠ investment; only real-activity investment earns return.","Table 4.1: liquidity, guarantee and return differ across modes.","Fixed returns are allowed in trade and Ijarah, never on loans/debts.","Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman: profit follows liability.","Islamic banks deal in goods, not money; face extra asset, Shari’ah and withdrawal risks.","Only one counter-value may be deferred; currencies exchange spot.","Time value via pricing goods only; no indexation of debts.","Debt modes are permissible; equity preferred — preference, not prohibition.","'Islamic banking' is broader than 'interest-free banking': it must also avoid Gharar and other unethical practices while actively serving Islamic economic goals; a loan (guaranteed, no return) is never 'investment' — only funds tied to real activity qualify.","The major financing modes (Mudarabah, Musharakah, Murabaha-Mu'ajjal, Salam, Ijarah, Istisna'a) differ systematically in liquidity, collateral treatment and return predictability, as summarized in the chapter's Table 4.1.","'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' / 'Al Ghunm bil Ghurm' (no gain without bearing loss liability) is the universal Shari'ah test for the legality of any return on capital, applied differently across loans, trade, Ijarah and Shirkah.","'All Pre-fixed Returns are not Riba': the Shari'ah test is the nature of the transaction (sale/lease vs loan/debt), not whether the return is fixed — enabling Islamic banks to use interest-rate benchmarks merely as pricing references without becoming un-Islamic.","Islamic banks 'deal in goods and documents, not money' (unlike conventional banks, which 'deal in documents, not goods') — they must take genuine asset ownership and bear real risk, which is the source of their distinctive additional risks (asset, Shari'ah non-compliance, rate-of-return, legal, withdrawal).","Debt is not eliminated in Islamic finance — the real issue is a preference for equity over debt where suitable, plus ensuring debt, once created, can never increase like conventional interest-bearing debt.","Islamic banks are ordinary profit-seeking businesses with fiduciary duties to depositors, not charities; charitable activity must be funded separately from equity or a dedicated Charity Fund.","Time value of money is recognized only through genuine trade/lease pricing (credit price may exceed cash price) — never as a direct charge on a loan/debt; debts must be repaid in their exact original currency/amount regardless of inflation, since indexation is rejected as contrary to the clear Qur'anic text (Nass, 2:279)."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you explain why a bond purchase is not ‘investment’?","Can you reproduce Table 4.1?","Can you explain why fixed Ijarah rent is not interest?","Can you state and apply Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman?","Can you list the additional risks of Islamic banks and how they are mitigated?","Can you explain how forward FX cover can be provided Islamically?","Can you explain time value of money in Islamic finance?","Can you state the OIC ruling on indexation and the due-date rule?"],"topics":[{"id":"t4.1","section":"4.1","title":"Introduction: Islamic Banking vs “Interest-free Banking”","pages":[73,73],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["islamic-banking"],"intuition":"Is Islamic banking just conventional banking minus interest? No — the author calls that view too narrow.","simpleExplanation":"“Interest-free banking” (a 1980s term) only means avoiding interest. Islamic banking also avoids Gharar and unethical practices and aims to achieve the goals of an Islamic economy.","academicExplanation":["Islamic banking is banking in consonance with the ethos and value system of Islam, governed by conventional good governance and risk management rules plus Shari’ah principles. “Interest-free banking” is a narrow concept denoting instruments that avoid interest; Islamic banking should also avoid Gharar and other unethical practices and participate in achieving the objectives of an Islamic economy."],"examExplanation":"Interest-free banking = narrow (avoid interest). Islamic banking = avoid interest + Gharar + unethical practices + serve goals of Islamic economy.","keyPoints":["Islamic banking is a more general term than interest-free banking."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Interest-free but not Islamic","kind":"practice","text":"A bank offers a “zero-interest” product whose return comes from a prize draw among depositors. It avoids interest, yet it involves Maisir. That is why the book treats Islamic banking as broader than “interest-free banking”: it must also avoid Gharar and unethical practice (p. 73)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.2","t8.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why does the author consider ‘interest-free banking’ a narrow concept?","options":["It ignores deposits","It only denotes avoiding interest, not Gharar, unethical practices and the goals of an Islamic economy","It is a Western term","It includes Takaful"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 73."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.1","sectionTitle":"Introduction / The Philosophy of Islamic Finance","title":"The Philosophy of Islamic Finance: Asset-Backing and the Definition of 'Investment'","covers":["t4.1"],"overview":"The author distinguishes the narrower 'interest-free banking' from the broader 'Islamic banking', and lays out the philosophy's core: all financial transactions must be tied to real, asset-backed activity, with a precise definition of what counts as a genuine 'investment' versus a mere financial/monetary transaction.","simpleExplanation":"In the 1980s people called this 'interest-free banking', but that's too narrow — Islamic banking must also avoid Gharar (excessive uncertainty) and other unethical practices, and actively support the goals of an Islamic economy, not just avoid interest. The core idea: money on its own can't earn a return — it must be tied to a real transaction (buying/selling goods, leasing, or a real business). So lending money and just getting it back with interest is not 'investment' at all; buying goods to resell at a profit, or buying a real asset, is investment. Buying a government bond or financial paper doesn't count as investment either, since no real activity is involved — it's just a financial transaction.","academicExplanation":"The author distinguishes 1980s-era 'interest-free banking' (a narrow concept describing instruments avoiding interest) from the broader 'Islamic banking', which must also avoid Gharar and other unethical practices while actively participating in achieving an Islamic economy's goals. The philosophy rests on the Riba-prohibition/trade-permission pairing in Qur'an 2:275, driving financial activity toward asset-backed businesses/transactions — all financial transactions must represent real transactions or the sale of goods, services or benefits. Riba is defined operationally as a premium taken from the debtor: either the return on money-for-money exchange, or a delay-based addition to an agreed sale/debt price; it is prohibited for generating economic imbalances. A key structural implication: debt contracts cannot be sold at a premium/discount, and exchanges of money or money-representing goods (gold, silver) must be equal-for-equal and hand-to-hand — the 'hand to hand' requirement specifically prevents one party benefiting from using received money/currency before giving its counter-value, which would let them benefit from something the other party hasn't yet received value from. The author gives a precise, operational definition of 'investment': a loan is a purely monetary/financial transaction (funds exchange hands, full repayment guaranteed, no return to the creditor) and is NOT investment. Investment requires becoming part of real activity or being itself a real activity — so purchasing a government/corporate bond, or depositing into a conventional bank (a loan), is not investment (merely a financial transaction, no real activity); but using funds to purchase real goods/services and reselling at a profit IS investment. Using interest-borrowed funds to buy/build a physical asset remains impermissible (since the underlying financing itself is Riba-tainted), and buying/selling a financial document (with no real activity by the holder) is also not investment. Loan exchanges must be of equal amounts: fungible goods (e.g. currency) must be repaid exactly in kind; nonfungible goods are valued in money terms; excess payment on exchange of two similar goods is prohibited even in a sale (not just a loan) — this excess is Riba. Currency notes represent Thaman (price/monetary value), and trading in Thaman is usurious except when exchanged hand-to-hand and equal-for-equal (for similar currencies). The chapter also flags avoidance of excessive risk-taking/games of chance as part of this philosophy, requiring certainty about subject matter/exchange value, transparency, disclosure and free consent.","examExplanation":"'Interest-free banking' (1980s term) is narrower than 'Islamic banking' (must also avoid Gharar, other unethical practices, and actively support Islamic economic goals). Core philosophy: financial transactions must be asset-backed/represent real transactions (Qur'an 2:275). Key operational definition: a loan (funds transfer, guaranteed full repayment, no return) is NOT investment; investment requires real activity (buying real goods/services and reselling at profit) — buying bonds or making conventional bank deposits (both loans) is not investment; buying/selling a financial document (no real activity) is also not investment.","keyPoints":["'Interest-free banking' (narrow, avoids only interest) is distinct from the broader 'Islamic banking' (also avoids Gharar and other unethical practices, and actively supports Islamic economic goals).","The core philosophy, grounded in Qur'an 2:275, drives financial activity toward asset-backed businesses — all financial transactions must represent real transactions or the sale of goods/services/benefits.","A loan is a purely monetary/financial transaction (funds change hands, full repayment guaranteed, no return to the creditor) and is explicitly NOT considered 'investment' in the Islamic framework.","Investment requires becoming part of real activity or being itself real activity: buying real goods/services and reselling at a profit qualifies; buying a bond, making a conventional bank deposit, or trading a financial document (no real activity involved) does not.","In loan/exchange transactions, equal amounts must be exchanged: fungible goods (e.g. currency) are repaid exactly in kind; excess payment on exchange of similar goods is Riba even within a sale transaction, not only a loan.","The 'hand to hand' requirement for money/monetary-unit exchange exists specifically to prevent one party from benefiting from currency received before giving its counter-value, which would let them profit from something not yet reciprocated."],"definitions":[{"term":"Investment (Islamic framework)","definition":"A use of funds that becomes part of real economic activity or is itself real activity (e.g. purchasing real goods/services for resale at a profit) — as distinct from a purely monetary/financial transaction like a loan or bond purchase, which does not qualify as investment."},{"term":"Thaman","definition":"Price — the monetary value of a commodity in an exchange; currency notes represent Thaman, and trading in Thaman is usurious except when exchanged hand-to-hand and equal-for-equal."}],"conditions":["Exchange of money or money-representing goods (gold, silver) must be equal for equal and hand to hand.","In a loan, if the borrowed item is fungible, exactly its like must be repaid; excess on exchange of similar goods is Riba even in a sale transaction."],"principles":["All financial transactions and instruments must be representative of real transactions or the sale of goods, services or benefits — the asset-backing principle at the core of Islamic finance philosophy."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often conflate 'lending money' with 'investing money' — Chapter 4 explicitly distinguishes them: a loan (guaranteed repayment, no return) is never investment; only funds deployed into real economic activity (with risk) count as investment."],"distinctions":["'Interest-free banking' (narrow: avoiding only interest) vs 'Islamic banking' (broader: avoiding interest, Gharar, and other unethical practices, plus actively supporting Islamic economic goals).","A loan/financial transaction (guaranteed return of principal, no permissible return) vs an investment (tied to real activity, entitled to profit but exposed to risk of loss)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Qard","Bai' al Sarf","Asset-backed finance"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.1","pages":[73,74]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Interest-free banking","b":"Islamic banking","text":"The former only avoids interest; the latter also avoids Gharar and unethical practices and pursues the objectives of an Islamic economy."}]},{"id":"t4.2","section":"4.2","title":"The Philosophy of Islamic Finance: Loan vs Investment","pages":[73,74],"tier":"core","concepts":["asset-backing","riba"],"intuition":"If you buy a government bond or make a deposit in a conventional bank, have you ‘invested’? Not in the Islamic sense.","simpleExplanation":"Verse 2:275 (trade permitted, Riba forbidden) pushes finance towards asset-backed transactions. A loan is a purely financial transaction that must be repaid in full without return. Investment means taking part in real activity — buying goods to sell at a profit, for example. Returns on loans are prohibited; returns on investment are allowed.","academicExplanation":["All financial transactions must represent real transactions — sale of goods, services or benefits. Riba is an increase taken as a premium from the debtor: the return on exchanging money for money, or an addition to an agreed price on account of delay. Hence debt contracts cannot be sold at a premium or discount, and exchange of money (or gold/silver) must be equal for equal and hand to hand — otherwise one party benefits from money received before giving its counter value.","A loan is only a monetary transaction with a guarantee of full repayment and no return; it is not an investment. Investment must be part of, or itself be, real activity. Purchasing a bond or making a conventional deposit is not investment; using funds to buy goods and sell them at a profit is. Borrowing on interest to buy a physical asset is not permissible; trading a financial document is not investment. If fungible items are borrowed (1 ton of wheat, $1000), exactly the same must be returned. Certainty about subject matter and exchange value, transparency, disclosure and free consent are key."],"examExplanation":"2:275 → asset-backed finance. Loan = monetary transaction, full repayment, no return, not investment. Investment = participation in real activity; its return is permissible. Debts cannot be sold at premium/discount; money exchange equal and spot.","keyPoints":["All financial transactions must represent real transactions.","Loans are not investments.","Bonds and conventional deposits are financial transactions, not investments.","Debt cannot be sold at a premium or discount.","Currency exchange must be hand to hand, otherwise one party benefits from money it received before giving the counter value."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"What counts as investment?","kind":"textbook","text":"Buying a government bond or placing a conventional deposit is merely a financial transaction, not investment. Using funds to buy real goods and sell them at a profit is investment. Buying an asset with money borrowed on interest is not permissible (p. 74)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.2.1","t4.2.6","t1.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which is an ‘investment’ in the Islamic sense described in Section 4.2?","options":["Buying a government bond","A deposit in a conventional bank","Using funds to buy goods and sell them at a profit","Buying a debt document at a discount"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Investment must be part of real activity (p. 74)."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Loan","b":"Investment","text":"Loan: funds change hands with guaranteed full repayment and no return. Investment: funds become part of real activity, and the return is permissible."}]},{"id":"t4.2.1","section":"4.2.1","title":"Avoiding Interest","pages":[74,75],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba"],"intuition":"What exactly may an Islamic bank not do with loans and debts?","simpleExplanation":"Islamic banks will not give or take loans seeking any increase over the principal. They may buy and sell on cash or credit for profit, and price credit sales considering time — but once the debt is created, it cannot be increased. They cannot charge cost of funds on loans, overdrafts, guarantees or bills, nor sell debt instruments.","academicExplanation":["The most important feature of the Islamic theory of finance, based on 2:275 and 2:279, is avoiding interest or any ex ante return on a loan/debt. The lender gives the lent goods/money for the loan period without worldly compensation. Islamic banks will not take or give loans or enter contracts seeking any increase over the principal of loans or debts created by credit transactions. Pricing goods with regard to the credit period is acceptable provided there is no enhancement of the debt once created. It follows that an Islamic bank may not earn a time-based charge on money — whether called cost of funds or rent — on any loan, overdraft, guarantee or bill/receivable financing of any tenor, and it may not sell its debt instruments."],"examExplanation":"No ex ante return on loans/debts. Credit-sale pricing may consider time, but debt once created cannot increase. No cost of funds on loans, overdrafts, guarantees, bills; no sale of debt instruments.","keyPoints":["Once a debt is created the seller cannot demand more than the stipulated credit price.","Lending on interest is alien to Islamic banks."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Fungible vs non-fungible loans","kind":"textbook","text":"If one ton of wheat or 1,000 dollars is borrowed, exactly one ton or 1,000 dollars is repaid; any excess is usurious. Hanafis allow lending only fungibles; other schools allow lending any property, with its price paid if a like is unavailable (p. 74, n. 1)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic banks may charge a “cost of funds” on overdrafts if it is called a fee.","right":"They may not charge cost of funds or rent on money in any loan, overdraft, guarantee, bill or receivable financing, nor sell debt instruments (p. 75)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1","t4.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which charge is NOT permitted to an Islamic bank?","options":["Profit in a credit sale priced before execution","Rent on a leased building","Cost of funds on an overdraft","A service fee under Wakalah"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Cost of funds on loans/overdrafts is prohibited (p. 75)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.2.1","sectionTitle":"Avoiding Interest, Gharar, and Gambling","title":"How Islamic Banks Operationally Avoid Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar","covers":["t4.2.1"],"overview":"The author explains the practical, product-level implications of the three core prohibitions (from Chapter 3) for Islamic banking operations: no interest on any loan/debt, no dealings involving excessive uncertainty, and no involvement in games of chance.","simpleExplanation":"Building on Chapter 3's prohibitions, this section spells out what they mean in practice for a bank. No interest: Islamic banks can price goods for credit sales (accounting for the payment delay), but once a debt is fixed, they can never add anything more — no fees on loans, no rent on money, no selling debt instruments. No Gharar: banks must avoid speculative share trading, short-selling, discounting bills, or trading in items whose delivery is unclear — many derivatives and futures/options markets are flagged as problematic for this reason, though ordinary stock markets would be fine if cleaned of these elements. No gambling: any product resembling a lottery or prize scheme (including conventional insurance, which combines interest and gambling elements) is off-limits.","academicExplanation":"Building directly on Chapter 3, the author operationalizes the three core prohibitions for Islamic banking products. Avoiding Interest (4.2.1): grounded in Qur'an 2:275 and 2:279, the lender must give away lent money/goods without seeking worldly compensation — Islamic banks cannot take or give loans/enter contracts seeking any increase over principal on loans or debts from credit transactions. Buying/selling goods (cash or credit) for profit is permissible, and pricing goods with the credit period in mind is acceptable, provided it involves no addition to the principal once the debt is created — once a debt exists, the seller cannot demand more than the stipulated credit price. Consequently, Islamic banks cannot charge fees on lending as 'cost of funds', rent on money, or sell their debt instruments at a premium/discount; they also cannot charge anything over the principal of debts arising from trade or Ijarah. Avoiding Gharar (4.2.2): defined again as absolute risk/uncertainty about a contract's ultimate result, the nature/quality/specifications of the subject matter, or the parties' rights/obligations; also includes inadequate or inaccurate value-relevant information (Jahl) leading to uncertainty/exploitation — deceit, fraud or deliberate withholding of relevant information is Gharar. Islamic banks must not engage in any bargain with a hidden result, since certainty about deliverability is necessary for a genuine business transaction. Current futures/options market practices of financial institutions/insurers are flagged as un-Islamic due to Gharar, interest and gambling elements combined; ordinary stock market transactions would be Islamic if cleansed of these elements. Specific prohibited practices: speculative share trading, short-selling, discounting of bills/securities, trading unidentified items; IPO involvement requires care due to potential information asymmetry between investors and promoters in early company stages; derivatives trading is flagged as a 'grey area' involving Gharar. Avoiding Gambling and Games of Chance (4.2.3): repeats the Chapter 3 rules — prize bonds/lotteries with chance-based inducement or disproportionate prizes via lot-drawing are prohibited; a scheme where principal is safe but prizes derive from pooled-capital interest violates both Riba and Qimar. Gambling is embedded in various conventional bank schemes Islamic banks must avoid; conventional insurance is non-compliant (Riba + Maisir); government/corporate lottery-based fundraising is prohibited; futures/options settled purely by price differences fall under gambling. Prize-carrying schemes are specifically flagged because only a small number of participants benefit at other bondholders' expense, without work or liability undertaken for it.","examExplanation":"Practical banking implications of the three prohibitions: (1) No interest — banks can price credit sales (embedding a markup for the payment delay) but cannot add anything once a debt is fixed, cannot charge fees on lending as 'cost of funds', cannot charge rent on money, and cannot sell debt instruments at a premium/discount. (2) No Gharar — banks must avoid speculative share trading, short-selling, bill/security discounting, and trading unidentified items; derivatives are flagged as a Gharar-related 'grey area'; conventional futures/options markets are deemed un-Islamic (Gharar + interest + gambling combined), though cleansed stock market transactions would be acceptable. (3) No gambling — prize bonds, lotteries, conventional insurance (Riba + Maisir), and price-difference-settled futures/options are all prohibited for Islamic banks.","keyPoints":["Islamic banks may price goods for credit sales (embedding the payment-delay factor into the price) but cannot add anything to a debt once it is created — no interest-like additions are permitted after the debt is fixed.","Prohibited banking practices under the interest ban: charging fees on lending as 'cost of funds', charging rent on money, and selling debt instruments at a premium or discount.","Gharar in banking practice means engaging in a bargain with a hidden/uncertain result — Islamic banks must avoid speculative share trading, short-selling, bill/security discounting, and trading unidentified items.","Derivatives trading and conventional futures/options markets are flagged as problematic due to combined Gharar, interest and gambling elements; ordinary stock market transactions could be Islamic if cleansed of these elements.","Islamic investment banks must exercise care in IPO involvement, since information asymmetry between investors and company promoters in a company's early stages can itself constitute Gharar.","Conventional insurance is deemed non-Shari'ah-compliant due to combined Riba and Maisir involvement; prize bonds and lottery-based fundraising are similarly prohibited."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["A profit margin may be embedded in a credit-sale price to reflect the payment delay, but once the debt/liability is created, no further addition is permissible."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Ordinary stock market trading is not inherently un-Islamic; the chapter specifically says contemporary stock market transactions would be Islamic 'if cleansed of' Gharar, interest and gambling elements — the prohibition targets specific practices (speculation, short-selling, undisclosed uncertainty), not equity markets as such."],"distinctions":["Legitimate credit-sale pricing (markup embedded before the debt is fixed) vs prohibited post-debt addition (any charge added after the debt/liability is created)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Gharar","Maisir/Qimar","Derivatives","Conventional insurance"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.1","pages":[74,75,76]}}]},{"id":"t4.2.2","section":"4.2.2","title":"Avoiding Gharar","pages":[75,75],"tier":"core","concepts":["gharar","derivatives"],"intuition":"Which banking activities are ruled out because of Gharar?","simpleExplanation":"Islamic banks must not enter bargains with hidden results. Speculative share trading, short-selling, discounting bills, trading unidentified items and most futures/options are ruled out. Derivatives are a grey area; IPOs need care because of information asymmetry.","academicExplanation":["Gharar is entering a contract in absolute risk or uncertainty about the result, subject matter or rights, including lack of value-relevant information (Jahl); deceit or withholding information amounts to Gharar. Futures and options practices are un-Islamic because of Gharar, interest and gambling; stock market transactions cleansed of these would be Islamic. Islamic banks should not engage in speculative trading in shares, short-selling, discounting bills and securities, or trading unidentified items. IPO involvement needs care due to information asymmetry between promoters and investors. Derivatives trading is a grey area."],"examExplanation":"Avoid bargains with hidden results; no speculative share trading, short-selling, bill discounting, unidentified items; futures/options un-Islamic (Gharar, interest, gambling); derivatives grey area; IPOs need care.","keyPoints":["Cleansed stock markets would be Islamic.","Short-selling and bill discounting are excluded.","Gharar rules out speculative share trading, short-selling, discounting bills, trading unidentified items, and requires care in IPOs because of information asymmetry.","Stock-market transactions cleansed of Riba, Gharar and gambling would be Islamic; derivatives remain a grey area."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Short sale ruled out","kind":"practice","text":"A dealer sells 1,000 shares he does not own, hoping to buy them back cheaper later. He sells what he neither owns nor possesses, so the deal is Gharar and speculation of the kind the book excludes for Islamic banks (p. 75)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"All stock-market activity is prohibited for Islamic banks.","right":"The author says stock-market transactions, if cleansed of Gharar, interest and gambling, would be Islamic; speculation and short-selling are what must be avoided (p. 75)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.2","t8.8.8"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which activity does the prohibition of Gharar rule out for Islamic banks?","options":["Spot purchase of identified goods","Short-selling","Ijarah of a building","Musharakah in a project"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 75."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t4.2.3","section":"4.2.3","title":"Avoiding Gambling and Games of Chance","pages":[76,76],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["maisir"],"intuition":"Which conventional products fall foul of the gambling prohibition?","simpleExplanation":"Prize bonds, lotteries and draw-based schemes, conventional insurance (Riba and Maisir) and futures/options settled only by price differences involve gambling, which IFIs must avoid.","academicExplanation":["Instruments offering inducements based on uncertain events, disproportionate prizes by lots, or prizes funded by interest on safe capital are repugnant due to Riba and Qimar. Conventional insurance involves Riba and Maisir; public lotteries and draws are prohibited; futures and options settled by price difference are gambling; prize-carrying bonds give prizes to a few at others’ cost without liability or work."],"examExplanation":"Avoid prize bonds/lotteries (Riba + Qimar), conventional insurance (Riba + Maisir), price-difference futures/options.","keyPoints":["Prize schemes benefit a few at the cost of others without liability or work.","Prize bonds benefit a few participants at the cost of the rest, without work or liability — hence Maisir as well as Riba."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Prize bonds","kind":"textbook","text":"Holders of prize bonds keep their capital safe, while prizes drawn by lot go to a few, funded by the return on everyone’s money. A few gain at the cost of the rest without work or liability, which is both Riba and Maisir (p. 76)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Conventional insurance is non-compliant due to which elements, according to the author?","options":["Riba and Maisir","Only Najash","Only Khalabah","None"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 76."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t4.2.4","section":"4.2.4","title":"Alternative Financing Principles","pages":[76,78],"tier":"core","concepts":["mudarabah","musharakah","murabaha","salam","ijarah","istisna","qard-hasan"],"intuition":"Without interest, how does an Islamic bank finance anything? Through sharing, deferred trading, leasing and combinations.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic banks use: Mudarabah and Musharakah (profit and loss sharing), Murabaha–Mu’ajjal (credit sale at a profit), Salam (prepaid forward purchase), Ijarah (leasing) and Istisna‘a (order to manufacture), plus return-free loans in specific cases. These differ in liquidity, collateral and whether the return is known.","academicExplanation":["Techniques: the participation and sharing principle (Musharakah, Mudarabah and variants); the deferred trading principle (Mu’ajjal and Salam); combinations (Shirkah with Ijarah; Murabaha with Salam/Istisna‘a); and return-free loans in specific situations. Mudarabah: one party provides capital, the other entrepreneurial skill; loss borne by the financier, profit shared in a pre-agreed ratio. Musharakah: permanent equity, a fixed-duration project partnership, or diminishing partnership. Murabaha–Mu’ajjal: acquiring goods and selling on credit at a profit margin, creating a debt payable irrespective of the buyer’s profit or loss (the case of Hind bint Utbah’s loan from Umar, which could not be waived though she lost in trade). Salam: prepayment for precisely defined goods. Ijarah: leasing, with the lessor owning the asset’s risk and reward. Istisna‘a: engaging a party to manufacture/construct and supply for an explicit sum.","Return is known in credit sales and Ijarah, but risk profiles differ: in Ijarah the bank bears asset risk and ownership expenses, so net return is quasi-fixed. Return is basically unknown in Salam and Istisna‘a (sale price on delivery uncertain), though it can be partly known through a parallel contract or a promise to purchase. In sharing modes the return is unknown. Security: in debt modes banks may take mortgage, lien or charge; in forward purchase, security for delivery; in PLS modes security only against nonperformance — it cannot be enforced for business loss without misconduct or negligence. Liquidity: Murabaha, Salam and Istisna‘a receivables cannot be sold like debt instruments (non-liquid); Ijarah and sharing-mode assets can be sold in the secondary market (liquid)."],"examExplanation":"Principles: sharing (Musharakah/Mudarabah), deferred trading (Murabaha–Mu’ajjal, Salam), Istisna‘a, Ijarah, combinations, Qard al Hasan. Table 4.1: debt modes non-liquid with collateral; Ijarah liquid, known return; sharing modes liquid, unknown return, penalty only for misconduct.","keyPoints":["Mudarabah loss is borne by the financier.","Murabaha debt is payable irrespective of the buyer’s profit or loss.","Ijarah net return is quasi-fixed because the lessor bears asset risk.","Collateral in PLS modes cannot be enforced for loss without misconduct/negligence.","Debt-mode receivables are non-liquid; Ijarah and sharing assets are liquid."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Hind bint Utbah’s loan","kind":"textbook","text":"Hind took 4000 from the public exchequer to trade and lost; Umar held the loan could not be waived — a debt is payable irrespective of the debtor’s business loss."},{"title":"Hind bint Utbah’s loan","kind":"textbook","text":"Hind borrowed 4,000 from the public treasury to trade and suffered a loss; Umar held that the loan could not be waived. A debt must be repaid irrespective of business outcome — the logic that applies to Murabaha receivables (pp. 76–77)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"In Musharakah the bank can seize collateral whenever the business makes a loss.","right":"Collateral in PLS modes is only against nonperformance; it cannot be enforced for loss without misconduct or negligence."}],"relatedTopics":["t8.5.3","t14.3.2","t9.1","t11.1","t12.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to Table 4.1, which contract is classified as ‘semi-debt’ with a known return and liquid assets?","options":["Murabaha","Ijarah","Musharakah","Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Ijarah (p. 77)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.2.4","sectionTitle":"Alternative Financing Principles","title":"Alternative Financing Principles: The Major Islamic Finance Modes and Their Risk/Liquidity Profile","covers":["t4.2.4"],"overview":"The author introduces the major Islamic financing techniques (Mudarabah, Musharakah, Murabaha-Mu'ajjal, Salam, Ijarah, Istisna'a) and classifies them by liquidity, guarantee/collateral treatment, and predictability of return.","simpleExplanation":"Since interest is off the table, Islamic banks use several alternative tools: partnership-based sharing (Mudarabah, Musharakah), deferred/credit trading (Murabaha-Mu'ajjal, Salam), leasing (Ijarah), and manufacturing contracts (Istisna'a). These tools differ in how predictable their returns are and how easily the underlying assets can be resold for cash: credit-sale-based modes (Murabaha) give the bank a known return but are hard to resell (non-liquid, since debt can't be sold at a discount); leasing (Ijarah) gives a known-ish return and is liquid (the leased asset itself can be sold); partnership modes (Musharakah/Mudarabah) are liquid but their return is genuinely unknown in advance, since it depends on actual business performance.","academicExplanation":"With interest unavailable, Islamic banks rely on: (1) the participation/sharing principle (Musharakah, Mudarabah and variants); (2) deferred trading (credit sales/Mu'ajjal and forward sales/Salam); (3) combinations (Shirkah with Ijarah, Murabaha with Salam/Istisna'a); and (4) return-free loans (Qard al-Hasan) in specific situations. The major modes: Mudarabah — a partnership where one party provides capital, the other provides entrepreneurial skill; loss is borne solely by the financier, profit is shared per a pre-agreed ratio. Musharakah — another profit-loss-sharing (PLS) arrangement, taking the form of permanent equity investment, a fixed-duration project partnership, or Diminishing Musharakah (the bank's share is progressively bought out over time by the client), especially useful for housing/fixed-asset financing. Murabaha-Mu'ajjal — acquiring goods on a customer's demand (or otherwise) and their credit sale at a profit margin, creating a debt (cost plus margin) that must be repaid regardless of the buyer's subsequent profit or loss, or even if the goods are destroyed in the buyer's ownership — illustrated by a historical precedent (Caliph Umar's ruling that Hind bint-e Utbah's public-exchequer trading loan could not be waived despite her trading loss, since the loan liability is independent of the borrower's business outcome). Salam — providing funds against a forward purchase of precisely defined goods, with full prepayment. Ijarah — leasing an asset for rental income; the lessor retains ownership and bears the ownership-related risk/reward throughout the lease. Istisna'a — engaging an agent (who may be a financier) to manufacture/construct and supply an item at a future date for an explicit sum, paid periodically; the agent subcontracts a manufacturer, and customers cover production cost plus profit margin. The author classifies these into three categories (Table 4.1): Debt-creating modes — Qard al-Hasan (no liquidity feature listed, collateral, nil return — a benevolent loan), Credit sales/Murabaha (non-liquid, collateral, known return), Salam (non-liquid, collateral, unknown/known return), Istisna'a (non-liquid, collateral, unknown/known return); Semi-debt mode — Ijarah (liquid, collateral, known return); Sharing modes — Musharakah, Restricted Mudarabah, General Mudarabah (all liquid, penalty-for-misconduct as guarantee mechanism, unknown return). Return predictability: known in Murabaha, Musawamah and Ijarah (though Ijarah's net return is 'quasi-fixed' since the bank bears asset risk/ownership expenses); basically unknown in Salam/Istisna'a (since the bank cannot know in advance the eventual resale price of goods to be delivered later) — though a parallel contract or promise for onward disposal at a stipulated price can partially fix this, subject to counterparty performance risk; genuinely unknown in advance for sharing modes, which depend entirely on actual business results. On Security/Collateral: banks may take security (mortgage, lien, charge) in debt-creating modes and forward-purchase modes to ensure delivery/performance; in PLS modes, security guards against contractual nonperformance but the bank cannot enforce collateral for ordinary business loss absent misconduct/negligence by the client-partner. On Liquidity: refers to how easily the bank can convert related assets to cash — Murabaha receivables (like Salam and Istisna'a assets) are non-liquid because debt cannot be sold like conventional debt instruments; Ijarah and sharing-mode assets/instruments are liquid since they can be sold in a secondary market (as actual ownership interests, not debt).","examExplanation":"Six major Islamic financing modes: Mudarabah (capital + management partnership; loss borne by financier alone, profit shared per agreed ratio), Musharakah (joint-capital partnership; includes Diminishing Musharakah), Murabaha-Mu'ajjal (credit sale at cost-plus-margin, creating a fixed debt independent of the buyer's business outcome — illustrated by the Hind bint-e Utbah precedent), Salam (full prepayment for defined future-delivered goods), Ijarah (leasing; lessor retains ownership/risk), Istisna'a (manufacture-to-order with periodic payment). Table 4.1 classification: Debt-creating modes (Murabaha, Salam, Istisna'a — non-liquid, collateralized, known or unknown return); Semi-debt mode (Ijarah — liquid, collateralized, known return); Sharing modes (Musharakah, Mudarabah — liquid, penalty-for-misconduct guarantee, unknown return). Liquidity = ease of converting bank assets to cash: Murabaha/Salam/Istisna'a are non-liquid (debt cannot be sold at a discount); Ijarah and sharing-mode assets are liquid (can be sold in a secondary market as real ownership interests).","keyPoints":["Six major Islamic financing modes: Mudarabah, Musharakah (incl. Diminishing Musharakah), Murabaha-Mu'ajjal, Salam, Ijarah, Istisna'a.","In Murabaha-Mu'ajjal, the resulting debt (cost plus profit margin) must be repaid regardless of the buyer's subsequent business profit/loss or even destruction of the goods in the buyer's ownership — illustrated by the historical Hind bint-e Utbah precedent under Caliph Umar.","Table 4.1 classification: Debt-creating modes (Murabaha, Salam, Istisna'a: non-liquid, collateralized, known or unknown return); Semi-debt mode (Ijarah: liquid, collateralized, known return); Sharing modes (Musharakah, Mudarabah: liquid, penalty-for-misconduct guarantee, unknown return).","Return predictability varies: known in Murabaha/Musawamah/Ijarah; essentially unknown in Salam/Istisna'a (future resale price uncertain, though partially mitigable via a parallel contract or promise); genuinely unknown in sharing modes (depends on actual business performance).","Liquidity (ease of converting to cash) differs sharply: Murabaha receivables, Salam and Istisna'a assets are non-liquid (debt cannot be sold like conventional debt instruments); Ijarah and sharing-mode assets/instruments are liquid (sellable in a secondary market as genuine ownership interests).","In PLS (profit-loss-sharing) modes, the bank may take security against contractual nonperformance, but cannot enforce collateral for ordinary business loss that occurred without misconduct or negligence by the client-partner."],"definitions":[{"term":"Mudarabah","definition":"A partnership arrangement in which one party provides capital while the other provides entrepreneurial skill; any loss is borne by the financier (capital provider) alone, while profit is shared per a pre-agreed ratio."},{"term":"Musharakah","definition":"A profit-loss-sharing partnership that may take the form of a permanent equity investment, a fixed-duration project partnership, or a diminishing partnership (the bank's share is progressively bought out over time), often used for housing/fixed-asset financing."},{"term":"Murabaha-Mu'ajjal","definition":"Acquiring goods on a customer's demand (or otherwise) and selling them on credit at a profit margin, creating a fixed debt (cost plus margin) that must be paid regardless of the buyer's subsequent profit, loss, or destruction of the goods in their ownership."},{"term":"Istisna'a","definition":"A contract engaging an agent (which may be a financier) to manufacture/construct and supply a specified item at a future date for an explicit sum, paid on a periodic basis; the agent subcontracts a manufacturer, and customers cover production cost plus profit margin."}],"conditions":["In PLS modes, the bank cannot enforce collateral against a client-partner for ordinary business loss occurring without their misconduct or negligence."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Hind bint-e Utbah's trading loan (Caliph Umar's ruling)","kind":"textbook","text":"Hind bint-e Utbah borrowed 4000 dinars from the public exchequer to trade, on condition of repayment. She traded in the area of Banu Kalb but suffered a loss. Caliph Umar ruled the loan could not be waived, since it was from the public exchequer — illustrating that a debt obligation from a credit/loan transaction is independent of the borrower's subsequent business outcome."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume all Islamic financing modes carry unknown/variable returns; Table 4.1 shows Murabaha, Salam (partially), Istisna'a (partially) and Ijarah can have known or quasi-fixed returns, while only genuine sharing modes (Musharakah/Mudarabah) have fully unknown returns."],"distinctions":["Debt-creating modes (Murabaha, Salam, Istisna'a — non-liquid, fixed obligation once created) vs semi-debt mode (Ijarah — liquid, ownership retained by lessor) vs sharing modes (Musharakah, Mudarabah — liquid, genuinely variable/unknown return)."],"relatedConcepts":["Shirkah","Qard al-Hasan","Diminishing Musharakah","Parallel Salam"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,77,78]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Collateral in debt modes","b":"Security in PLS modes","text":"In debt-creating modes the bank can enforce collateral on default; in PLS modes security covers only misconduct or negligence and cannot be enforced for an honest business loss (p. 78)."}],"table":{"caption":"Table 4.1 — Features of Islamic financing tools","head":["Category","Contract","Liquidity","Guarantee","Rate of return"],"rows":[["Debt-creating","Qard al Hasan","—","Collateral","Nil"],["Debt-creating","Credit sales","Non-liquid","Collateral","Known"],["Debt-creating","Salam","Non-liquid","Collateral","Unknown/known"],["Debt-creating","Istisna‘a","Non-liquid","Collateral","Unknown/known"],["Semi-debt","Ijarah","Liquid","Collateral","Known"],["Sharing","Musharakah","Liquid","Penalty for misconduct","Unknown"],["Sharing","Restricted Mudarabah","Liquid","Penalty for misconduct","Unknown"],["Sharing","General Mudarabah","Liquid","Penalty for misconduct","Unknown"]]}},{"id":"t4.2.5","section":"4.2.5","title":"Valid Gains on Investment (Pre-fixed Returns and Benchmarks)","pages":[78,81],"tier":"core","concepts":["fixed-return","benchmark","time-value"],"intuition":"Is every fixed return Riba? The author says no — it depends on the nature of the transaction.","simpleExplanation":"Profit is a legitimate reward for capital, but capital must bear the risk of loss. Returns can be fixed in trade (credit price higher than cash price) and leasing (fixed rent), but never on loans or debts. Islamic banks need two benchmarks — for pricing (mark-up/rent) and for sharing ratios. Using an interest-based benchmark for pricing does not make trade or Ijarah un-Islamic if their rules are followed.","academicExplanation":["Profit is recognised as a reward of capital; Islam permits — even encourages — gainful deployment of resources, but liability for loss rests with capital itself. Financial transactions must be associated with tangible real assets; money is not capital and cannot earn profit in itself. The fund provider is also an entrepreneur; profit must relate to assets exposed to direct or indirect business risk.","All pre-fixed returns are not Riba. Loans/debts cannot fetch any increase; in sale of goods or usufruct one may profit per the rules. Bai‘ is definite transfer of ownership against a price (spot, deferred or advance in Salam); risk and reward pass to the buyer, so banks have no recourse to the sold goods for rollover or re-pricing. Hibah is permanent free transfer. Riba Al-Nasiah is temporary transfer against payment; if free, it is Qard al Hasan/Tabarru‘. In Ijarah, rent is allowed if the lessor bears ownership risk and expenses; the analogy “fixed rent includes time value, so fixed interest should too” fails because the usufruct is uncertain, the lessor owns the risk, and consumables like money cannot be leased.","Variable rates: Shirkah modes give variable returns and are the main deposit-side mode; quasi-fixed returns arise when Shirkah is attached to trade or Ijarah (e.g. Diminishing Musharakah, Shirkah-based securitisation). Partners agree profit ratios freely, but loss must be exactly in proportion to investment.","Benchmarks: Fiqh recognises Ujratul-mithl (matching wage), Ribh-al-mithl (matching profit), Qirad mithl and Musaqat mithl — customary compensation when a contract becomes Fasid. Service charges on loans may be based on Ujratul-mithl. Islamic finance needs two reference scales: a price (mark-up/rent) scale and a sharing-ratio scale (e.g. central bank or inter-bank Mudarabah ratio), ideally set by market forces. Scholars hold that using an interest-based benchmark for pricing goods and usufruct does not make operations un-Islamic so long as other rules of trade and Ijarah are applied."],"examExplanation":"Profit is reward of capital with liability for loss; must relate to real assets. Fixed return OK in trade and Ijarah, never on loans/debts. Rent ≠ interest: usufruct uncertain, lessor bears ownership risk. Two benchmarks: pricing scale and sharing-ratio scale; interest benchmark for pricing doesn’t invalidate if rules followed. Mithl concepts.","keyPoints":["Money is not capital and cannot earn profit in itself.","A seller cannot re-price sold goods (no Murabaha rollover).","Loss in Shirkah must be exactly in proportion to investment.","Two benchmarks needed; interest-based benchmark acceptable for pricing if rules followed."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ujratul-mithl","definition":"Matching/customary wage — used e.g. as the basis for service charges on loans."},{"term":"Ribh-al-mithl","definition":"Matching rate of profit — customary compensation when a contract becomes voidable."},{"term":"Qard al Hasan","definition":"A benevolent, return-free loan (temporary transfer free of payment)."}],"examples":[{"title":"Benchmark: Ujratul-mithl","kind":"textbook","text":"Classical Fiqh knows matching rates — Ujratul-mithl (wage), Ribh-al-mithl (profit), Qirad mithl and Musaqat mithl — used as customary compensation when a contract becomes Fasid. Banks may charge service charges on loans on the basis of Ujratul-mithl (pp. 80–81)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Using LIBOR/KIBOR to price a Murabaha makes it interest.","right":"Scholars say using an interest-based benchmark for pricing does not make trade/Ijarah un-Islamic as long as the other rules are applied."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.2.6","t6.5.2","t11.3.2","t17.4.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why does the author reject the analogy between fixed Ijarah rent and fixed interest?","options":["Rent is always variable","Rent is based on an asset’s uncertain usufruct and the lessor bears ownership risk","Interest is permitted in Ijarah","There is no difference"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 79."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.2.5","sectionTitle":"Valid Gains on Investment","title":"Valid Gains on Investment: Fixed and Variable Returns, and Benchmarks","covers":["t4.2.5"],"overview":"The author explains that not all gains on capital are prohibited — profit is a legitimate reward tied to risk-bearing — and clarifies that even fixed/pre-determined returns can be Shari'ah-compliant depending on the transaction's nature, plus how Islamic finance uses benchmarks/reference rates.","simpleExplanation":"Not every 'fixed return' is interest — the test is not whether a number is fixed in advance, but what kind of transaction produced it. A fixed profit margin in a trade or lease is fine; a fixed charge on a loan/debt is never fine, no matter how small. Islamic finance needs two different reference rates instead of conventional finance's single interest rate: one for pricing debt/lease-type deals, and one for sharing ratios in partnership deals. Interestingly, Islamic banks are allowed to use conventional interest rates as a pricing benchmark for their trade/lease deals (as a reference number only) without that making the underlying transaction un-Islamic, as long as the actual trade/lease rules are still followed.","academicExplanation":"The author states not all gains on investment/principal are prohibited: Shari'ah scholars have identified methods for gainful deployment of surplus resources, since profit is recognized as capital's legitimate 'reward', and productive investment is even considered obligatory (a Hadith warns against selling a house without reinvesting the proceeds 'in something similar'; Caliph Umar urged developing wealth/cultivating land). Ex post profit (as opposed to a guaranteed ex ante return) symbolizes entrepreneurship and value creation, but the liability of loss risk stays specifically with capital — no other factor can be made to bear capital's loss burden. Since money itself isn't recognized as capital and cannot earn profit alone, the fund provider is inherently also an entrepreneur: managing the business themselves yields profit/loss on capital plus wages for labour; providing capital to another manager yields a profit share (with the manager's 'wages' also structured as a profit share) — but the capital owner bears any loss while the manager's labour goes unrewarded if the business loses. A key clarifying subsection, 'All Pre-fixed Returns are not Riba': Shari'ah compliance does not require variable income — several legitimate structures produce a fixed return. The test is transaction nature, not fixed-vs-variable form: loans/debts culminating from credit transactions can never fetch any increase; but genuine sale of goods/usufructs can yield profit per the relevant rules — e.g., cash-and-carry vs deferred-payment prices for the same commodity can legitimately differ, with cash/credit prices set by market forces (subject to specific conditions distinguishing interest from legitimate profit). Four transaction types clarified: Bai' (sale) — definite ownership transfer against a price (spot/deferred/advance); risk/reward pass to the buyer, who must pay regardless of how he uses the goods or his own profit/loss — banks therefore have no recourse to re-price goods already sold via Murabaha. Hibah (gift) — permanent, payment-free ownership transfer, irrevocable without the recipient's consent. Riba al-Nasiah — temporary ownership transfer against payment (interest, prohibited); if payment-free, it is Qard al-Hasan/Tabarru' (encouraged). Ijarah — usufruct transfer against rent, with the lessor bearing ownership-related risk/expense; the chapter explicitly refutes the analogy sometimes drawn between Ijarah's predetermined rent (accepted) and predetermined loan interest (claimed to be permissible 'by analogy') — this fails because Ijarah rent reflects the asset's usufruct-generating capacity (inherently uncertain over the asset's full economic life) and because only non-consumable assets (not money, yarn, edibles, fuel) can be leased, since the lessor must be able to bear ownership-risk on an asset that continues to exist. 'Variable Rates on Investments': Shirkah-based modes (Musharakah, Mudarabah, variants) give genuinely variable returns tied to actual business results — the major deposit-mobilization tool for Islamic banks — though products can be structured for a quasi-fixed return by combining Shirkah with fixed-earning modes (e.g. Diminishing Musharakah on Shirkatulmilk basis, or Shirkah-based securitization). Shirkah carries more business risk than trade/Ijarah, since partners must set the profit-sharing ratio by mutual consent while losses must track investment proportion exactly; empirical studies show equity financing has significant global advantages, though Islamic banks have not yet fully exploited Shirkah's potential. 'Benchmarks/Reference Rates': financial institutions need reference rates for pricing/administration; Fiqh literature recognizes Ujratul-mithl (matching wage), Ribh-al-mithl (matching profit rate), Qirad mithl (matching Mudarabah rate) and Musaqat mithl (matching crop-sharing rate) as customary-compensation benchmarks used specifically when an underlying contract becomes voidable (Fasid) due to unmet conditions. Conventional finance needs only one benchmark (the interest rate); Islamic finance needs two — a price (markup/rent) reference scale for debt/semi-debt contracts, and a sharing-ratio reference scale (e.g. via central bank or inter-bank Mudarabah ratios) for equity contracts. Crucially, Shari'ah scholars hold that Islamic banks using an interest-based benchmark (like LIBOR) merely as a pricing reference for trade/Ijarah operations does not itself make those operations un-Islamic, provided the actual rules of trade/Ijarah are properly followed — the benchmark is a number, not the transaction's legal basis.","examExplanation":"Not all gains on capital are prohibited — the test is the nature of the underlying transaction, not whether the return is fixed or variable. 'All Pre-fixed Returns are not Riba': a fixed profit margin from genuine trade (Bai') or fixed rent from Ijarah is valid; any fixed (or any) charge on a loan/debt is Riba regardless of size. Islamic finance requires two benchmark types (vs conventional finance's single interest-rate benchmark): a price/markup-rent reference scale for debt/semi-debt contracts, and a sharing-ratio reference scale for equity contracts. Using a conventional interest-rate benchmark (e.g. LIBOR) merely as a pricing reference number for Islamic trade/Ijarah deals does not itself make those deals un-Islamic, provided the actual Shari'ah trade/Ijarah rules are followed.","keyPoints":["Productive investment is considered obligatory, not merely permitted, in Islamic economics (per Hadith on reinvesting sale proceeds, and Caliph Umar's exhortation to develop wealth/cultivate land).","'All Pre-fixed Returns are not Riba': the Shari'ah test is the nature of the transaction, not whether the return is fixed — a fixed trade profit margin or fixed Ijarah rent is valid, while any addition on a loan/debt (fixed or not) is Riba.","The analogy 'if predetermined Ijarah rent is allowed, predetermined loan interest should be too' is explicitly refuted: Ijarah rent reflects an asset's inherently uncertain usufruct-generating capacity over its economic life, and only non-consumable assets (not money) can be leased.","Shirkah-based modes (Musharakah, Mudarabah) give genuinely variable returns tied to actual business results and are the main deposit-mobilization tool for Islamic banks, though products can be structured for a quasi-fixed return by combining Shirkah with fixed-earning modes.","Islamic finance requires two distinct benchmark/reference-rate types (unlike conventional finance's single interest rate): a price (markup/rent) scale for debt/semi-debt contracts, and a sharing-ratio scale for equity contracts.","Using a conventional interest-rate benchmark merely as a pricing reference number for Islamic trade/Ijarah transactions does not itself make the transaction un-Islamic, provided the substantive Shari'ah rules of trade/Ijarah are actually followed."],"definitions":[{"term":"Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman / Al Ghunm bil Ghurm","definition":"The Shari'ah maxim that entitlement to any profit/gain (Ghunm) must be matched by liability for loss/responsibility (Ghurm/Daman) — the criterion for the legality of any return on capital."},{"term":"Ujratul-mithl / Ribh-al-mithl","definition":"Customary/matching-rate benchmarks (matching wage; matching profit rate) used to determine fair compensation when an underlying contract becomes voidable (Fasid) due to unmet conditions."}],"conditions":["A commodity's cash and credit prices may legitimately differ, provided one price is settled before the parties separate and there is no subsequent change to the liability once created."],"principles":["The Shari'ah test for whether a return is permissible is the nature of the underlying transaction (sale, lease, or loan/debt), not whether the return happens to be fixed or variable in amount."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume Islamic finance requires all returns to be variable/profit-and-loss-based; the chapter explicitly corrects this — fixed returns from genuine trade or lease transactions are fully Shari'ah-compliant, and only fixed returns from loans/debts are prohibited.","Using an interest-rate index (e.g. LIBOR) as a benchmark number for pricing is sometimes assumed to taint a transaction with Riba; the chapter clarifies the benchmark is just a reference figure — Shari'ah compliance depends on the underlying trade/Ijarah rules being followed, not on which number is used as a pricing reference."],"distinctions":["Fixed return from a genuine trade/lease transaction (permissible) vs fixed return from a loan/debt (Riba, always prohibited, regardless of size)."],"relatedConcepts":["Shirkah","Diminishing Musharakah","Riba","Bai'","Ijarah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,79,80,81]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Fixed rent in Ijarah","b":"Fixed interest on a loan","text":"Rent is paid for an uncertain usufruct of an asset whose ownership risk the lessor bears; interest is an increase over a loaned principal with no risk."}]},{"id":"t4.2.6","section":"4.2.6","title":"Entitlement to Profit — With Risk and Responsibility","pages":[81,82],"tier":"core","concepts":["kharaj-daman","risk-sharing"],"intuition":"What single maxim tells you whether a return is legitimate?","simpleExplanation":"“Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman” / “Al Ghunm bil Ghurm”: whoever wants profit must bear the risk of loss. Ownership cannot be separated from its risk. Banks can mitigate risk but not eliminate it, and cannot pass risk to others without passing the reward too.","academicExplanation":["Assumption of business risk is a precondition for profit. The legitimacy of any return on capital is judged by the maxim Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman (also expressed as Al Ghunm bil Ghurm): gain goes with liability. Profit is earned by bearing the risk and reward of ownership through pricing goods, services or usufruct. Money grows when it joins entrepreneurship; in itself it is not capital. Reward should depend on productive behaviour, so interest, lotteries and gambling are prohibited.","In debt modes banks face credit, ownership transfer, market, commodity, rate of return, legal/documentation and mode-specific risks; they may mitigate within Shari’ah but not totally eliminate risk. Transfer of commercial risk without the related reward is not permissible. Ownership cannot be separated from risk: lenders get the principal back whatever the use; in trade the seller bears risk until sale, then the buyer bears it (a credit buyer pays even if the asset is destroyed; Takaful may mitigate but does not affect the liability); in Ijarah the lessor earns rent only while keeping the asset usable and bearing its risks.","Shirkah modes carry far more risk: all loss falls on capital while the manager loses labour. Depositors face business and profit uncertainty, which justifies profit. There is consensus that depositors should not bear losses from management negligence; experts favour protecting them to raise confidence."],"examExplanation":"Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman / Al Ghunm bil Ghurm: profit only with liability for loss. Ownership inseparable from risk. Risk mitigated not eliminated; cannot transfer risk without reward. Depositors not to bear losses from management negligence.","keyPoints":["Maxim: Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman (Al Ghunm bil Ghurm).","Risk can be mitigated but not eliminated.","Credit buyer must pay even if the goods are destroyed after sale.","Lessor earns rent only while bearing ownership risks.","Consensus: depositors should not bear losses caused by management negligence or fraud."],"definitions":[{"term":"Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman / Al Ghunm bil Ghurm","definition":"Maxim: one must bear loss, if any, to be entitled to profit on investment — the criterion of legality of any return on capital."}],"examples":[{"title":"Goods destroyed after a credit sale","kind":"textbook","text":"A client buys a machine from the bank on credit and it is destroyed a week later. The client must still pay the full price, because the risk passed to him with ownership at the sale. Before the sale the loss would have been the bank’s (pp. 81–82)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Taking Takaful on sold goods removes the buyer’s obligation to pay if the goods are destroyed.","right":"Takaful mitigates the buyer’s risk but has no link with his liability to pay the price at the settled time (p. 82)."}],"relatedTopics":["t1.5","t5.5.7","t4.2.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"After a credit sale, the goods are destroyed in the buyer’s possession. The buyer:","options":["Is released from paying","Must still pay the price at the settled time","Pays half","Can return the goods"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Risk transferred on sale; the price remains payable (p. 82)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.2.6","sectionTitle":"Entitlement to Profit — With Risk and Responsibility","title":"Entitlement to Profit With Risk and Responsibility: Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman","covers":["t4.2.6"],"overview":"The author elaborates the maxim 'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' (profit follows liability) as the criterion for the legality of any return, and details how ownership-linked risk plays out across loans, trade, Ijarah, and Shirkah-based modes.","simpleExplanation":"The rule 'if you want the gain, you must bear the loss risk' applies to every type of Islamic finance transaction, but in different ways. In a loan, the lender gets nothing extra (and bears no business risk either, since the loan is guaranteed regardless). In a sale, the seller bears risk only while they still own the goods; once sold (even on credit), the risk passes to the buyer who must pay the price regardless. In leasing, the lessor must keep bearing ownership-related risk and expenses throughout the lease to keep earning rent. In partnerships (Shirkah), risk is highest: capital providers can lose their entire capital, and depositors in Islamic banks bear real uncertainty about their return, though this isn't seen as a bad thing — it's what actually justifies profit at all.","academicExplanation":"The chapter restates 'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' ('Al Ghunm bil Ghurm') as the criterion for legality of any return on capital: one must bear potential loss to be entitled to any profit above principal. Profit must be earned by sharing the risk and reward of ownership through pricing goods, services or usufruct — reiterating that 'investment' requires being part of real activity (money alone, absent entrepreneurship, cannot earn a return). All economic activities carry some commercial risk, and bearing that risk is what validates any profit/earning claim — return must correspond to willingness/ability to add value and bear potential loss, which is precisely why interest, lotteries and gambling are prohibited (they either reject business risk-bearing or rest on pure luck/chance). Islamic banks in debt-creating modes face a specific risk taxonomy: credit/party risk, ownership-transfer risk, market risk, commodity risk, price/rate-of-return risk, legal/documentation risk, and other mode-specific risks — banks may take Shari'ah-compliant risk-mitigation measures (risk can be mitigated, not eliminated), but transferring commercial risk to another party without also transferring the corresponding reward is impermissible. The core principle: ownership cannot be separated from the risk of related loss — worked through by transaction type: in loans, no profit entitlement exists at all, since the creditor recovers the full amount regardless of the debtor's use/outcome. In trade, the seller bears destruction risk only while the asset remains with him; once sold, risk transfers to the buyer, who must pay the settled price even if the asset is later destroyed (he may mitigate this personally via Takaful, but that has no bearing on his payment obligation). In Ijarah, the lessor is entitled to rent only for as long as he keeps the asset usable by incurring ownership-related expenses and bearing the asset's risks. In Shirkah-based modes, business risk exceeds trade/Ijarah risk substantially, since all business loss falls on capital while the manager/entrepreneur only loses his labour. For bank depositors, risk stems from business failure and profit-level uncertainty — but this is explicitly framed as not discouraging depositors (justifying rather than deterring the profit earned), evidenced by continuously increasing Islamic bank deposits. For banks financing via Shirkah, risk includes clients potentially disguising profits, and the possibility of losing even the principal (since 'loss' in Islamic finance specifically means capital loss, not merely a shortfall from expected profit). Regarding investment depositors' risk scope: while there is consensus depositors should not bear losses from management fraud, carelessness, mismanagement or loan concentration (only genuine market risk), experts consider protecting depositors from these non-market risks desirable both to build confidence and to incentivize more careful bank risk management and stricter supervisory oversight.","examExplanation":"'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' / 'Al Ghunm bil Ghurm' = profit entitlement requires bearing corresponding loss liability; this is the universal test for legality of any capital return. Risk-by-transaction-type: Loans — no profit entitlement at all (full guaranteed recovery, no risk-bearing). Trade — seller bears risk only while holding the asset; risk transfers to buyer at sale, who must pay the settled price even if the asset is later destroyed. Ijarah — lessor entitled to rent only while bearing ownership-related risk/expenses (keeping the asset usable). Shirkah — highest risk: all business loss falls on capital, manager only loses labour; depositors bear genuine uncertainty (but should not bear losses from bank fraud/mismanagement/negligence — market risk only).","keyPoints":["'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' / 'Al Ghunm bil Ghurm': the universal Shari'ah criterion that entitlement to profit requires bearing the corresponding liability for loss.","Islamic banks face a specific risk taxonomy in debt-creating modes (credit/party, ownership-transfer, market, commodity, price/rate-of-return, legal/documentation, mode-specific risks); risk can be mitigated but never fully eliminated, and transferring commercial risk without the corresponding reward is impermissible.","Core principle: ownership cannot be separated from the risk of related loss — this determines exactly when a bank's risk-bearing (and thus profit entitlement) begins and ends in each transaction type.","In trade, the seller bears risk only while holding the asset; once sold, risk transfers fully to the buyer, who must pay the settled price even if the asset is later destroyed (he may separately insure via Takaful, but that doesn't affect his payment obligation).","Shirkah-based modes carry substantially more risk than trade/Ijarah: all business loss falls on capital, while the manager/entrepreneur only loses their labour if the business fails.","There is consensus that investment depositors should bear genuine market risk but should NOT bear losses caused by bank fraud, carelessness, mismanagement or loan concentration — protecting them from these non-market risks builds confidence and incentivizes better bank risk management."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Commercial risk may be transferred to another party only together with the corresponding reward — transferring risk alone, without reward, is not permissible."],"principles":["Ownership cannot be separated from the risk of related loss — a party's entitlement to a transaction's profit tracks exactly the period/extent during which they bear its ownership-related risk."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["'Loss' in Islamic finance specifically means loss of capital, not merely earning less profit than expected — a lower-than-hoped-for profit share is not a 'loss' requiring special treatment; only an actual erosion of principal counts."],"distinctions":["Market risk (genuine business/investment risk, which depositors should bear) vs operational risk from bank fraud/mismanagement/negligence (which depositors should NOT bear, per expert consensus)."],"relatedConcepts":["Takaful","Shirkah","Risk-sharing","Ijarah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[81,82]}}],"principles":["Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman — entitlement to gain goes with liability for loss.","Ownership cannot be separated from the risk of related loss."]},{"id":"t4.2.7","section":"4.2.7","title":"Islamic Banks Dealing in Goods not in Money","pages":[82,83],"tier":"core","concepts":["asset-backing","parallel-salam","wakalah"],"intuition":"Conventional banks “deal in documents, not goods”. What do Islamic banks deal in?","simpleExplanation":"Islamic banks deal in goods and documents, not money. They buy goods (directly or via an agent), own them and bear the risk, then sell at cost plus profit or lease them. In Salam they own goods after delivery; a Parallel Salam must be independent. Forward currency deals with delayed payment are not allowed. They may earn fees (e.g. Wakalatul Istismar) but not a fee on lending as cost of funds.","academicExplanation":["Conventional banks deal in money and documents without responsibility for the subject of contracts. Islamic banks use money only as a medium of exchange to purchase goods for sale or lease, intermediating through goods, assets or papers representing ownership of real assets. In Murabaha/Salam the bank buys directly or through an agent (Wakalah), takes ownership and risk, then sells at cost plus profit; the client then bears the risk and pays at the settled time. In Istisna‘a manufacturers deliver the asset with related risks.","In Salam, after delivery the asset and price risk is the bank’s. A Parallel Salam may be for the same date and quantity, but the two contracts must not be linked or conditional; the parallel contract must be honoured regardless of the first. Salam and Istisna‘a have potential for agricultural and rural micro-finance; forward FX with delayed payment and most financial futures are not available (spot FX is fine). In Ijarah, ownership and risk remain with the bank; if the asset is damaged without the lessee’s fault, rent ceases; transfer of ownership requires a separate sale or gift. In Musharakah/Mudarabah, earnings depend on the client’s business.","Banks may charge service/management fees, but not a fee on lending as cost of funds (Riba). Penalties for default are not credited to P&L. Under Wakalatul Istismar they manage funds for a fixed fee with profit/loss belonging to clients."],"examExplanation":"Islamic banks deal in goods/documents, not money; take ownership and risk before selling/leasing. Parallel Salam independent of original. Ijarah: bank bears asset risk; rent ceases if asset damaged without lessee fault; separate sale/gift for transfer. Fees allowed for services; no cost-of-funds fee; penalties to charity.","keyPoints":["Money used only as a medium of exchange.","Parallel Salam must not be conditional on the original.","Forward FX with deferred payment not permitted; spot FX fine.","Wakalatul Istismar: fixed management fee; P&L belongs to clients.","Wakalatul Istismar: bank manages clients’ funds for a fixed fee; profit and loss belong to clients.","“Conventional banks deal in documents not goods”; Islamic banks deal in goods and documents, not money."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Spot FX allowed, forward FX with delay not","kind":"textbook","text":"An Islamic bank may buy dollars for rupees on the spot. It may not agree today to exchange the currencies at a future date with the payment deferred, since exchange of monetary values must be simultaneous (pp. 82–83)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Parallel Salam must be linked to the first Salam so the bank is protected.","right":"The two contracts must not be linked or conditional; the parallel Salam must be honoured even if the first fails (p. 83)."}],"relatedTopics":["t10.10","t13.2.2","t11.3.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Under what condition is a Parallel Salam valid according to contemporary scholars cited here?","options":["It must be conditional on the first Salam","It must not be linked to or conditional on the original Salam","It must have a different commodity","It must be with the same party"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 83."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.2.7","sectionTitle":"Islamic Banks Dealing in Goods not in Money","title":"Islamic Banks Deal in Goods and Documents, Not Money","covers":["t4.2.7"],"overview":"The author contrasts conventional banks (which 'deal in documents, not goods', with no concern for the underlying assets) with Islamic banks (which must actually deal in goods and take on real asset ownership/risk), walking through how this plays out across each major mode.","simpleExplanation":"Conventional banks are famously described as dealing 'in documents, not goods' — they finance the purchase of goods but never actually own or risk the goods themselves; their only concern is the money and paperwork. Islamic banks are the opposite: they must actually buy the goods (or have the manufacturer/agent deliver them), take on real ownership and the risks that go with it, and only then sell or lease them on. This makes an Islamic bank act more like a real trader/lessor/partner than a pure financier — which is exactly the source of the many additional risks (asset risk, market risk) that Islamic banks face compared to conventional banks.","academicExplanation":"Conventional banks deal in money — taking deposits as loans (paying interest) and giving advances as loans (charging interest); even in trade-finance or finance-lease contexts, they deal only in documents facilitating goods' movement, undertaking no responsibility/risk for the goods themselves ('banks deal in documents not in goods'). Islamic banks, in contrast, deal in goods and documents, not money — money is used only as a medium to purchase goods for onward leasing or sale, generating income/profit; documents support executing the actual sale/lease contracts, subject to Shari'ah principles. This means Islamic banks intermediate between savers/fund users by actually involving goods, assets, or ownership-representing papers. Mode-by-mode: In Salam/Murabaha, banks deal in specific commodities (not money) — purchasing directly or via a Wakalah (agency) arrangement, either at their own discretion (maintaining inventory) or on client order; the bank takes on ownership and related risk, then sells at cost-plus-profit like an ordinary trader; risk transfers to the client only after sale execution, at which point the client is bound to pay at the settled time. In Istisna'a, manufacturers manufacture and deliver the asset to the bank along with related asset/market risks. In Salam, the bank receives goods against prepayments already made; asset and price risk thereafter belong to the bank, not the Salam seller — contemporary scholars permit a Parallel Salam (the bank selling a commodity purchased via Salam through an independent, unlinked parallel contract for the same/different delivery terms), with each contract's performance independent of the other's. Forward trading via Salam/Istisna'a has strong potential for agricultural and rural micro-finance development, but forward foreign exchange with delayed payment on either side, and most financial futures, are unavailable in a Shari'ah-compliant system (since these are hedging tools rooted in the interest-based system) — though the spot FX market functions without issue. In Ijarah, banks deal in physical assets, purchasing them for client lease; ownership and related risks/expenses stay with the bank throughout the lease — if the asset is damaged (through no lessee fault) and cannot deliver its intended benefit, the bank's right to rental ceases; transferring ownership to the lessee at lease-end requires a wholly separate sale or gift agreement. In Musharakah/Mudarabah, bank earnings depend directly on client business results — profit shared per agreed ratio, loss borne per capital share. Beyond these core modes, Islamic banks may also earn service charges/management fees (but never a fee on lending itself, as that would be Riba) and non-fund-based income (e.g. fund management under Wakalatul Istismar for a fixed fee, where all profit/loss belongs to the client, and the bank earns only its fixed management fee for the service). Default penalties charged to clients cannot be credited to the bank's own Profit & Loss account (they go to charity, per Chapter 3's rule).","examExplanation":"Conventional banks 'deal in documents, not goods' — no ownership/risk in the underlying assets, pure financiers. Islamic banks deal in goods and documents, not money — they must take actual ownership and bear real asset-related risk before earning profit. Mode-by-mode: Murabaha/Salam — bank purchases goods (directly or via Wakalah agency), bears risk until sale execution, then risk transfers to the client. Istisna'a — manufacturer delivers to the bank with related risks. Ijarah — bank retains ownership/risk throughout the lease; damage without lessee fault ends the bank's rental entitlement. Musharakah/Mudarabah — bank earnings/losses track the client's actual business results. Islamic banks may also earn service/management fees (e.g. Wakalatul Istismar — fixed fee for fund management, with all profit/loss belonging to the client) but never a fee purely on lending.","keyPoints":["Conventional banks 'deal in documents, not goods' — they finance goods purchases via documents/paperwork but take on no ownership or risk in the underlying goods themselves.","Islamic banks deal in goods and documents, not money — money is used only as a medium to purchase goods for onward sale/lease, and the bank must take genuine ownership and bear real risk before earning profit.","In Murabaha/Salam, the bank purchases goods (directly or via Wakalah agency) and bears ownership risk until sale execution, at which point risk transfers to the client, who must then pay at the settled time regardless.","In Ijarah, ownership and related risks/expenses remain with the bank throughout the lease; if the asset is damaged (without lessee fault) and can no longer deliver its intended benefit, the bank's right to receive rental ceases.","Forward foreign exchange with delayed payment on either side, and most financial futures, are unavailable in Shari'ah-compliant Islamic finance (being interest-based-system hedging tools), though the spot FX market functions without issue.","Islamic banks may earn non-fund-based income such as a fixed management fee under Wakalatul Istismar (fund management), where all profit/loss belongs to the client and the bank earns only its fixed service fee — never a fee purely for lending, which would be Riba."],"definitions":[{"term":"Wakalah","definition":"An agency arrangement, e.g. where an Islamic bank purchases goods through an appointed agent for its own inventory or on a client's order."},{"term":"Wakalatul Istismar","definition":"A fund-management agency arrangement in which the bank manages a client's investment for a fixed management fee, with all profit or loss belonging to the client, not the bank."},{"term":"Parallel Salam","definition":"A second, independent Salam contract entered into by a bank to sell a commodity it purchased under an original Salam contract — permissible provided the two contracts are not linked or made conditional on each other."}],"conditions":["A Parallel Salam contract must not be linked to or made conditional on the original Salam contract — each is honoured independently regardless of the other's performance.","Transfer of a leased asset's ownership to the lessee at lease-end requires a separate sale or gift agreement, with its own conditions."],"principles":["Islamic banks are entitled to profit/rental only for the period and to the extent that they actually bear the related ownership risk of the underlying goods or assets."],"processSteps":["Bank purchases/acquires the commodity (directly, via Wakalah agent, or via a manufacturer under Istisna'a), taking on ownership and risk.","Bank sells or leases the commodity/asset to the client (Murabaha sale, Salam delivery, or Ijarah lease).","Ownership-related risk transfers to the client upon sale execution (Bai'/Murabaha) or remains with the bank throughout the lease term (Ijarah)."],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume Islamic banks simply 'don't handle money' at all; in fact they use money constantly, but only as a medium for purchasing/exchanging real goods and assets — the distinction is that money itself is never the object being sold/leased for a return."],"distinctions":["Conventional banks ('deal in documents, not goods' — no asset ownership/risk) vs Islamic banks ('deal in goods and documents, not money' — must take genuine ownership and bear real asset risk)."],"relatedConcepts":["Murabaha","Salam","Istisna'a","Ijarah","Wakalah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[82,83]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Conventional banks","b":"Islamic banks","text":"Conventional: deal in money and documents, no responsibility for goods. Islamic: deal in goods/assets, owning them and bearing related risks."}]},{"id":"t4.2.8","section":"4.2.8","title":"Transparency and Documentation","pages":[83,84],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["transparency","murabaha"],"intuition":"Why must Islamic banks disclose more than conventional banks?","simpleExplanation":"Islamic banks must be more transparent. In Murabaha, cost and payment details must be disclosed or the transaction may become non-compliant. The Qur’an requires writing down credit transactions. As partners in trade, banks must know their clients’ business, making them less likely to be used for money laundering.","academicExplanation":["Islamic financial institutions must adopt transparency, disclosure and documentation to a greater extent than conventional banks. Lack of transparency in Murabaha (cost/price and payment mode) may render the transaction non-compliant. The Qur’an enjoins writing down and taking witnesses in credit transactions. Because Islamic finance funds specific physical assets, clients must have socially beneficial, value-adding business. As a partner in trade the bank must concern itself with the client’s business and profitability; the author believes Islamic banks are less likely to engage in money laundering and terrorism financing."],"examExplanation":"Greater transparency/disclosure/documentation; Murabaha cost disclosure essential; Qur’an requires recording credit; bank as trade partner must know client — author: less prone to money laundering.","keyPoints":["Murabaha without cost disclosure may be non-compliant.","The author believes Islamic banks are less likely to be used for money laundering and terrorist financing because they must know the client’s business."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Murabaha without disclosed cost","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank sells goods “at 110” without telling the client that the cost was 100. Murabaha is a trust sale based on disclosed cost, so hiding the cost or payment terms can make the transaction non-compliant (pp. 83–84)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Disclosure requirements for Islamic banks are the same as for conventional banks.","right":"They are stricter: lack of transparency on Murabaha cost and payment terms can make the transaction non-compliant, and banks must examine the nature of the client’s business (pp. 83–84)."}],"relatedTopics":["t9.3","t3.3.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why may lack of transparency invalidate a Murabaha?","options":["Murabaha requires disclosure of cost/price and payment mode","Murabaha is a loan","It creates Maisir","It is not a sale"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 83."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.2.8","sectionTitle":"Transparency and Documentation / Additional Risks Faced by Islamic Banks","title":"Transparency, Documentation, and the Additional Risks Islamic Banks Face","covers":["t4.2.8"],"overview":"The author explains why Islamic banks require even greater transparency/disclosure than conventional banks, and catalogs the additional, distinctive risks (asset, market, Shari'ah non-compliance, rate-of-return, fiduciary, legal, withdrawal) that come from actually owning and dealing in real assets.","simpleExplanation":"Because Islamic banks actually own and trade real assets (not just paperwork), they must be far more transparent about costs, prices and payment terms than conventional banks — especially in Murabaha deals, where hiding cost/price details can make the whole transaction non-compliant. Being a real trading/leasing partner (not just a passive lender) also means Islamic banks face risks conventional banks don't have to worry about as much: risk from actually owning goods, risk that a transaction turns out to be non-Shari'ah-compliant (with real financial and reputational consequences), and greater legal risk from being directly involved with physical assets. A large-scale banker survey found Islamic bankers see these unique risks as more serious than ordinary risks, and see profit-sharing and deferred-sale products (Salam, Istisna'a) as riskier than Murabaha and Ijarah.","academicExplanation":"Islamic banks/financial institutions must adopt transparency, disclosure and documentation to a greater degree than conventional banks; lack of transparency in Murabaha transactions (requiring full disclosure of cost/price and payment mode) can itself render a transaction non-Shari'ah-compliant. This is grounded in the Qur'anic injunction to write down and take witnesses for credit-involving transactions, and the Prophet's own encouragement of full disclosure about traded goods within a competitive, well-informed market. Islamic banks' disclosure standards are more stringent because their role isn't a passive financier concerned only with interest/loan-recovery — they finance specific physical assets (machinery, inventory, equipment), so their clients' businesses must be socially beneficial and genuinely value-adding, not merely paper-profitable or antisocial. As a partner in trade, an Islamic bank must actively assess its clients' business nature and profitability (to manage loss and reputational risk), leading the author to state his own view that Islamic banks are, as a result, less likely to engage in money laundering or terrorism financing than conventional banks. Additional Risks (4.2.9): even though Islamic banks can genuinely take collateral, heavy reliance on it is inappropriate given transaction-specific risks, requiring more careful risk evaluation. The distinctive additional risks are: asset risk, market risk, Shari'ah non-compliance risk, greater rate-of-return risk, greater fiduciary risk, greater legal risk, and greater withdrawal risk. Asset risk is present across modes but is particularly notable in Murabaha (before onward sale), Salam (post-delivery from the Salam seller) and Ijarah, since all ownership-related risk belongs to the bank while it holds the goods; in Shirkah modes, risk tracks ownership share. Government/economic policy shifts can create asset/price/rate-of-return risk; notably, Murabaha receivables cannot be increased even if a general market benchmark rate later rises (since the debt is fixed once created). Non-Shari'ah compliance carries compounding consequences: related income must go to the Charity Account, but it can also generate credibility/reputational risk, potentially triggering withdrawal risk and a broader industry 'contagion effect'. Physical-asset involvement also creates greater legal risk than conventional banks face. A cited survey (Khan and Habib, 2001, of 17 Islamic financial institutions) found IFIs face distinctive risks from profit-sharing investment deposits not faced by conventional institutions, which bankers rate as more serious than conventional risks; banks worry depositors will hold them responsible for below-market returns, risking withdrawal; the survey also found bankers judge profit-sharing modes and product-deferred sales (Salam, Istisna'a) riskier than Murabaha and Ijarah, and that while risk-management environments are relatively good, measurement/mitigation/monitoring processes and internal controls need further upgrading; problems identified include a lack of money-market instruments and an unsupportive legal/regulatory framework, which could itself be a source of systemic risk. Risk mitigation requires special Shari'ah expertise, since Shari'ah fixes each party's responsibilities/liabilities per contract, and these cannot be avoided; banks can manage risk only up to a limit, beyond which they must absorb it themselves. Specific mode-level mitigation mechanisms: in Ijarah, asset-loss risk (absent lessee negligence) stays with the bank — it cannot shift this to the lessee in addition to charging rent, per the juristic rule 'Al Ujrah wal Dhaman La Tajtami'an' (wage/rent and liability/responsibility do not combine); the bank may build risk-management costs into rentals with the lessee's free consent, subject to relevant rules. In Mudarabah, the bank (as Mudarib/manager) gets no remuneration at all if the business incurs loss. For Salam goods, the bank can transfer price/asset risk via Parallel Salam (with each contract remaining independent) or via a purchase promise from a prospective buyer. Default risk can be mitigated via a penalty clause (proceeds to the Charity Account) in most modes; in Istisna'a specifically, a price-decrease clause for delivery delay is used instead ('Shart-e-Jazai'), because Istisna'a's manufacturing/construction process depends heavily on the manufacturer's personal effort/commitment (who may be juggling other contracts simultaneously), unlike Murabaha/Salam where one party simply owes a stipulated deferred payment.","examExplanation":"Islamic banks require greater transparency/disclosure than conventional banks (especially in Murabaha, where cost/price non-disclosure can void Shari'ah compliance), grounded in the Qur'anic command to document credit transactions. Distinctive additional risks Islamic banks face: asset risk (notably in Murabaha, Salam, Ijarah — ownership-period risk), market risk, Shari'ah non-compliance risk (income to Charity Account, plus reputational/withdrawal/contagion effects), greater rate-of-return risk (Murabaha receivables can't be raised even if benchmarks rise), greater fiduciary risk, greater legal risk (from physical asset involvement), and greater withdrawal risk. The Khan and Habib (2001) survey of 17 IFIs found bankers see profit-sharing/deferred-sale (Salam, Istisna'a) risks as more serious than conventional risks, and riskier than Murabaha/Ijarah. Mode-specific risk mitigation: Ijarah — asset-loss risk stays with the bank (per 'Al Ujrah wal Dhaman La Tajtami'an' — rent and liability cannot combine on the same party); Mudarabah — no Mudarib remuneration if the business loses; Salam — risk transferable via Parallel Salam or purchase promise; default risk — penalty clause (proceeds to charity) in most modes, but a price-decrease clause ('Shart-e-Jazai') for delivery delay specifically in Istisna'a.","keyPoints":["Islamic banks require greater transparency/disclosure than conventional banks; lack of full cost/price/payment-mode disclosure in a Murabaha transaction can itself render it non-Shari'ah-compliant.","The author's own view: because Islamic banks act as trading/leasing partners requiring genuine business/profitability assessment of clients (to manage loss and reputational risk), they are less likely to engage in money laundering or terrorism financing than conventional banks.","Distinctive additional risks facing Islamic banks: asset risk, market risk, Shari'ah non-compliance risk, greater rate-of-return risk, greater fiduciary risk, greater legal risk, and greater withdrawal risk.","Non-Shari'ah-compliant income must go to the Charity Account, but non-compliance also creates credibility/reputational risk, which can trigger withdrawal risk and a broader industry 'contagion effect'.","The juristic rule 'Al Ujrah wal Dhaman La Tajtami'an' (wage/rent and liability/responsibility do not combine) means an Ijarah lessor bears asset-loss risk itself and cannot additionally shift that risk to the lessee while still collecting rent.","In Istisna'a specifically, delivery-delay risk is mitigated via a price-decrease clause ('Shart-e-Jazai') rather than the penalty-to-charity clause used in other modes, because Istisna'a depends heavily on the manufacturer's personal effort and commitment across potentially concurrent contracts."],"definitions":[{"term":"Al Ujrah wal Dhaman La Tajtami'an","definition":"A juristic rule meaning 'wage/rent and liability/responsibility do not combine' — an Ijarah lessor who bears asset-related liability cannot also shift that same risk onto the lessee while still collecting rent."},{"term":"Shart-e-Jazai","definition":"A price-decrease clause used in Istisna'a contracts specifically to address delivery delay, reflecting Istisna'a's dependence on the manufacturer's personal effort and commitment."}],"conditions":["Full disclosure of cost, price and payment mode is required in Murabaha transactions; lack of such transparency can render the transaction non-Shari'ah-compliant.","An Ijarah lessor cannot shift asset-loss risk (absent lessee negligence) onto the lessee while still collecting rent — risk-management costs may only be built into rentals with the lessee's free, mutual consent."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students may assume default penalties in Islamic finance function like conventional late fees (bank revenue); the chapter is clear that penalty proceeds go to the Charity Account, not the bank's own income, in virtually all modes except Istisna'a, which instead uses a price-decrease mechanism (Shart-e-Jazai)."],"distinctions":["Penalty-clause mitigation (proceeds to charity — used in most modes, e.g. Murabaha, Salam) vs price-decrease clause/Shart-e-Jazai mitigation (used specifically in Istisna'a, due to its dependence on the manufacturer's personal effort)."],"relatedConcepts":["Murabaha","Ijarah","Mudarabah","Salam","Istisna'a","Charity Account"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.8","pages":[83,84,85]}}]},{"id":"t4.2.9","section":"4.2.9","title":"Additional Risks Faced by Islamic Banks","pages":[84,85],"tier":"core","concepts":["risk-management"],"intuition":"Because Islamic banks own assets and share profits, what extra risks do they carry?","simpleExplanation":"Besides normal banking risks, Islamic banks face asset, market and Shari’ah non-compliance risks, and greater rate-of-return, fiduciary, legal and withdrawal risks. They can mitigate some (Parallel Salam, promises, penalty clauses to charity) but cannot shift risks Shari’ah assigns to them — e.g. in Ijarah the bank bears asset loss.","academicExplanation":["Islamic banks cannot rely heavily on collateral and must evaluate risk carefully. Additional risks: asset, market and Shari’ah non-compliance risk, and greater rate-of-return, fiduciary, legal and withdrawal risk. Asset risk arises in Murabaha (before onward sale), Salam (after delivery) and Ijarah (throughout); in Shirkah per ownership share. Murabaha receivables cannot be enhanced if benchmarks rise. Non-compliance sends income to charity and creates credibility risk, withdrawal risk and a contagion effect.","Khan and Habib’s (2001) survey of 17 IFIs: unique risks from profit-sharing deposits are considered more serious; banks feel returns must match competitors or depositors will withdraw; PLS modes and Salam/Istisna‘a are judged riskier than Murabaha and Ijarah; risk measurement, mitigation and controls need upgrading; lack of money-market instruments and an unsupportive legal/regulatory framework are problems.","Mitigation requires Shari’ah expertise; responsibilities assigned by Shari’ah cannot be avoided. In Ijarah the bank bears asset loss not caused by lessee negligence (maxim: Al Ujrah wal Dhaman La Tajtami‘an — rent and liability do not combine), though risk-management costs can be built into rentals by mutual consent. A Mudarib gets nothing if the Mudarabah loses. Salam risks can be transferred via Parallel Salam (independent contracts) or a promise to purchase. Default risk is mitigated by a penalty clause with proceeds to charity — in all modes except Istisna‘a, where a price-reduction clause for late delivery (Shart-e-Jazai) is allowed because manufacture depends on the manufacturer’s effort."],"examExplanation":"Extra risks: asset, market, Shari’ah non-compliance; greater rate-of-return, fiduciary, legal, withdrawal risks. Mitigate via Parallel Salam, promises, penalty-to-charity; Istisna‘a allows Shart-e-Jazai (price reduction for delay). Ijarah: lessor bears asset loss (Al Ujrah wal Dhaman La Tajtami‘an).","keyPoints":["Murabaha receivable cannot increase if market rates rise (rate-of-return risk).","Non-compliance → income to charity + reputational/withdrawal risk.","Shart-e-Jazai permitted only in Istisna‘a.","Rent and liability do not combine on the same party."],"definitions":[{"term":"Shart-e-Jazai","definition":"Penalty clause reducing the price of an Istisna‘a asset in case of delay in delivery."},{"term":"Al Ujrah wal Dhaman La Tajtami‘an","definition":"Maxim: wage/rent and liability/responsibility do not add up together — a lessee paying rent cannot also be made to bear the asset risk."}],"examples":[{"title":"Khan and Habib survey (2001)","kind":"textbook","text":"A survey of 17 IFIs found bankers view risks from profit-sharing deposits as more serious than conventional risks, fear withdrawals if returns lag, and see PLS modes and Salam/Istisna as riskier than Murabaha and Ijarah (p. 84)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t9.10","t10.10.1","t10.11.11","t11.3.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In which mode may a bank include a clause reducing the price for delayed delivery (Shart-e-Jazai)?","options":["Murabaha","Salam","Istisna‘a","Ijarah"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 85."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"table":{"caption":"Where asset risk sits (Section 4.2.9)","head":["Mode","When the bank bears asset risk"],"rows":[["Murabaha","From purchase until onward sale to client"],["Salam","After taking delivery from the Salam seller"],["Ijarah","Throughout the lease (unless lessee negligence)"],["Shirkah","In proportion to share in ownership"]]}},{"id":"t4.3","section":"4.3","title":"Debt versus Equity","pages":[85,86],"tier":"core","concepts":["mode-preference"],"intuition":"Are Murabaha and Ijarah second-class substitutes for ‘true’ Islamic finance? The author says the issue is preference, not prohibition.","simpleExplanation":"Debt will always be part of Islamic finance — the Prophet (pbuh) himself took debts. The rule is that debt must not carry interest. Equity-based modes are preferable, but trade and Ijarah suit risk-averse savers (e.g. a widow). The aim is a healthy balance.","academicExplanation":["Islamic institutions create genuine debt through trading. The issue is not debt versus equity, but greater reliance on equity and subjecting debt to the rule that once created it should not increase on the basis of opportunity cost. Shirkah modes are not always usable or advisable given investors’ risk profiles — a widow may need low-risk, compliant investment in trade and Ijarah. Contrary to writers who say only Shirkah can replace interest, debt has always existed and will remain important; the issue is preference for equity over debt-creating modes, not the permissibility of the latter. The aim is a healthy balance; heavy reliance on debt is risky (the US example)."],"examExplanation":"Not debt vs equity but preference for equity with interest-free debt. Debt modes (Murabaha, Salam, Ijarah) remain valid tools; balance needed; risk-averse investors need trade/Ijarah.","keyPoints":["Debt is permissible if it carries no interest.","Preference, not prohibition."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"The risk-averse widow","kind":"textbook","text":"A widow who cannot bear loss asks the bank to invest her money safely; as trustee the bank should use trade and Ijarah, which create debt — hence debt remains part of Islamic finance (pp. 85–86)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Only equity-based (Shirkah) modes are genuine alternatives to interest.","right":"The author rejects this; debt has always existed (the Prophet borrowed), and the issue is that debt must not carry interest, with a preference for equity (p. 86)."}],"relatedTopics":["t8.6","t17.3.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What is the author’s position on debt-creating modes?","options":["They are prohibited","They are permissible; equity modes are preferable but debt modes remain valid tools","They should replace equity","Only for governments"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 86."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.3","sectionTitle":"Debt versus Equity","title":"Debt versus Equity in Islamic Finance","covers":["t4.3"],"overview":"The author clarifies that Islamic finance is not simply 'equity-only' — debt-creating modes remain legitimate and necessary — with the real issue being a preference for equity over debt and ensuring debt, once created, cannot increase like conventional interest-bearing debt.","simpleExplanation":"A common myth is that true Islamic finance means only partnership/equity-style financing (Musharakah, Mudarabah) and that debt is somehow un-Islamic. That's wrong — debt naturally arises from trading and leasing activities that Islamic banks genuinely do, and it's shown honestly on their balance sheets. The real issue isn't 'debt vs. equity' as a yes/no choice — it's that Islamic finance prefers equity-based modes where suitable, while insisting that debt (once it exists) can never grow the way conventional interest-bearing debt does. Debt is even necessary for risk-averse clients (like a widow who can't afford to lose her principal in a risky partnership) — for them, a bank must use safer trade/lease-based modes, which naturally create debt.","academicExplanation":"The author corrects a common misconception: debt is not eliminated in Islamic finance — trading activities create genuine debt shown honestly on Islamic financial institutions' balance sheets. The real issue is not 'debt versus equity' as a binary, but rather (a) greater reliance on equity where appropriate, and (b) subjecting any debt, once created, to the Shari'ah principle that it cannot increase based on conventional opportunity-cost theory (i.e., no interest-like growth). Shirkah-based modes are often unsuitable or inadvisable for certain investors given their risk profile — the author's example: a risk-averse widow needing an Islamic banker to invest in less-risky (but still Shari'ah-compliant) business, since she cannot bear Shirkah-based loss risk; the bank, as trustee, must then invest such funds in trade/Ijarah-based activities, which inherently generate debt. The author explicitly rejects the view (held by 'many authors, both economists and financial experts') that Shirkah/equity-based modes are the ONLY legitimate alternative to interest — debt has existed 'forever' and remains a permanent, important part of individual/national economics; the Prophet himself incurred debt, both personally and for State needs (detailed in Chapter 7). The only real constraint is that debt must never carry interest — debt-creating modes (Murabaha, Salam, Ijarah) therefore remain legitimate operating tools for Islamic financial institutions; the issue is not the permissibility of debt-creating modes as such, but achieving a healthy balance and a preference for equity-based modes over debt-creating ones, for the prosperity of the economy and society. The author warns that an economy with heavy reliance on debt is inherently risky, citing US personal/public debt as a case where instability concerns would already have materialized if not for the US's unique position as sole superpower and Dollar reserve-currency status — with IMF, World Bank and WTO policies allegedly also helping sustain the US economy's heavy debt at the global economy's expense.","examExplanation":"Myth corrected: Islamic finance does not eliminate debt or require exclusively equity-based (Shirkah) financing — debt-creating modes (Murabaha, Salam, Ijarah) remain fully legitimate. The real issue is (a) preferring equity-based modes over debt-creating ones where appropriate, and (b) ensuring debt, once created, cannot increase per conventional opportunity-cost/interest logic. Debt-creating modes are necessary for risk-averse clients (e.g. the widow example) who cannot bear Shirkah-based loss risk. The Prophet himself incurred debt (personal and State), showing debt itself is not inherently problematic — only interest-bearing debt is.","keyPoints":["Debt is not eliminated in Islamic finance — trading/leasing activities genuinely create debt, honestly reflected on Islamic financial institutions' balance sheets.","The real issue is not 'debt versus equity' as a binary choice, but (a) a preference for equity-based modes where appropriate, and (b) ensuring debt, once created, cannot increase per conventional opportunity-cost/interest logic.","The author explicitly rejects the view that Shirkah/equity-based modes are the ONLY legitimate alternative to interest — debt has always existed and remains a permanent, important part of economic life.","Debt-creating modes are necessary for risk-averse clients (e.g. a widow who cannot bear Shirkah-based loss risk); the bank, as trustee, must invest such funds in trade/Ijarah-based activities, which inherently generate debt.","The Prophet himself incurred debt, both personally and for State needs, demonstrating debt itself is not inherently problematic in Islam — only interest-bearing debt is prohibited.","The author warns that heavy reliance on debt makes an economy inherently risky, citing US personal/public debt sustained largely by its unique superpower/reserve-currency status rather than sound underlying fundamentals."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Debt, once created through a legitimate trade or lease transaction, cannot subsequently increase based on conventional opportunity-cost/interest-like reasoning."],"principles":["Islamic finance's goal is a healthy balance between debt-based and equity-based financing, with a preference for equity where suitable, not the elimination of debt altogether."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The risk-averse widow example","kind":"textbook","text":"A widow needing safer investment cannot bear the loss risk inherent in Shirkah-based (equity/partnership) business. An Islamic bank, acting as trustee, must instead invest her funds in trade and Ijarah-based activities suited to her risk profile — activities that inherently generate debt, illustrating why debt-creating modes remain necessary, not just tolerated."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often equate 'true' or 'ideal' Islamic finance exclusively with Musharakah/Mudarabah (equity/PLS modes); the chapter explicitly corrects this, stating debt-creating modes (Murabaha, Salam, Ijarah) are equally legitimate and necessary, not a lesser or compromised form of Islamic finance."],"distinctions":["Debt as such (permissible, has always existed, including for the Prophet himself) vs interest-bearing debt (Riba, always prohibited) — the chapter is emphatic these are not the same thing."],"relatedConcepts":["Murabaha","Shirkah","Qard","Risk profile"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.3","pages":[85,86]}}],"debate":[{"issue":"Should Islamic banks use only Shirkah-based modes?","criticism":"Many writers, following the pioneers, say Shirkah/equity modes are the only true alternative to interest.","response":"Debt has always existed; the Prophet (pbuh) incurred debts; the only condition is that debt carries no interest. The issue is preference for equity, not prohibition of debt modes.","alternative":"The minority/pioneer view that Islamic banking in letter and spirit means Shirkah-based transactions (noted in the Preface).","takeaway":"Use all permitted modes with a preference and balance towards equity, considering savers’ risk profiles."}]},{"id":"t4.4","section":"4.4","title":"Islamic Banking: Business versus Benevolence","pages":[86,86],"tier":"core","concepts":["qard-hasan"],"intuition":"Should an Islamic bank give interest-free loans to everyone? No — it manages depositors’ money as a trust.","simpleExplanation":"It is a myth that Islamic banks should be charities giving free loans. Business and benevolence are separate. Banks hold depositors’ money in trust and must earn Halal returns through trade, leasing and services. They may lend free from their own equity or charity fund, but not at the cost of depositors.","academicExplanation":["Islamic banks do business like conventional banks while observing Shari’ah. The myth that they should be social security centres providing only return-free loans must be removed. Individuals may spend on benevolence from their own income, but banks holding depositors’ money in trust cannot dole it out. Middle-class savers’ funds are used by generally richer business groups; banks share income with fund owners. Return-free loans from the bank’s own equity or Charity Fund (with Shari’ah advisor approval) are possible but must not affect fiduciary duties. Social/welfare activities are possible but are not the normal course of business."],"examExplanation":"Business ≠ benevolence. Banks hold deposits in trust → trade, Ijarah, services. Free loans only from own equity/charity fund without harming fiduciary duties.","keyPoints":["Middle-class deposits often fund richer businesses — giving them away free would be unjust to depositors."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Whose money is it?","kind":"textbook","text":"A widow’s savings deposited in a bank fund a large trading firm. If the bank lent them free, the richer firm would gain at the widow’s expense. Free loans can come only from the bank’s own equity or Charity Fund, with Shari’ah approval (p. 86)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic banks should provide cost-free money.","right":"Islamic banking is a business using savers’ funds; it does not mean cost-free money."}],"relatedTopics":["t17.3.9","t7.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Out of which funds may an Islamic bank give return-free loans?","options":["Depositors’ investment funds at its discretion","Its own equity or accumulated Charity Fund, with Shari’ah approval","Current account balances","None at all"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 86."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.4","sectionTitle":"Islamic Banking: Business versus Benevolence","title":"Islamic Banking: Business versus Benevolence","covers":["t4.4"],"overview":"The author dispels the myth that Islamic banks should function as charities/social-security institutions, clarifying that Islamic banks are businesses with fiduciary duties to depositors, while benevolence remains a separate, optional activity funded differently.","simpleExplanation":"Some people think Islamic banks should mainly give out free (interest-free, no-strings) loans as charity. That's a myth the author wants to correct: business and benevolence/charity are two separate things. Individual people can and should be charitable with their own money, and get spiritual reward for it — but a bank holds other people's (depositors') money as a trust, and can't just give that money away at its own discretion. Since it's usually middle-class savers whose money funds business activity for wealthier entrepreneurs, the bank's job is to do real, profitable, Shari'ah-compliant business (trading, leasing, profit-sharing) and pass income back to depositors — charitable activities can only come from the bank's own equity funds or a separate accumulated Charity Fund, with Shari'ah-board approval, and shouldn't compromise the bank's core duty to depositors.","academicExplanation":"The author identifies and dispels a specific myth: that Islamic banks should function as social-security centres, providing mainly return-free loans/charity to the needy. He insists business and benevolence are two separate things — individuals may spend for benevolence from their own income (rewarded in the Hereafter per Shari'ah), but banks holding depositors' money as a trust cannot dole out those trust funds at their own discretion. He notes the typical funding-flow pattern: the 'middle class' across societies generally keeps funds in banks, which are then used by business groups who are generally more affluent/richer than the masses — Islamic banks do business with these mobilized funds and pass a portion of the resulting income back to fund owners (depositors/investors). A bank may choose to provide return-free loans specifically from its own equity funds or an accumulated 'Charity Fund' (with Shari'ah advisor approval), or engage in other social-security activities, but this must not compromise its fiduciary responsibilities toward depositors. To meet these fiduciary duties, banks conduct trading and Ijarah business, provide agency-based fee services, and apply risk-mitigation techniques within Shari'ah limits. In sum: Islamic banks sell goods they've purchased at a profit, lease assets for rentals, and share profit/bear loss from Shirkah-based investments — thereby helping society by facilitating asset-based investment and the supply of risk-based capital; they may participate in social/welfare activities subject to board policy and stakeholder consultation, but this is not, and should not become, their normal course of business.","examExplanation":"Myth dispelled: Islamic banks are NOT meant to function as charities/social-security institutions providing free loans as their core business. Business and benevolence are separate: individuals may give charity from their own income, but banks hold depositors' money as a trust and cannot dole it out at will. Charitable/return-free-loan activity by a bank must come specifically from the bank's own equity funds or a separate accumulated Charity Fund (with Shari'ah board approval) — never from depositor funds meant for the bank's fiduciary business activities. Islamic banks' actual business: selling purchased goods at a profit, leasing assets for rentals, sharing Shirkah-based profit/loss.","keyPoints":["Myth dispelled: Islamic banks are not meant to primarily function as charities or social-security centres providing free loans — business and benevolence are two distinct things.","Individuals may spend for benevolence from their own personal income and be rewarded accordingly in the Hereafter, but banks hold depositors' money as a trust and cannot dole out trust funds at their own discretion.","Typical funding flow: middle-class savers deposit funds in banks, which are then deployed toward business activity by generally more affluent business groups; Islamic banks pass back a portion of resulting income to depositors/investors.","A bank's return-free loans or charitable activity must be funded specifically from its own equity funds or a separate accumulated 'Charity Fund' (requiring Shari'ah advisor approval), not from depositor funds meant for its core business.","Islamic banks' actual, normal business consists of selling purchased goods at a profit, leasing assets against rentals, and sharing profit/bearing loss from Shirkah-based investments — social/welfare participation is possible but subordinate to this core business."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["A bank's charitable/return-free-loan activity must be funded from its own equity funds or a dedicated, Shari'ah-advisor-approved Charity Fund — not from depositor trust funds intended for the bank's core fiduciary business."],"principles":["Business and benevolence are two separate things: an Islamic bank's fiduciary duty to depositors takes priority over discretionary charitable activity with depositor-sourced funds."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume 'Islamic banking' implies interest-free = free-of-charge banking; the chapter is explicit that Islamic banks are ordinary profit-seeking businesses (avoiding Riba/Gharar/Maisir), not charitable institutions — 'interest-free' does not mean 'cost-free'."],"distinctions":["Individual benevolence (an individual's discretionary charitable spending from their own income) vs institutional fiduciary duty (a bank's obligation to manage depositor trust funds for genuine business purposes, not discretionary charity)."],"relatedConcepts":["Qard al-Hasan","Charity Fund","Shirkah","Trust (Amanah)"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.4","pages":[86,87]}}]},{"id":"t4.5","section":"4.5","title":"Exchange Rules","pages":[87,89],"tier":"core","concepts":["bai-sarf","riba-fadl","forex"],"intuition":"Conventional markets allow both sides of a trade to be deferred. Why can’t Islamic finance?","simpleExplanation":"In Islamic finance only one side of an exchange may be deferred, and you cannot sell what you do not own or possess. Currencies must be exchanged spot (OIC Fiqh Council, 1998). Forward cover can only be a promise, with the actual exchange simultaneous; Salam in currencies is not valid.","academicExplanation":["The strategic difference: in conventional finance both items can be deferred and goods/options sold without ownership or risk; in Islamic finance only one item may be delayed and goods not owned or possessed cannot be sold. Goods (other than monetary units), durables and shares in asset pools can be exchanged for money at market prices with at least one item spot. Athman follow Bai‘ al Sarf. Usufruct and services may be paid in advance, spot or deferred. Loans/debts are paid without premium or discount and cannot be sold except to the original debtor at face value.","OIC Fiqh Council (11th session, 1998): deferred sale of currencies, or fixing a future date for exchange, is not permissible; governments should regulate money markets. Ninth session (1995): crediting a customer’s account (directly, by transfer, via Sarf purchase, or via cross-currency transfer) is valid; usual processing delay is allowed, but the beneficiary cannot deal in the currency until it is drawable. Nawavi’s rules; futures in gold and silver (as Thaman) are forbidden. Debt settlement should reference the original legal standard and date.","Currency futures: some scholars forbid all; others forbid one spot/one deferred but permit a future exchange of both at an agreed rate. Forward cover can therefore be taken only as a promise, for real trade needs and not speculation, with simultaneous exchange. Salam in currencies (even in Fulus) is invalid: paper money can only be a price; deferring both sides is debt against debt."],"examExplanation":"Only one counter-value may be deferred; no sale of unowned goods. Currencies: spot only (OIC 1998). Forward cover via promise for genuine needs, simultaneous exchange. Salam in currency invalid. Debts not sold except to debtor at face value.","keyPoints":["Only one item of an exchange may be deferred.","Currencies: same currency — equal and spot; different — spot.","Usual crediting delay allowed, but no dealing until drawable.","Promise-based forward cover only for real needs."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Bank transfer across currencies (OIC 1995)","kind":"textbook","text":"When a bank debits one currency and credits another, a short customary delay before funds are available is allowed, but the beneficiary cannot deal in the currency until it is fully credited (pp. 87–88)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A currency forward contract can be structured as Salam.","right":"Paper money can only be a price; currency exchange needs simultaneous payment, so Salam in currencies is invalid."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1.d","t8.8.7","t14.4.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"How can an Islamic bank provide forward cover in currencies to an importer, per Section 4.5?","options":["A forward contract with one side deferred","A promise to exchange at an agreed rate, with simultaneous actual exchange later, for real needs","Salam in currency","An option contract"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 89."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.5","sectionTitle":"Exchange Rules","title":"Exchange Rules in Islamic Finance: Currencies, Commodities, and Deferred Settlement Limits","covers":["t4.5"],"overview":"The author details the Shari'ah rules governing exchange contracts — spot vs deferred settlement, currency-specific rules under Bai' al Sarf, and how these differ fundamentally from the flexibility of conventional finance, including OIC Fiqh Council resolutions on currency dealing and Salam's inapplicability to currency exchange.","simpleExplanation":"Conventional finance lets you delay both sides of a trade (both payment and delivery) and even resell an 'option' without ever really owning the underlying asset. Islamic finance is much stricter: at most one side of an exchange can be delayed, and you can never sell something you don't actually own or possess. Currencies and precious metals have their own special rule (from the Hadith on the six commodities): if you're exchanging the same currency, it must be equal amounts and immediate; different currencies can be exchanged in different amounts but still immediately (hand-to-hand) — no waiting. This is why an official Islamic finance council ruled that you can't sell currencies for future delivery or fix a future date to exchange them, and why currency futures (delayed on either side) are generally forbidden, though a 'promise' to exchange later at today's rate is allowed as long as the real exchange itself still happens all at once when it occurs.","academicExplanation":"Islamic banks' activities involve exchanging goods for money in various forms (simultaneous spot exchange, spot delivery with deferred payment, spot payment with deferred delivery). Shari'ah exchange rules differ fundamentally from conventional finance's flexibility: conventionally, both exchange items can be deferred, and goods/options can be resold without the seller ever taking ownership or possession-related risk. In Islamic finance, only ONE side of an exchange contract may be delayed, and goods not owned/possessed cannot be sold. Exchange rules vary by contract/wealth type: (1) goods other than gold/silver/monetary units, durable assets and pooled-asset shares can be exchanged for money at market-based pricing, with at least one side delivered on the spot; (2) gold, silver, or monetary units (Athman) fall under Bai' al Sarf rules — equal-for-equal and hand-to-hand for homogeneous currency, hand-to-hand only (excess/deficiency allowed) for different currency units; (3) usufruct/services can be exchanged for rentals/wages paid in advance, on the spot, or deferred; (4) loans/debts must be repaid without premium/discount and cannot be sold except at face value with recourse to the original debtor. These rules trace to the six-commodities Hadith (gold, silver, wheat, barley, dates, salt — two monetary-unit items, four edible items), from which jurists derived two 'Illah (effective causes) applied via analogy to other goods. The OIC Fiqh Council (11th session, 1998) resolved it is impermissible to sell currencies by deferred sale or even to fix a future date for exchanging them (grounded in Qur'an, Sunnah, Ijma'), and separately observed that contemporary money-market transactions are a major factor behind global financial crises/instability, recommending Muslim governments regulate currency/money markets per Islamic Shari'ah as 'the safety valve against economic disaster'. The OIC Fiqh Council (9th session, 1995) also ruled on bank-account currency-crediting mechanics: money credited to a customer's account (directly, via transfer, via Sarf currency purchase, or via bank-executed debit/credit between accounts/currencies) may involve a brief delay to enable the beneficiary to draw the funds, provided it doesn't exceed the transaction's normal customary period — but the beneficiary cannot deal in the credited currency until the crediting is fully effective (drawable). Imam Nawavi's three-tier exchange rule (restated, applied to commutative contracts generally): different 'Illah (e.g. gold/dollars for wheat) — excess/deficiency and delay both permitted; same commodities (gold for gold, wheat for wheat) — both prohibited, must be equal and immediate; same 'Illah but heterogeneous (gold for silver, Rupees for Dollars, wheat for rice) — excess/deficiency allowed, delay prohibited — hence futures trading in gold/silver (as Thaman) is forbidden. Two further Fiqh points: exchange must be without 'excess', meaning a debt must be settled per the 'original legal standard' (money as medium of exchange), illustrated by the Khyber-dates Hadith (selling low-quality dates for cash, then buying better-quality dates, to avoid Riba); and because money's value can rise/fall with inflation/deflation, debt settlement should reference the original agreement date as a base (illustrated by the Prophet advising a Companion to stipulate price in gold dinars but pay in silver dirhams). Loan contracts specifically require equal-amount repayment: fungible items (currency) repaid exactly in kind; nonfungible goods valued in money terms; excess payment on exchange of two similar goods is prohibited even within a sale (not just a loan). Barter is rare today, but FX dealing is routine banking activity, so currency sale transactions must occur instantly (not deferred) — supported by numerous Hadith. On currency futures specifically: some scholars forbid them outright; others distinguish a forbidden case (one currency delivered spot, the other delayed) from a permitted case (future exchange of both currencies simultaneously at a previously agreed rate) — meaning forward currency cover can only take the form of a non-binding-on-delivery-timing promise to fulfil genuine (not speculative) trader exchange needs, with actual simultaneous exchange occurring when the promise is executed. Using Salam for currency exchange (as some subcontinent scholars suggested for Fulus/inferior-metal coins) is invalid: paper money can only serve as a price (Thaman), not as a Salam commodity, since Salam requires one deferred item (the commodity) and one immediate item (the price) — deferring both (as currency-for-currency Salam effectively would) creates a prohibited debt-for-debt exchange.","examExplanation":"Core rule: in Islamic finance, only ONE side of an exchange contract may be delayed (unlike conventional finance, which permits both sides deferred); goods not owned/possessed cannot be sold. Currency/Athman rules (Bai' al Sarf): same currency — equal amount, hand-to-hand (no excess, no delay); different currencies — excess/deficiency allowed, but still hand-to-hand (no delay). OIC Fiqh Council (1998): selling currencies by deferred sale, or fixing a future exchange date, is impermissible. Currency futures: forbidden if one side is delivered spot and the other delayed; a 'promise' to exchange both currencies simultaneously at a pre-agreed rate (for genuine trade needs, not speculation) is permitted, since the actual exchange remains simultaneous when executed. Salam cannot be used for currency-for-currency exchange, since paper money can only serve as price (Thaman), not as the deferred Salam commodity.","keyPoints":["Core Islamic exchange rule: only ONE side of an exchange contract may be delayed (unlike conventional finance, where both sides can be deferred); goods not owned/possessed cannot be sold.","Bai' al Sarf rules for gold, silver and monetary units: same currency exchange requires equal amount and hand-to-hand (immediate) settlement; different currency exchange allows excess/deficiency in amount but still requires hand-to-hand settlement (no delay permitted on either side).","The OIC Fiqh Council (1998) ruled it is impermissible to sell currencies by deferred sale or to fix a future date for currency exchange, and recommended Muslim governments regulate currency/money markets per Islamic Shari'ah as a safeguard against financial crises.","Currency futures: forbidden if one currency is delivered spot and the other delayed; permitted only as a non-speculative 'promise' to exchange both currencies simultaneously at a previously agreed rate when the actual (simultaneous) exchange later occurs.","Salam cannot be used for currency-for-currency exchange: paper money can only function as the price (Thaman) in Salam, not as the deferred commodity — deferring both currencies would create a prohibited debt-for-debt exchange."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai' al Sarf","definition":"The exchange of gold, silver or monetary units (Athman), governed by rules requiring equal amount and hand-to-hand settlement for homogeneous currency, and hand-to-hand (but not necessarily equal) settlement for different currency units."},{"term":"Athman","definition":"Gold, silver, or any monetary units — items functioning as money/price, governed by the specific exchange rules of Bai' al Sarf."}],"conditions":["In any exchange contract, at most one of the two items being exchanged may have its delivery/payment delayed; goods not owned or possessed by the seller cannot be sold.","Exchange of the same currency/monetary unit must be equal in amount and settled hand-to-hand (no delay); exchange of different currencies allows amount excess/deficiency but still requires hand-to-hand settlement."],"principles":["A debt must be settled with reference to the original legal standard/currency of the agreement; since money's value fluctuates with inflation/deflation, settlement should reference the original agreement date."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Crediting a customer's account across currencies (OIC Fiqh Council, 1995)","kind":"textbook","text":"When a bank debits a customer's account and credits another account in a different currency (per the customer's order), a brief delay to enable the beneficiary to draw the funds is permitted, provided it does not exceed the normal customary period for such transactions — but the beneficiary cannot deal in the credited currency until the crediting is fully effective and drawable."},{"title":"Stipulating price in gold, paying in silver","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet advised a Companion to stipulate a price in gold dinars but pay in silver dirhams — illustrating that debt settlement should reference the original agreement's terms/date as a base, given that the relative value of different monetary metals/currencies fluctuates over time."}],"commonConfusions":["Students may assume currency futures are entirely and universally forbidden; the chapter distinguishes a forbidden form (one side spot, one side delayed) from a permitted 'promise' mechanism (both currencies exchanged simultaneously at a pre-agreed rate when the promise is executed, for genuine — not speculative — trade needs)."],"distinctions":["Conventional finance (both exchange items may be deferred; resale of unowned 'options' permitted) vs Islamic finance (only one side of an exchange may be delayed; unowned/unpossessed goods cannot be sold)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba al-Fadl","Riba al-Nasiah","Salam","Thaman"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87,88,89]}}],"table":{"caption":"Exchange rules by asset type (Section 4.5)","head":["Asset","Rule"],"rows":[["Goods, durables, shares in asset pools","Market pricing; at least one counter-value spot"],["Gold, silver, currencies (Athman)","Bai‘ al Sarf: equal and spot (same); spot (different)"],["Usufruct and services","Rent/wages in advance, spot or deferred"],["Loans/debts","No premium or discount; not sold except to original debtor at face value"]]}},{"id":"t4.6","section":"4.6","title":"Time Value of Money in Islamic Finance","pages":[89,90],"tier":"core","concepts":["time-value"],"intuition":"Does Islam deny the time value of money? No — it recognises it only through the pricing of goods, not in loans.","simpleExplanation":"Scholars almost unanimously allow a credit price higher than the cash price, provided one price is fixed before the parties separate. That is time value through pricing goods. But once the price is fixed, no addition for delay is allowed. Salam prices are lower than spot prices. Time preference is positive in an Islamic economy.","academicExplanation":["There is near consensus that the credit price may exceed the cash price if one price is settled before separation; the Fiqh Academy and Shari’ah boards approve this — acceptance of time value of money in pricing goods. Prohibited is any addition to the agreed price for delay, since the sold commodity belongs to the buyer and the seller cannot re-price it. Time and place legitimately affect prices through market forces. Spot-only exchange of monetary values also reflects time value (one cannot benefit from money received without giving its counter value). In Salam the advance price is genuinely lower than the spot price.","Time valuation is possible only in trade of goods, not in exchanging monetary values or in loans/debts. Valuing the credit period for pricing goods or usufruct differs from conventional opportunity cost or time value; mark-up in trade is permissible if trade rules are observed, but no time value can be added to a loan or debt once created. An overwhelming majority of Islamic economists believe agents have positive time preference; there is no justification for assuming zero time preference."],"examExplanation":"Time value recognised only via pricing of goods/usufruct (credit price > cash price if fixed before separation; Salam price < spot). No time value on loans/debts once created. Positive time preference in Islamic economy.","keyPoints":["One price must be settled before the parties separate.","No re-pricing of sold goods.","Salam price is lower than spot price.","Positive, not zero, time preference."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Place and time affect price","kind":"textbook","text":"A good sold for 100 in a posh area may cost 50 in a middle-class area, or 100 in the morning and 50 in the evening; genuine market factors may likewise make a credit price higher and a Salam price lower than the spot price (p. 89)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic economics assumes a zero rate of time preference.","right":"The author says agents have a positive time preference, reflected through pricing of goods; there is no justification for assuming zero (p. 90)."}],"relatedTopics":["t6.5.3","t17.3.4","t1.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Under what condition may a credit price be higher than the cash price?","options":["Never","If one price is settled before the parties separate","If interest is disclosed","Only for governments"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 89."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t11"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.6","sectionTitle":"Time Value of Money in Islamic Finance","title":"Time Value of Money in Islamic Finance: Pricing, Not Interest","covers":["t4.6"],"overview":"The author explains the near-consensus Shari'ah position that credit prices may legitimately exceed cash prices (time has value in pricing), while sharply distinguishing this from interest — time value is recognized only in genuine sale/lease pricing, never as a return on a loan or debt itself.","simpleExplanation":"Almost all Shari'ah scholars agree: a credit price for something can legitimately be higher than its cash price, as long as the price is fixed and agreed before the deal is finalized. This is the Islamic version of 'time value of money' — but it's fundamentally different from interest. It's not a charge for the passage of time on a loan; it's a genuine pricing decision reflecting market conditions (just like the same item can cost different amounts in different neighborhoods, or morning vs evening). Once a debt is actually created, though, the price is locked — the seller can never add anything more for further delay. And crucially, this kind of 'time value' only applies to trading and leasing of goods, never to loans, debts, or currency exchange — money on its own has no time value in Islamic finance, which is precisely why interest is banned.","academicExplanation":"There is near-consensus among Shari'ah scholars that a commodity's credit price may genuinely exceed its cash price, provided one price is settled before the contracting parties separate — many jurists consider this difference approved by the Nass (clear Shari'ah text); the OIC Fiqh Academy and all Islamic banks' Shari'ah boards approve this legality, which amounts to accepting time value of money in the pricing of goods. What remains prohibited is any addition to an already-agreed price due to subsequent payment delay — because a sold commodity (even on credit) generates a permanent debt belonging to the purchaser, and the seller loses any right to re-price something no longer his. The author elaborates: jurists treat the cash/credit price gap as genuine market practice, no different in principle from how both time and place affect price (a commodity might cost $100 in an affluent area but $50 in a middle-class one; $100 in the morning but $50 by evening) — all acceptable when driven by genuine market forces; similarly, credit prices naturally exceed cash prices at a point in time, while in forward contracts like Salam, the future-delivery price is naturally LESS than the spot price (the seller accepts a discount for receiving payment now). Further evidence for a time-value concept: the Shari'ah's prohibition on gold/silver/monetary-value exchanges except when simultaneous (hand-to-hand) — because a party who received money/currency without giving its counter-value immediately could benefit from using it while the other party gets nothing yet. The Salam contract itself illustrates time value through pricing: it enables immediate payment for future-delivered goods precisely because the advance-paid price is genuinely LESS than the equivalent cash-and-carry price at contract execution. A crucial scope-limiting principle: time valuation is possible ONLY in business/trade of goods, NOT in exchange of monetary values or loans/debts. Islamic economics genuinely allows converting money into assets (whose utility can then be measured/priced), but loaning remains a virtuous act from which no benefit may be taken. So while Islamic finance concedes time value of money to the extent of pricing in credit sales, it does not uphold generating rent on capital the way interest does on credits/advances (which the author says creates a 'rentier class' in society) — valuation of the credit period for pricing goods/usufructs is explicitly different from conventional 'opportunity cost' or 'time value of money' concepts; 'mark-up' in genuine trade (following Shari'ah trade rules) is permissible, but interest (an increase over a loan/debt) remains prohibited; no time value can ever be added to a loan/debt's principal once created or once the purchaser's liability is stipulated. The author closes with a memorable distinction: 'Time is invaluable; once wasted, it cannot be refurbished. So it should not be compared with money, which, if stolen or snatched, can be restored' — business accounts for time as a natural factor balancing supply/demand forces, but this is conceptually distinct from treating time itself as inherently 'renting' money. Consequently, most Islamic economists hold that economic agents in an Islamic economy have a positive time preference (approximated by demand/supply-driven market indicators), rejecting as unjustified the zero-time-preference assumption made in some studies of Islamic investment behaviour.","examExplanation":"Near-consensus Shari'ah position: a credit price may legitimately exceed a cash price, provided one fixed price is settled before the parties separate (approved by clear Shari'ah text, per the OIC Fiqh Academy and Islamic banks' Shari'ah boards) — this IS accepted as 'time value of money' in goods pricing. What's prohibited: any addition to an already-agreed price for subsequent payment delay (the debt, once created, is fixed). Scope limit: time valuation applies ONLY to genuine trade/business pricing of goods (Bai', Ijarah) — NEVER to loans, debts, or monetary exchange, since money itself has no time value and loaning remains a purely virtuous, benefit-free act. This is explicitly different from conventional 'opportunity cost'/interest-based time value. Most Islamic economists hold agents have a positive (not zero) time preference, reflected in market-driven pricing.","keyPoints":["Near-consensus: a commodity's credit price may legitimately exceed its cash price, provided one fixed price is settled before the parties separate — approved by clear Shari'ah text (Nass), the OIC Fiqh Academy, and Islamic banks' Shari'ah boards.","What remains prohibited: any addition to an already-agreed price due to subsequent payment delay — once a debt is created, the price/liability is fixed and the seller cannot re-price something that is no longer theirs.","The cash/credit price gap is treated like the ordinary market effects of time and place on price (e.g. same commodity costing different amounts in different neighbourhoods or times of day) — a genuine market phenomenon, not interest.","In Salam, time value manifests inversely: the advance-paid price for future delivery is genuinely LESS than the equivalent cash-and-carry price at contract execution (a discount for prepayment, not a premium).","Critical scope limit: time valuation applies ONLY in genuine trade/business of goods (sale, lease) — NEVER in exchange of monetary values or loans/debts, since money has no time value on its own and loaning is a virtuous, benefit-free act.","Valuation of the credit period for pricing goods/usufructs is explicitly different from the conventional concepts of 'opportunity cost' or 'time value of money' — Islamic finance accepts pricing-based time value but rejects rent-on-capital (interest), which the author says creates a 'rentier class' in society.","Most Islamic economists hold that economic agents in an Islamic economy have a positive (not zero) time preference, reflected in market-driven pricing indicators — rejecting the zero-time-preference assumption made in some Islamic investment-behaviour studies."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["A commodity's cash and credit prices may differ only if one fixed price is settled before the contracting parties separate; no addition may be made to the agreed price after the debt/liability is created."],"principles":["Time valuation of money is recognized only within the pricing of genuine trade/lease transactions (Bai', Ijarah) — never as a direct charge for the passage of time on a loan or debt, which is the fundamental basis for prohibiting interest while permitting credit-sale markups."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Price variation by time and place (Ayub's illustration)","kind":"textbook","text":"A commodity sold for $100 in an affluent area might sell for $50 in a middle-class residential area; an item priced at $100 in the morning might sell for $50 by evening — both are acceptable, genuine market-driven price variations, illustrating that time (and place) naturally affects price without this constituting Riba."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often conflate the Islamic acceptance of 'time value of money in pricing' with the conventional finance concept of 'time value of money'/opportunity cost applied to interest; the chapter explicitly distinguishes them — the Islamic version only values time through the pricing of an actual sale/lease of goods, never as a direct charge on money/debt itself."],"distinctions":["Islamic 'time value of money' (recognized only via genuine trade/lease pricing, e.g. credit vs cash price) vs conventional 'time value of money'/opportunity cost (a direct charge on money itself, i.e. interest) — the chapter treats these as fundamentally different concepts despite superficial similarity."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Salam","Bai' al Sarf","Positive time preference"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[89,90]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Time value in pricing goods","b":"Time value on money/debts","text":"The first is permitted (credit price, Salam price); the second is Riba (interest, increasing a debt)."}]},{"id":"t4.7","section":"4.7","title":"Money, Monetary Policy and Islamic Finance","pages":[90,90],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["money"],"intuition":"Why does the role of money differ between conventional and Islamic finance?","simpleExplanation":"Conventional finance treats money as a commodity that can be bought, sold and rented. Islamic finance treats money as a medium of exchange; the Prophet (pbuh) favoured money over barter, and the prohibition of Riba Al-Fadl supports the move to a money economy.","academicExplanation":["In the conventional system money is a commodity that can be sold, bought and rented regardless of its use by the borrower. In Islamic finance its status, role and functions differ, so the principles and operations differ. The advantages of money as a medium of exchange are conceded; the Prophet (pbuh) favoured money over barter, and prohibiting Riba Al-Fadl is a step towards a money economy, making barter rational and free from injustice."],"examExplanation":"Conventional: money = commodity to rent/sell. Islamic: money = medium of exchange; Riba Al-Fadl prohibition supports transition to money economy.","keyPoints":["Money is not a commodity for rent in Islamic finance.","The prohibition of Riba Al-Fadl encouraged the move from barter to a money economy."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why the rules of Riba al-Fadl encourage money","kind":"textbook","text":"Exchanging 2 kg of poor dates for 1 kg of good dates is barred. Instead, the Prophet (pbuh) directed selling one for money and buying the other, which moves exchange towards a money economy and away from unfair barter (p. 90; the Hadith is discussed on p. 437)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.7.1","t4.7.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"How does conventional finance treat money, according to the author?","options":["Only as a medium of exchange","As a commodity that can be sold, bought and rented","As gold","As Zakat"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 90."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch4-t12"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch4-t12","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"4.7","sectionTitle":"Money, Monetary Policy and Islamic Finance","title":"Money and Monetary Policy from the Islamic Perspective: Paper Money, Currency Trading, Money Creation, and Inflation","covers":["t4.7","t4.7.1","t4.7.2","t4.7.3","t4.7.4"],"overview":"The author covers the Shari'ah status of paper/fiat money (treated like gold/silver), the restrictions on trading currencies, the Islamic view on money/credit creation (requiring real-asset backing), and the rule that debts must be repaid in their exact original currency/amount regardless of inflation.","simpleExplanation":"Modern paper money, even though it has no value in itself (unlike gold/silver), is treated by Shari'ah exactly like gold and silver used to be — meaning all the rules about interest, Zakat, and exchange apply fully to it. Because of this, you can't sell a $10 bill for $11 (that would be Riba on pure money), and currency trading has its own strict hand-to-hand rules. On creating money: conventional banks can create money/credit essentially 'out of thin air' through ledger entries, but Islamic finance insists new money/credit growth should track real growth in goods and services in the economy — creating money without real backing generates injustice and distortion. Islamic economists have long debated exactly how strict this should be (some favor 100% reserve banking; others think some credit creation is fine as long as interest is removed from the system). Finally, on inflation: if you lend or are owed money in a certain currency, you must repay/be repaid in that exact currency and amount on the due date — no adjusting for inflation is allowed ('indexation'), even though this sounds unfair when a currency depreciates significantly. Scholars have specifically rejected indexation as a solution, partly because it doesn't actually solve inflation and partly because — as a leading Islamic finance judge put it — it would eliminate any profit margin for banks, since they'd have to pay depositors and charge borrowers the exact same inflation-based rate.","academicExplanation":"4.7.1 Status of Paper Money: modern currency notes are Thaman (a unit of account/price-serving function), just as gold/silver once were — wanted only for exchange/payment, not for intrinsic value; present fiat/fiduciary money represents monetary value for all payment purposes, and all countries' currencies are unlimited legal tender that creditors must accept for debt recovery. Money's productive use, when linked to labour/other resources, initiates real production processes benefiting society. Consequently, paper money is subject to ALL Shari'ah tenets on Riba, debts and Zakat: one cannot sell a $10 bill for $11 (it represents pure money, no intrinsic value); notes of the same currency can only be exchanged equal-for-equal; notes of different currencies (different monetary 'species') can be exchanged without an equality requirement but subject to Bai' al Sarf conditions (hand-to-hand). Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench held paper money has 'practically become almost like natural money' equal in exchange facility/credibility to old silver/gold coins, and is thus subject to the same Qur'an/Sunnah injunctions; the OIC Fiqh Council (3rd session, 1986) similarly resolved paper money is 'real money' possessing all characteristics of value, subject to the same Shari'ah rules as gold/silver regarding Riba, Zakat, Salam and other transactions. 4.7.2 Trading in Currencies: paper currencies cannot be bought/sold like intrinsic-value goods; Shari'ah treats money differently on two counts: (1) money of the same denomination is not the 'subject matter of trade' like other commodities — its use is restricted to its basic purpose (medium of exchange/measure of value); (2) if money must exceptionally be exchanged for money (or borrowed), both sides' payment must be equal, preventing money from being used for a purpose (trade in money itself) it's not meant for. Justice Taqi Usmani (SAB) notes money, unlike commodities of varying quality, has no quality except as a value-measure/exchange-medium — all units of the same denomination are 100% equal to each other. The author cites Professor John Gray's observation (in False Dawn) that daily FX market transactions reached ~$1.2 trillion (over 50x world trade volume), ~95% speculative (often via complex derivatives/futures/options) — creating a 'virtual financial economy' with terrible potential to disrupt the real economy (citing Barings Bank's 1995 collapse). This is echoed by a quote attributed to Imam Al-Ghazali (900 years ago): Riba is prohibited because it lets someone earn more money from money without engaging in real economic activity, thereby harming humanity's genuine interests, which depend on real trade skills, industry and construction. 4.7.3 Creation of Money from the Islamic Perspective: unlike bullion money, paper/fiduciary money can be created via mere ledger entries or securities issuance, without any corresponding real increase in goods/services — generating distortion and exploitation. In the Islamic financial system (which strictly prohibits exploitation), money/credit supply growth should match the growth in goods/services supply; minor mismatches are tolerable, but persistent mismatches generate payment-system distortions and injustice. A defining feature of Islamic finance: financial instruments must be real-asset-based — Islamic banks cannot create money out of nothing or without real-asset backing (unlike the conventional system); they can only securitize their asset-based operations for liquidity, transferring ownership (with risk/reward) to security holders. This means Islamic banks/institutions cannot finance government budget deficits until governments have sufficient real assets to raise Shari'ah-compliant funds, or until existing debt stock is converted into Shari'ah-compliant securities. Regulators should monitor three monetary-expansion sources: government deficit financing via central bank borrowing (the major source), secondary credit creation by commercial banks, and exogenous factors; central bank monetary policy should target money-supply growth that is neither 'inadequate' nor 'excessive', sufficient only to let the economy fully exploit its capacity to supply goods/services for broad-based welfare. Commercial bank deposits (primary deposits = base money; derivative deposits = credit-extension-created money in a fractional-reserve system) both contribute to money supply, so derivative-deposit expansion also requires regulation (e.g. via base-money availability and restricting banks from neutralizing cash reserves via reserve-sweep programmes). The author surveys the debate among Islamic economists on credit/bank money: early writers saw something morally wrong with credit money, doubting its necessity and attributing its growth to banks' vested interests (creating purchasing power 'out of thin air', benefiting from artificially-created demand from asset-liquidators seeking guaranteed income) — these economists favour a 100% reserve system, with any additional needed money issued directly by the central bank. Others favouring credit creation argue Islamic-system credit would be created only to the extent genuine wealth-creating productive opportunities exist, naturally self-limiting via profit-sharing demand and depositor/bank profit expectations — credit should not be blamed as 'interest's child', since a bank's ability to create credit is independent of the terms on which it's created. All Islamic economists agree interest itself is the real villain: measured, interest-free credit/money creation may not harm the financial/payment system; abolishing interest would substantially curtail credit creation's harmful features. They locate the real cause of trade cycles in interest's role within a credit system, not credit creation as such — under an interest-based system, entrepreneurs must target profit rates several multiples of the interest rate, forcing either higher prices or lower wages, both of which slash effective demand; the proposed remedy is reshaping credit so loans carry no interest and profits fall to levels that pay only for enterprise labour. Under a Shirkah-based interest-free system, overexpansion possibilities should be naturally limited, since loss liability attaches directly to the credit-creating banking system itself (an Islamic bank relates to clients as partner/investor/trader, not creditor/debtor) — Islam's stress on mutually-consented equitable profit/loss sharing between capital and enterprise underlies this. Bank credit creation still depends on public deposit-holding habits and cheque-based payments, letting banks meet cash demand via fractional reserves; overall credit volume fluctuates with changes in banks' cash reserves (driven by public cash demand or central bank policy). 4.7.4 Currency Rate Fluctuation and Settlement of Debts: even conventional finance doesn't normally use indexation for inflation losses, preferring floating-rate provisions (applied only prospectively, not to already-accrued liability) in agreements. Islamic banks are categorically NOT allowed to index any debt/receivable for inflation, though certain modes/products may stipulate a floating/variable rate for FUTURE periods only (e.g. Ijarah rental increases for remaining lease periods per pre-agreed terms) — this never affects already-accrued liability. Clear Qur'an/Sunnah injunctions require that a loan/debt be repaid exactly in the same kind and quantity, regardless of any change in currency value or lent-commodity price at repayment time — applying to loans, debts, credit, barter, deferred currency exchange, delayed remuneration after devaluation/revaluation, indemnity, and currency-unit changes at redemption. Exception: if the debt's original currency becomes extinct/unavailable, its counter value is paid at the DUE DATE's rate (illustrated: a debt of 10 Saudi Riyals due 31 December must be paid as SR 10 value on 31 December, regardless of the Riyal's cross-rate movements, if paid in another currency due to unavailability). Currency depreciation/inflation stems mainly from unlimited money/credit creation, burdening debtors generally and future generations particularly. Governments have tried various inflation-fighting measures including wage/financial-obligation indexation (widely used in 1980s Latin America), which failed to control inflation (which rose over 2000% annually in some countries), forcing a strategic pivot away from indexation. In Islamic finance, indexation is sometimes proposed to counter inflation, but the Nass (clear text) of Qur'an 2:279 allows recovery of only the principal, declaring any addition Riba — since clear textual guidance (Nass) exists, Ijtihad (which applies only in Nass's absence) cannot be invoked to justify debt-purchasing-power-indexation. Historically, bullion-money value was represented by its metal content; paper/debased money's value rests on official commitment rather than physical content — but during inflation, money's intrinsic functions (medium of exchange, unit of account) remain intact; only its relative future exchange value changes, a phenomenon that has always existed (even silver dirhams depreciated against gold dinars in the early Caliphate era) without any historical Islamic-economics literature invoking 'indexation' as a response. Justice Taqi Usmani (SAB) specifically refutes the 'interest compensates for inflation loss' argument and rejects debt-indexation as a workable interest substitute, on a distinctly PRACTICAL (not just textual) ground: indexation would give banks and depositors the SAME inflation-based rate on both sides of their balance sheet (what banks receive from borrowers = what they must pay depositors, both tracking inflation), leaving nothing for the bank itself — 'no bank can be run without a profit.' The OIC Fiqh Council (5th session, 1988) resolved that a fixed debt must be repaid in its own currency (not a counter value), since debts are settled in the same currency — fixed debts, whatever their source, cannot be attached to currency fluctuation.","examExplanation":"Paper/fiat money is treated exactly like gold/silver under Shari'ah (per Pakistan's SAB and the OIC Fiqh Council, 1986) — all Riba/Zakat/exchange rules apply fully; you cannot sell a $10 bill for $11. Currency trading: same-denomination notes exchange equal-for-equal; different-currency notes exchange without an equality requirement but still hand-to-hand (Bai' al Sarf). Money creation: Islamic finance requires money/credit growth to track real growth in goods/services (no creation 'out of nothing'/unbacked by real assets); Islamic institutions cannot finance government deficits absent sufficient real government assets or Shari'ah-compliant securitized debt. Debt settlement and inflation: debts must be repaid in their exact original currency/amount on the due date regardless of inflation (Qur'an 2:279's Nass overrides Ijtihad-based indexation proposals); if the original currency becomes unavailable, the counter-value is calculated at the DUE DATE's rate. Justice Taqi Usmani's key practical objection to indexation: it would give banks and depositors identical inflation-tracking rates, leaving no profit margin for the bank.","keyPoints":["Paper/fiat money is treated by Shari'ah exactly like gold and silver were (per Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench and the OIC Fiqh Council, 1986) — all Riba, Zakat, Salam and exchange rules apply fully to it, despite having no intrinsic value.","Currency trading rules: same-denomination notes must be exchanged equal-for-equal; different-currency notes may be exchanged without an equality requirement, but still hand-to-hand (Bai' al Sarf), never on a deferred basis.","A defining feature of Islamic finance: financial instruments must be real-asset-based — Islamic banks cannot create money 'out of nothing' or finance government budget deficits absent sufficient real government assets or Shari'ah-compliant securitized debt.","Islamic economists have historically debated money/credit creation: some favour a 100% reserve system (only the central bank issues new money); others argue Shirkah-based credit creation is naturally self-limiting and not inherently 'interest's child' — but all agree interest itself, not credit creation as such, is the real villain behind harmful trade cycles.","Debts must be repaid in their exact original currency and amount on the due date, regardless of inflation/currency depreciation — indexation of debts to inflation/purchasing power is rejected, since the clear Qur'anic text (Nass, 2:279) allows recovery of only the principal, and Ijtihad cannot override clear Nass.","If a debt's original currency becomes extinct/unavailable, its counter-value must be calculated at the exchange rate prevailing on the DUE DATE, not the original agreement date.","Justice Taqi Usmani's decisive practical objection to debt indexation: it would give a bank's borrowers and depositors the identical inflation-based rate on both sides of its balance sheet, leaving no margin for the bank's own profit — 'no bank can be run without a profit.'"],"definitions":[{"term":"Fiduciary/fiat money","definition":"Modern paper currency, which has no intrinsic value but serves as a unit of account (Thaman) for exchange and payment purposes; treated by Shari'ah exactly as gold and silver were, subject to the same Riba, Zakat and exchange rules."}],"conditions":["Currency notes of the same denomination must be exchanged equal-for-equal; notes of different currencies may be exchanged without an equality requirement but must still be settled hand-to-hand.","A loan or debt must be repaid in exactly the same kind and quantity/currency as originally lent/owed, irrespective of any change in the currency's value or the lent commodity's price at repayment time.","If the original currency of a debt becomes extinct or unavailable, its counter value must be calculated using the exchange rate prevailing on the debt's due date."],"principles":["Money/credit supply growth in an Islamic financial system should track real growth in the supply of goods and services; persistent mismatches generate payment-system distortions and injustice.","Debt indexation to inflation/purchasing power is rejected: the clear Qur'anic text (Nass, 2:279) permits recovery of only the principal, and Ijtihad cannot be invoked where clear Nass already governs."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Justice Taqi Usmani's rebuttal of debt indexation","kind":"textbook","text":"Usmani argues that even setting aside whether indexation is Shari'ah-compliant in principle, it is impractical for banking: indexation would give the bank's borrowers and depositors the identical inflation-based rate, since both track the same measure. This leaves nothing for the bank itself, and 'no bank can be run without a profit.'"},{"title":"Settlement of a Saudi Riyal debt on its due date","kind":"textbook","text":"A credit sale executed 1 July generates a debt of 10 Saudi Riyals (SR) payable 31 December. On the due date, the purchaser must pay SR 10 regardless of the Riyal's value against other currencies. If forced to pay in another currency (e.g. because SR became unavailable), the exchange rate used must be the one prevailing on 31 December — the due date, not the original contract date."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Islamic finance permits some form of inflation-protection for lenders/creditors given how strongly it protects distributive justice; the chapter is explicit that indexation is rejected outright, based on the clear Qur'anic text (Nass) permitting recovery of only the principal — this holds even though the author acknowledges inflation's genuine harm to creditors."],"distinctions":["Recovering the exact original currency/amount at the due date's exchange rate (permitted, when the original currency is unavailable) vs indexing the debt itself to inflation/purchasing power (prohibited, since the Nass allows only the principal)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Zakat","Bai' al Sarf","Shariat Appellate Bench"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7","pages":[90,91,92,93,94,95,96]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Money in conventional finance","b":"Money in Islamic finance","text":"Conventionally money is a commodity that can be sold and rented for a return; in Islamic finance it is a medium of exchange and measure of value only (p. 90)."}]},{"id":"t4.7.1","section":"4.7.1","title":"Status of Paper Money","pages":[91,91],"tier":"core","concepts":["money","bai-sarf"],"intuition":"Do Riba rules for gold and silver apply to paper notes? Yes.","simpleExplanation":"Paper money is Thaman — like gold and silver in the past — with no intrinsic value. It is subject to all Shari’ah rules on Riba, debts and Zakat. A $10 bill cannot be sold for $11. Different countries’ currencies are different species: exchange at any rate, but hand to hand.","academicExplanation":["Present fiat money is Thaman (a unit of account serving as price), wanted only for exchange and payments, with unlimited legal tender status. Paper money is subject to all Shari’ah tenets on Riba, debts and Zakat. Notes of the same currency exchange equal for equal; currencies of different countries are different species — no equality condition but Bai‘ al Sarf (hand to hand) applies. The Shariat Appellate Bench and the OIC Fiqh Council (3rd session, 1986) held paper money to be real money subject to the rules governing gold and silver for Riba, Zakat, Salam and all transactions."],"examExplanation":"Paper money = Thaman, real money (OIC Fiqh Council 1986; SAB). Subject to Riba, Zakat, Sarf rules. Same currency: equal + spot; different currencies: spot.","keyPoints":["$10 bill cannot be sold for $11.","OIC Fiqh Council 1986 resolution."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"A 10-dollar bill for 11","kind":"textbook","text":"A 10-dollar note cannot be sold for 11 because it represents pure money with no intrinsic value; notes of different countries are different species and may be exchanged unequally, but hand to hand (p. 91)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Paper money has no intrinsic value, so Riba and Zakat rules for gold and silver do not apply.","right":"The SAB and the OIC Fiqh Council (1986) hold paper money is real money subject to all rules on Riba, Zakat and Salam (p. 91)."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.5","t4.7.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the OIC Fiqh Council (1986), paper money is:","options":["A commodity with intrinsic value","Real money subject to the rules governing gold and silver","Exempt from Zakat","Not subject to Riba rules"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 91."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t4.7.2","section":"4.7.2","title":"Trading in Currencies","pages":[91,92],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["money","forex"],"intuition":"Why can’t money be traded like goods?","simpleExplanation":"Money has no quality except being a measure of value and medium of exchange; all units of the same denomination are identical. So money of the same kind is not a subject of trade, and money-for-money exchanges must be equal. Most FX trading is speculative and destabilising; Al-Ghazali warned that interest deters real economic activity.","academicExplanation":["Shari’ah treats money differently from commodities: money of the same denomination is not the subject matter of trade, being restricted to its role as medium of exchange and measure of value; if exchanged or borrowed, payment on both sides must be equal. Taqi Usmani (SAB): commodities have different qualities and are identified, while all money units of the same denomination are equal. John Gray (False Dawn): FX transactions of about $1.2 trillion a day, over fifty times world trade, about 95% speculative, can disrupt the real economy (Barings, 1995). Imam Al-Ghazali: interest is prohibited because it prevents people from real economic activities."],"examExplanation":"Money not subject of trade (same denomination); exchange/borrowing must be equal. FX speculation destabilises; Al-Ghazali: interest deters real activity.","keyPoints":["All units of money of the same denomination are equal.","Al-Ghazali: interest keeps people from real economic activity — trade, industry and construction."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"The size of FX speculation","kind":"textbook","text":"John Gray noted foreign-exchange turnover of about $1.2 trillion a day — over fifty times world trade — with around 95% speculative; he cites the 1995 collapse of Barings (p. 92)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.7.1","t8.8.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which classical scholar is quoted as saying interest prevents people from undertaking real economic activities?","options":["Ibn Khaldun","Imam Al-Ghazali","Ibn Taymiyah","Shah Waliullah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 92."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t4.7.3","section":"4.7.3","title":"Creation of Money from the Islamic Perspective","pages":[92,94],"tier":"detailed","concepts":["money","monetary-policy"],"intuition":"Can Islamic banks create money out of nothing? No — their instruments must be real-asset based.","simpleExplanation":"Money and credit growth should match the supply of goods and services. Islamic banks cannot create money without real assets; they can securitise asset-based operations. Regulators should monitor deficit financing by the central bank, commercial banks’ credit creation and external factors. Economists differ: some want 100% reserves, others accept credit creation without interest.","academicExplanation":["Fiat money can be created by ledger entries without corresponding goods, causing distortions and exploitation. In Islamic finance, money/credit growth should match goods and services; persistent mismatches are inconsistent with its principles. Islamic instruments must be real-asset based; banks cannot create money out of nothing but can securitise asset-based operations, transferring ownership, risk and reward to security holders. Deficit financing by Islamic banks is not possible until governments have sufficient real assets or convert debt to compliant securities.","Regulators should monitor three sources of monetary expansion: central bank financing of budget deficits (the major source), secondary credit creation by commercial banks (derivative deposits in a fractional reserve system — regulate base money and prevent reserve-sweep programmes), and exogenous factors (e.g. mop up liquidity from capital inflows and invest in commodity-producing avenues). Monetary growth should be neither inadequate nor excessive.","The literature assumes a two-tier Mudarabah/Shirkah model, though current practice uses fixed-income modes. Early writers saw credit money as morally wrong and favoured 100% reserves; others argue credit will be created only to the extent of genuine profitable opportunities. All agree interest is the villain; under an interest-based system entrepreneurs must target profits well above interest, raising prices or cutting wages. Under Shirkah, overexpansion is limited as losses attach to the credit-creating banks; the bank–client relationship is partner/investor/trader, not creditor/debtor."],"examExplanation":"Money/credit growth should match real goods/services; Islamic instruments real-asset based; banks can securitise but not create money from nothing. Monitor: central bank deficit financing, derivative deposits, exogenous flows. Debate: 100% reserve vs interest-free credit creation.","keyPoints":["Three sources of monetary expansion to monitor.","Monetary growth neither inadequate nor excessive.","Debate: 100% reserve system vs permitted credit creation.","Interest, not credit creation per se, drives trade cycles (in this view).","Three sources of monetary expansion to monitor: central-bank financing of deficits (the major one), secondary credit creation by banks, and exogenous factors.","Money supply should be neither “inadequate” nor “excessive”."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Three sources of new money","kind":"practice","text":"In a year, the central bank lends 50 bn to the government, commercial banks create 30 bn of derivative deposits, and capital inflows add 20 bn. The book asks regulators to watch all three, the first being the major one, and to keep total money growth in line with goods and services (pp. 92–94)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t2.5.5","t15.3","t18.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What does the author identify as the major source of monetary expansion?","options":["Exports","Financing government budget deficits by borrowing from the central bank","Zakat","Islamic bank deposits"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 92."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"100% reserve advocates","b":"Credit-creation supporters","text":"Early writers saw credit money as morally wrong and wanted full reserves; others argue credit is harmful only through interest, and under PLS banks would create credit only as far as productive profit opportunities exist (pp. 93–94)."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Should Islamic banks create credit?","criticism":"Early Islamic economists: credit money is morally wrong, creates artificial purchasing power; adopt 100% reserves and let the central bank issue extra money.","response":"Others: in an interest-free system, credit will be created only to the extent genuine profitable opportunities exist; credit is not ‘the child of interest’.","alternative":"Both sides agree interest is the villain; measured credit without interest may not harm the payment system.","takeaway":"The key issue is interest in the credit system, not credit creation as such."}]},{"id":"t4.7.4","section":"4.7.4","title":"Currency Rate Fluctuation and Settlement of Debts","pages":[94,96],"tier":"core","concepts":["indexation","dayn"],"intuition":"If inflation erodes the value of a receivable, can an Islamic bank index it? No — debts are repaid in the same currency and amount.","simpleExplanation":"Debts must be repaid in the same kind and quantity regardless of changes in currency value. Islamic banks cannot index receivables, though some modes (like Ijarah) may use a floating rate for future periods if agreed upfront. If the debt currency becomes unavailable, its counter value is paid at the due-date rate. The OIC Fiqh Council (1988) forbids linking fixed debts to currency fluctuation.","academicExplanation":["Even conventional finance rarely uses indexation; it uses floating rates for future periods without affecting accrued liability. Islamic banks may not link any debt/receivable for indexation; in certain modes they may stipulate a floating rate for remaining periods (e.g. Ijarah rentals, if provided for in the agreement), but accrued rentals cannot be indexed.","Loans and debts are repaid in the same kind and quantity irrespective of value changes — applicable to credit, barter, deferred currency exchange, delayed remuneration after devaluation, indemnity and currency changes. If the debt currency is extinct or unavailable, its counter value is paid at the rate of the due date (e.g. SR 10 due 31 December paid in Rupees at the 31 December rate). Latin American indexation in the 1980s failed to control inflation. The Nass (2:279) permits only the principal, so Ijtihad cannot justify indexation. Taqi Usmani: indexation is impractical for banks since they would receive and pay the same inflation rate, leaving nothing for the bank. OIC Fiqh Council (5th session, 1988): a fixed debt is repaid in its own currency; it is not permitted to attach fixed debts to currency fluctuation."],"examExplanation":"No indexation of debts (Nass 2:279; OIC Fiqh Council 1988). Floating rate allowed only for future periods if agreed (e.g. Ijarah). Unavailable currency → counter value at due-date rate. Indexation also impractical for banks (Usmani).","keyPoints":["Accrued debts/rentals cannot be indexed.","Floating rentals for future periods may be agreed in Ijarah.","Due-date exchange rate applies if the debt currency is unavailable.","Ijtihad does not operate where a Nass exists."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Saudi Riyal debt","kind":"textbook","text":"A credit sale on 1 July creates a debt of SR 10 payable on 31 December. The buyer owes SR 10 whatever the Riyal’s value; if payment must be in Rupees, the 31 December exchange rate applies."},{"title":"Debt in Saudi Riyals","kind":"textbook","text":"A credit sale on 1 July creates a debt of SR 10 due 31 December. The buyer owes SR 10 whatever its value; if he must pay in Rupees, the rate of 31 December applies (p. 95)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Indexation of debts is a practical substitute for interest.","right":"Latin American indexation failed to control inflation; Justice Usmani notes a bank indexing both deposits and financing would earn nothing, and 2:279 allows only the principal (pp. 95–96)."}],"relatedTopics":["t7.17","t3.2.1.c","t11.3.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A debt of SR 10 falls due on 31 December but must be paid in Rupees. Which exchange rate applies?","options":["The rate on the contract date","The rate on the due date (31 December)","An inflation-adjusted rate","Any rate the creditor chooses"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 95."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"calc":{"type":"fx-settlement"}}],"questions":[{"id":"q4.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following would count as ‘investment’ in the Islamic sense?","options":["Purchasing a corporate bond","A conventional fixed deposit","Buying goods and selling them on at a profit","Buying a promissory note at a discount"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Investment must involve real activity (p. 74).","topicId":"t4.2","chapter":4,"section":"4.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish loan from investment","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2","pages":[73,74]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.2","type":"match","prompt":"Match each contract to its liquidity in Table 4.1.","pairs":[["Murabaha/credit sale","Non-liquid"],["Ijarah","Liquid"],["Musharakah","Liquid"],["Istisna‘a","Non-liquid"]],"explanation":"Debt-mode receivables cannot be sold; Ijarah and sharing-mode assets can.","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Classify modes by liquidity","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,78]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.3","type":"tf","prompt":"Using an interest-based benchmark (such as LIBOR) to price a Murabaha makes the transaction un-Islamic even if all trade rules are followed.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Scholars say it does not, so long as the rules of trade and Ijarah are applied (p. 81).","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain benchmarks","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,81]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.4","type":"scenario","prompt":"A leased machine is destroyed by fire through no fault of the lessee. According to Chapter 4:","options":["The lessee must pay rent and replace the machine","The bank bears the loss and its right to rent ceases","The lessee pays rent but not replacement","The loss is shared 50:50"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Ownership risk stays with the lessor; rent ceases if the asset cannot deliver its benefit without lessee fault (pp. 83, 85).","topicId":"t4.2.7","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply ownership risk in Ijarah","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[82,83]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.5","type":"identify","prompt":"“One has to bear loss, if any, if he wants to get any profit over his investment.” This maxim is:","options":["Al Ujrah wal Dhaman La Tajtami‘an","Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman","Ibahatul Asliyah","Shart-e-Jazai"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 81.","topicId":"t4.2.6","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify key maxims","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[81,82]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.6","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are additional (or greater) risks Islamic banks face according to Section 4.2.9? (Select all.)","options":["Shari’ah non-compliance risk","Asset risk","Withdrawal risk","No legal risk at all","Fiduciary risk"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4],"explanation":"They face greater legal risk, not none.","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"List Islamic banks’ risks","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[84,85]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.7","type":"application","prompt":"An importer wants to lock in a USD/PKR rate for payment in 3 months. Which structure is consistent with Section 4.5?","options":["A forward contract with PKR paid now, USD in 3 months","A promise to exchange at the agreed rate in 3 months, both currencies then exchanged simultaneously","A Salam contract selling USD","An option contract"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Forward cover as a promise for real needs; actual exchange simultaneous (p. 89).","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.5","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply currency exchange rules","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87,89]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.8","type":"tf","prompt":"Islamic finance recognises the time value of money only through the pricing of goods and usufructs, not in loans or debts.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 90.","topicId":"t4.6","chapter":4,"section":"4.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain time value of money","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[89,90]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.9","type":"mcq","prompt":"What did the OIC Fiqh Council (1988) resolve on linking debts to currency fluctuation?","options":["It is recommended","It is not permitted; fixed debts are repaid in their own currency","Allowed for Islamic banks only","Allowed if inflation exceeds 10%"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 96.","topicId":"t4.7.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall rulings on indexation","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","pages":[94,96]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.10","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does the bank’s net return in Ijarah differ from that in a credit sale?","options":["Ijarah return is quasi-fixed because the bank bears asset risk and ownership expenses","Both are fully fixed and risk-free","Ijarah has unknown return like Musharakah","Credit sale return is variable"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 77.","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare risk–return profiles","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,78]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.11","type":"scenario","prompt":"A widow asks an Islamic bank to invest her savings with low risk. Which view does the author support?","options":["Only Musharakah is allowed","The bank as trustee may invest her funds in trade- and Ijarah-based activities","She must accept Mudarabah risk","Islamic banks cannot help risk-averse savers"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 4.3.","topicId":"t4.3","chapter":4,"section":"4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply mode preference","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.3","pages":[85,86]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.12","type":"short","prompt":"Explain why Islamic banks cannot provide return-free loans from depositors’ funds at their discretion.","keywords":["trust","fiduciary","depositors","equity","charity"],"correctAnswer":"Banks hold depositors’ money as a trust; business and benevolence are separate. Doling out trust funds would breach fiduciary duty to (mainly middle-class) depositors. Free loans may be given only from the bank’s own equity or charity fund with Shari’ah approval without affecting depositors.","explanation":"Section 4.4.","topicId":"t4.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain business vs benevolence","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.4","pages":[86,86]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.13","type":"order","prompt":"Put the steps of a bank Murabaha (as described in Section 4.2.7) in order.","items":["Bank purchases goods directly or through an agent","Bank takes ownership and bears the related risk","Bank sells the goods to the client at cost plus profit","Risk passes to the client, who pays at the settled time"],"explanation":"p. 83.","topicId":"t4.2.7","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Sequence trade-based financing","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[82,83]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.14","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does the author distinguish “interest-free banking” from “Islamic banking”?","options":["They are identical","Interest-free banking only avoids interest; Islamic banking also avoids Gharar and unethical practices and serves the goals of an Islamic economy","Islamic banking allows some interest","Interest-free banking is broader"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 73.","topicId":"t4.1","chapter":4,"section":"4.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish the two terms","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.1","pages":[73,73]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.15","type":"tf","prompt":"Buying a government bond counts as “investment” in the Islamic sense.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"It is a merely financial transaction with no real activity (p. 74).","topicId":"t4.2","chapter":4,"section":"4.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Define investment in the Islamic context","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2","pages":[73,74]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.16","type":"multi","prompt":"Which activities does the prohibition of Gharar rule out for Islamic banks? (Select all.)","options":["Speculative share trading","Short-selling","Discounting of bills","Spot purchase of identified goods","Trading in unidentified items"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4],"explanation":"p. 75.","topicId":"t4.2.2","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply Gharar to banking activities","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.2","pages":[75,75]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.17","type":"mcq","prompt":"In Table 4.1, which modes are classified as “liquid”?","options":["Credit sales and Salam","Ijarah and the sharing modes","Istisna and Qard al Hasan","Only Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Ijarah and sharing-mode assets can be sold in a secondary market; Murabaha, Salam and Istisna receivables cannot (pp. 77–78).","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall liquidity of modes","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,78]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.18","type":"scenario","prompt":"In a Musharakah the business loses money through market conditions, with no negligence by the client. Can the bank enforce the client’s security to recover its capital?","options":["Yes, always","No — security in PLS covers only misconduct or negligence","Yes, but only half","Only if the loss exceeds 10%"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 78.","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply collateral rules in PLS","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,78]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.19","type":"identify","prompt":"Which Caliph refused to waive a loan from the public treasury that a woman lost in trade?","options":["Abu Bakr","Umar","Uthman","Ali"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Hind bint Utbah’s loan of 4,000 (pp. 76–77).","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the Hind bint Utbah precedent","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,78]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.20","type":"order","prompt":"Order the categories in Table 4.1 as the author presents them.","items":["Debt-creating modes (Qard, credit sales, Salam, Istisna)","Semi-debt modes (Ijarah)","Sharing modes (Musharakah, Mudarabah)"],"explanation":"p. 77.","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the classification of modes","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,78]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.21","type":"match","prompt":"Match each Fiqh benchmark to its meaning.","pairs":[["Ujratul-mithl","Matching wage"],["Ribh-al-mithl","Matching rate of profit"],["Qirad mithl","Matching rate in Mudarabah"],["Musaqat mithl","Matching rate in crop sharing"]],"explanation":"p. 80.","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall classical benchmarks","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,81]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.22","type":"application","prompt":"A lessor argues: “Rent includes time value, so a fixed time value on a loan should also be allowed by analogy.” Why does the author reject this?","options":["Rent is also prohibited","Rent depends on the asset’s uncertain capacity to yield usufruct and the lessor bears ownership risk; a loan bears no such risk","Loans are always long-term","Analogy is never allowed"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 79.","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate the rent–interest analogy","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,81]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.23","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the author, Islamic finance needs only one benchmark, like conventional finance.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Two: a price (mark-up/rent) scale for debt/semi-debt contracts and a sharing-ratio scale for equity contracts (p. 81).","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the dual benchmark idea","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,81]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.24","type":"scenario","prompt":"After a credit sale, the goods are destroyed in the buyer’s warehouse. The buyer had Takaful cover. What is his obligation to the bank?","options":["He is released","He must still pay the price at the settled time; Takaful only mitigates his own risk","The bank shares the loss","The debt is halved"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 82.","topicId":"t4.2.6","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply risk transfer in sale","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[81,82]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.25","type":"comparison","prompt":"Why is Shirkah riskier for banks than Murabaha or Ijarah?","options":["It has higher fees","All business loss falls on capital and clients could disguise profits, so even the principal may be lost","It is not permitted","It lasts longer"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 82.","topicId":"t4.2.6","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare risk across modes","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[81,82]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.26","type":"identify","prompt":"Under which arrangement does a bank manage clients’ funds for a fixed fee while all profit and loss belong to the clients?","options":["Mudarabah","Wakalatul Istismar","Salam","Kafalah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 83.","topicId":"t4.2.7","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify fee-based fund management","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[82,83]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.27","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the author think Islamic banks are less likely to be used for money laundering?","options":["They are smaller","As partners in trade they must know the nature of the client’s business and avoid reputational risk","They do not accept cash","They are exempt from regulation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 84.","topicId":"t4.2.8","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain due-diligence effects","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.8","pages":[83,84]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.28","type":"application","prompt":"An Istisna manufacturer is late. Which clause may the bank use, which is not available in Murabaha?","options":["A penalty paid to charity","Shart-e-Jazai — a reduction in price for delayed delivery","An interest charge","Automatic cancellation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Istisna depends on the maker’s effort; in Murabaha and Salam the deferred liability is already fixed (p. 85).","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Shart-e-Jazai","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[84,85]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.29","type":"tf","prompt":"The author argues that debt-creating modes such as Murabaha and Ijarah should be abandoned in favour of equity only.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"The issue is preference for equity, not abandoning debt; debt must simply carry no interest (p. 86).","topicId":"t4.3","chapter":4,"section":"4.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand the debt versus equity view","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.3","pages":[85,86]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.30","type":"scenario","prompt":"A customer wants the Islamic bank to give him a return-free loan from depositors’ money. What should the bank say?","options":["Yes, Islamic banks are charities","Depositors’ money is a trust; return-free loans may come only from the bank’s own equity or charity fund with Shari’ah approval","Only if he pays a fee","Only for large amounts"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 86.","topicId":"t4.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply business versus benevolence","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.4","pages":[86,86]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.31","type":"mcq","prompt":"What did the OIC Fiqh Council (1998) resolve on currency sales?","options":["Deferred sale of currencies is allowed","It is not permissible to sell currencies by deferred sale or to fix a future date for exchanging them","Only USD may be deferred","Currency Salam is allowed"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 87.","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the ruling on currency sales","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87,89]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.32","type":"tf","prompt":"Forward sale of currencies through Salam is a valid way to hedge FX risk.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Paper money is price, not a Salam commodity; currency exchange needs simultaneous delivery (p. 89).","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate Salam in currencies","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87,89]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.33","type":"short","prompt":"How can a trader obtain forward cover for a real import payment in Islamic finance?","keywords":["promise","agreed rate","simultaneous","real need"],"correctAnswer":"Through a promise with the bank to buy or sell a certain amount of currency at an agreed rate on a future date, for genuine trade needs and not speculation; the actual exchange of both currencies takes place simultaneously on that date.","explanation":"p. 89.","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain permissible forward cover","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87,89]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.34","type":"identify","prompt":"Which contract illustrates time value through pricing, with the future-delivery price lower than the spot price?","options":["Salam","Hibah","Qard","Wadiah"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 90.","topicId":"t4.6","chapter":4,"section":"4.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify time value in Salam","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[89,90]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.35","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which body resolved in 1986 that paper money is real money subject to the rules of gold and silver?","options":["AAOIFI","OIC Islamic Fiqh Council","IFSB","State Bank of Pakistan"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Third session, October 1986 (p. 91).","topicId":"t4.7.1","chapter":4,"section":"4.7.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the ruling on paper money","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.1","pages":[91,91]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.36","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are the three sources of monetary expansion regulators must monitor? (Select all.)","options":["Central-bank financing of government deficits","Secondary credit creation by commercial banks","Exogenous factors such as capital inflows","Zakat collection"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"pp. 92–93.","topicId":"t4.7.3","chapter":4,"section":"4.7.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall sources of monetary expansion","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.3","pages":[92,94]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q4.37","type":"application","prompt":"An Ijarah agreement provides for rental revision. Inflation rises sharply. What may the bank do?","options":["Index rentals already accrued","Apply a higher rental to the remaining period as provided in the agreement, but not to rentals already due","Increase the principal","Nothing at all"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 94.","topicId":"t4.7.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply floating rates without indexation","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","pages":[94,96]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch4-1","type":"scenario","prompt":"An individual purchases a government bond, expecting to receive fixed periodic interest payments and principal at maturity. Is this 'investment' under the Islamic framework described in Chapter 4?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states that purchasing a bond issued by a government or corporation is NOT considered investment, because it is merely a financial transaction with no real activity involved — investment requires becoming part of real activity or being itself real activity.","topicId":"t4.1","chapter":4,"section":"4.1","topicLabel":"The Philosophy of Islamic Finance: Asset-Backing and the Definition of 'Investment'","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain why 'interest-free banking' is a narrower concept than 'Islamic banking', and state what makes a transaction a genuine 'investment' in the Islamic framework","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.1","pages":[73,74]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, since money is being used productively","No — a bond purchase is merely a financial/monetary transaction (a loan) involving no real activity, so it does not qualify as investment","Yes, but only if the bond is government-issued","It depends only on the interest rate offered"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-2","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: 'Interest-free banking' and 'Islamic banking' are identical concepts in Chapter 4.","explanation":"False. 'Interest-free banking' is described as a narrower concept (avoiding only interest); 'Islamic banking' is broader, also avoiding Gharar and other unethical practices while actively supporting Islamic economic goals.","topicId":"t4.1","chapter":4,"section":"4.1","topicLabel":"The Philosophy of Islamic Finance: Asset-Backing and the Definition of 'Investment'","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why 'interest-free banking' is a narrower concept than 'Islamic banking', and state what makes a transaction a genuine 'investment' in the Islamic framework","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.1","pages":[73]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch4-3","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is explicitly prohibited for Islamic banks under the interest-avoidance rule?","explanation":"Islamic banks are explicitly not allowed to sell their debt instruments (at a premium or discount) — this would constitute trading in debt for an increase, which is Riba. Pricing a credit sale (embedding the delay factor before the debt is fixed), buying/selling goods for profit, and Ijarah rental are all permissible.","topicId":"t4.2.1","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.1","topicLabel":"How Islamic Banks Operationally Avoid Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how Islamic banks operationally avoid Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar in their products","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.1","pages":[74,75]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Pricing goods for credit sale with the payment delay factored into the price","Selling their own debt instruments at a discount","Buying and selling goods for profit","Charging rental on leased vehicles"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-4","type":"multi","prompt":"Which practices does Chapter 4 identify as prohibited for Islamic banks under the Gharar-avoidance principle?","explanation":"The prohibition of Gharar requires Islamic banks to avoid speculative share trading, short-selling, discounting of bills/securities, and trading in unidentified items. Genuine trade in physically identified, deliverable goods is the normal, permissible core of Islamic banking business.","topicId":"t4.2.2","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.2","topicLabel":"How Islamic Banks Operationally Avoid Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain how Islamic banks operationally avoid Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar in their products","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.2","pages":[75,76]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Speculative trading in shares","Short-selling","Discounting of bills and securities","Genuine trade in physically identified, deliverable goods","Trading in unidentified items"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4]},{"id":"q-ch4-5","type":"match","prompt":"Match each mode to its Table 4.1 classification: Murabaha, Ijarah, Musharakah, Qard al-Hasan.","explanation":"Per Table 4.1: Murabaha is a debt-creating mode (non-liquid, collateral, known return); Ijarah is a semi-debt mode (liquid, collateral, known return); Musharakah is a sharing mode (liquid, penalty for misconduct, unknown return); Qard al-Hasan is a debt-creating mode (collateral, nil return, since it's a benevolent loan).","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","topicLabel":"Alternative Financing Principles: The Major Islamic Finance Modes and Their Risk/Liquidity Profile","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"List the major Islamic financing modes (Mudarabah, Musharakah, Murabaha-Mu'ajjal, Salam, Ijarah, Istisna'a) and classify them by liquidity, guarantee and rate-of-return predictability","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[77]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"pairs":[["Murabaha","Debt-creating (non-liquid, collateral, known return)"],["Ijarah","Semi-debt (liquid, collateral, known return)"],["Musharakah","Sharing mode (liquid, penalty for misconduct, unknown return)"],["Qard al-Hasan","Debt-creating (collateral, nil return)"]]},{"id":"q-ch4-6","type":"short","prompt":"Why are Murabaha receivables considered 'non-liquid' while Ijarah assets are considered 'liquid', per Chapter 4?","explanation":"Liquidity refers to how easily the bank can sell related assets to obtain cash when needed; debt-based receivables (Murabaha, Salam, Istisna'a) cannot be sold like conventional debt, while ownership-based instruments (Ijarah, Shirkah) can.","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","topicLabel":"Alternative Financing Principles: The Major Islamic Finance Modes and Their Risk/Liquidity Profile","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"List the major Islamic financing modes (Mudarabah, Musharakah, Murabaha-Mu'ajjal, Salam, Ijarah, Istisna'a) and classify them by liquidity, guarantee and rate-of-return predictability","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[77,78]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Murabaha receivables represent debt, which cannot be sold like conventional debt instruments (at a premium or discount) — the same is true of Salam and Istisna'a. Ijarah assets (and sharing-mode instruments) represent real ownership interests, which CAN be sold in a secondary market, making them liquid."},{"id":"q-ch4-7","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank uses LIBOR (a conventional interest-rate benchmark) purely as a pricing reference number to determine the profit margin on a Murabaha transaction, while otherwise following all Shari'ah rules for a valid trade contract. Does this make the transaction un-Islamic?","explanation":"The chapter states that using an interest-based benchmark for pricing goods/usufructs in Islamic banks' trade and Ijarah activities does not make their operations un-Islamic, so long as other rules of trade and Ijarah are properly applied — the benchmark is a reference number, not the transaction's legal basis.","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","topicLabel":"Valid Gains on Investment: Fixed and Variable Returns, and Benchmarks","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain how Islamic banks operationally avoid Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar in their products","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[80,81]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, any reference to an interest rate automatically makes the transaction Riba","No — Shari'ah scholars hold that using an interest-based benchmark merely as a pricing reference does not itself make the operation un-Islamic, provided the actual rules of trade are followed","Yes, but only if the bank profits more than the benchmark rate","No, but only for Ijarah transactions, never for Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-8","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Shari'ah compliance requires that all Islamic financing returns be variable (profit-and-loss based), never fixed.","explanation":"False. Chapter 4's 'All Pre-fixed Returns are not Riba' subsection explicitly clarifies that a fixed profit margin from genuine trade (Bai') or fixed rent from Ijarah is Shari'ah-compliant — the test is the nature of the transaction, not whether the return is fixed.","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","topicLabel":"Valid Gains on Investment: Fixed and Variable Returns, and Benchmarks","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how Islamic banks operationally avoid Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar in their products","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,79]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch4-9","type":"short","prompt":"State the Shari'ah maxim that serves as the criterion for legality of any return on capital.","explanation":"This maxim is the universal test applied throughout the chapter to determine whether a given return on capital is legitimate.","topicId":"t4.2.6","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","topicLabel":"Entitlement to Profit With Risk and Responsibility: Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the Shari'ah maxim 'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' / 'Al Ghunm bil Ghurm' and explain its role as the criterion for legality of return on capital","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[81]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman (or Al Ghunm bil Ghurm) — meaning one has to bear loss, if any, if he wants to get any profit over his investment; entitlement to profit requires bearing the corresponding liability for loss."},{"id":"q-ch4-10","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank sells goods to a client via Murabaha; the sale is executed and the debt created. Before the client pays, the goods (now owned by the client) are destroyed by an accident unrelated to the bank. Must the client still pay the full price?","explanation":"Per Chapter 4, in trade the seller bears risk only while the asset remains with him; once sold, risk transfers to the buyer, who must pay the settled price even if the asset is later destroyed (he may mitigate this personally via Takaful, but that doesn't affect his payment obligation).","topicId":"t4.2.6","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","topicLabel":"Entitlement to Profit With Risk and Responsibility: Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"State the Shari'ah maxim 'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' / 'Al Ghunm bil Ghurm' and explain its role as the criterion for legality of return on capital","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[82]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No, the bank must absorb the loss since it originally owned the goods","Yes — once the sale is executed, risk transfers to the buyer, who must pay the settled price at the agreed time regardless of what happens to the asset afterward","No, the debt is automatically cancelled","Only half the price is owed"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-11","type":"mcq","prompt":"What risks should investment depositors in Islamic banks bear, per the consensus described in Chapter 4?","explanation":"There is consensus that depositors should not be burdened due to negligence/follies of bank management — they should bear only genuine market risk, not losses from fraud, carelessness, mismanagement or loan concentration.","topicId":"t4.2.6","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","topicLabel":"Entitlement to Profit With Risk and Responsibility: Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the Shari'ah maxim 'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' / 'Al Ghunm bil Ghurm' and explain its role as the criterion for legality of return on capital","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[82]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["All risks including bank fraud and mismanagement","Only genuine market risk — not losses caused by bank fraud, carelessness, mismanagement or loan concentration","No risk at all; their principal is always guaranteed","Only risks related to government policy changes"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-12","type":"comparison","prompt":"Contrast the famous descriptions of conventional and Islamic banks regarding what they 'deal in'.","explanation":"Conventional banks finance goods purchases via documents/paperwork but take no ownership/risk in the underlying goods. Islamic banks must actually purchase, own and bear risk in real goods/assets before earning profit from their sale or lease.","topicId":"t4.2.7","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","topicLabel":"Islamic Banks Deal in Goods and Documents, Not Money","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why Islamic banks are said to 'deal in goods, not money' and what this implies for their risk profile versus conventional banks","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[82]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Conventional banks 'deal in documents, not goods' (no asset ownership/risk); Islamic banks 'deal in goods and documents, not money' (must take genuine ownership and bear real asset risk)","Both deal exclusively in money","Conventional banks deal in goods; Islamic banks deal only in documents","There is no meaningful difference between the two"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch4-13","type":"short","prompt":"What is a Parallel Salam, and what condition must be met for it to be valid?","explanation":"This mechanism lets a bank manage price/asset risk from Salam purchases while keeping the two contracts legally independent.","topicId":"t4.2.7","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","topicLabel":"Islamic Banks Deal in Goods and Documents, Not Money","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain why Islamic banks are said to 'deal in goods, not money' and what this implies for their risk profile versus conventional banks","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[83]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"A Parallel Salam is a second, independent Salam contract a bank enters into to sell a commodity it purchased under an original Salam contract. For validity, the original and parallel contracts must not be linked or made conditional on each other — each must be honoured independently regardless of the other's performance."},{"id":"q-ch4-14","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why can a lack of transparency in a Murabaha transaction render it non-Shari'ah-compliant?","explanation":"The chapter states that lack of transparency regarding cost/price and payment mode in Murabaha may render the transaction non-Shari'ah-compliant, since Islamic banks' disclosure standards must be more stringent than conventional banks'.","topicId":"t4.2.8","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.8","topicLabel":"Transparency, Documentation, and the Additional Risks Islamic Banks Face","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why Islamic banks are said to 'deal in goods, not money' and what this implies for their risk profile versus conventional banks","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.8","pages":[83,84]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because Murabaha requires no documentation at all","Because Islamic banks are required to provide full details of the cost/price and payment mode — this stringent disclosure requirement is fundamental to Murabaha's validity","Because transparency is only relevant for Ijarah contracts","Because conventional banks have stricter disclosure standards"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-15","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the additional/distinctive risks Islamic banks face, per Chapter 4?","explanation":"Chapter 4 lists asset risk, market risk, Shari'ah non-compliance risk, greater rate-of-return risk, greater fiduciary risk, greater legal risk, and greater withdrawal risk as distinctive additional risks for Islamic banks, arising from their actual asset ownership and Shari'ah compliance requirements.","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","topicLabel":"Transparency, Documentation, and the Additional Risks Islamic Banks Face","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain why Islamic banks are said to 'deal in goods, not money' and what this implies for their risk profile versus conventional banks","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[83,84]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Asset risk","Shari'ah non-compliance risk","Greater withdrawal risk","Currency risk (identical to conventional banks, no difference)","Greater legal risk"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4]},{"id":"q-ch4-16","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Istisna'a manufacturer is running late on delivering a contracted asset. Which risk-mitigation mechanism does Chapter 4 say applies specifically to Istisna'a for this situation, and why is it different from other modes?","explanation":"In Istisna'a, a price-decrease clause (Shart-e-Jazai) is used for delivery delay, rather than the penalty-to-charity clause used in other modes, because manufacturing/construction depends significantly on the manufacturer's personal effort, unlike Murabaha/Salam's simpler deferred-payment liability structure.","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","topicLabel":"Transparency, Documentation, and the Additional Risks Islamic Banks Face","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain why Islamic banks are said to 'deal in goods, not money' and what this implies for their risk profile versus conventional banks","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[85]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A penalty clause with proceeds credited to the bank's own P&L","A price-decrease clause ('Shart-e-Jazai') for the delay, because Istisna'a depends heavily on the manufacturer's personal effort and commitment, who may be handling concurrent contracts","No mitigation is available in Istisna'a","The bank must always cancel the contract"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-17","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the actual issue at stake in the 'debt versus equity' discussion in Chapter 4?","explanation":"The author explicitly states the issue is not 'debt versus equity' as a binary but rather putting greater reliance on equity while subjecting debt (once created) to the rule that it cannot increase based on conventional opportunity-cost theory.","topicId":"t4.3","chapter":4,"section":"4.3","topicLabel":"Debt versus Equity in Islamic Finance","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the debt-versus-equity issue in Islamic finance and why debt-creating modes remain legitimate, not just equity-based ones","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.3","pages":[85]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Whether debt should be entirely eliminated from Islamic finance","A preference for greater reliance on equity where appropriate, plus ensuring debt, once created, cannot increase per conventional opportunity-cost theory","Whether equity financing is prohibited in Islam","Whether banks should only serve wealthy clients"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-18","type":"scenario","prompt":"A risk-averse widow wants to invest savings without exposure to potential loss of principal in a profit-and-loss-sharing business. What should an Islamic bank, acting as trustee, do for her, per Chapter 4?","explanation":"The author uses this exact example to show debt-creating modes (trade, Ijarah) remain necessary for risk-averse investors who cannot bear Shirkah-based loss risk — the bank as trustee must accommodate this.","topicId":"t4.3","chapter":4,"section":"4.3","topicLabel":"Debt versus Equity in Islamic Finance","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the debt-versus-equity issue in Islamic finance and why debt-creating modes remain legitimate, not just equity-based ones","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.3","pages":[85,86]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Refuse to serve her, since Islamic finance only offers Shirkah-based products","Invest her funds in trade and Ijarah-based activities suited to her risk profile, which will generate debt-based returns rather than exposing her to Shirkah loss risk","Force her into a Musharakah regardless of her risk tolerance","Give her an interest-bearing conventional deposit instead"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-19","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Islamic banks are expected to primarily function as charitable institutions providing free loans to the needy.","explanation":"False — this is explicitly identified as a myth. Islamic banks are ordinary businesses with fiduciary duties to depositors; charitable/return-free-loan activity must be funded separately from the bank's own equity or a dedicated Charity Fund.","topicId":"t4.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.4","topicLabel":"Islamic Banking: Business versus Benevolence","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the debt-versus-equity issue in Islamic finance and why debt-creating modes remain legitimate, not just equity-based ones","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.4","pages":[86,87]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch4-20","type":"short","prompt":"From what sources may an Islamic bank fund return-free loans or charitable activity, per Chapter 4?","explanation":"This distinction preserves the bank's fiduciary responsibility toward depositors while still allowing discretionary charitable activity from appropriate sources.","topicId":"t4.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.4","topicLabel":"Islamic Banking: Business versus Benevolence","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the debt-versus-equity issue in Islamic finance and why debt-creating modes remain legitimate, not just equity-based ones","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.4","pages":[86,87]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"From the bank's own equity funds or an accumulated Charity Fund, with the approval of the Shari'ah advisor — never from depositor trust funds meant for the bank's core fiduciary business."},{"id":"q-ch4-21","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the fundamental difference between Islamic and conventional exchange contract rules, per Chapter 4?","explanation":"The chapter states the most strategic difference: conventional finance allows both exchange items to be delayed/deferred and goods/options to be sold without taking ownership; Islamic finance permits only one item to be delayed, and unowned/unpossessed goods cannot be sold.","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.5","topicLabel":"Exchange Rules in Islamic Finance: Currencies, Commodities, and Deferred Settlement Limits","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the Shari'ah exchange rules for currencies and commodities, and explain the concept of time value of money in Islamic finance as distinct from conventional opportunity cost/interest","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Islamic finance allows both items of exchange to be delayed; conventional finance allows neither","In Islamic finance, only ONE item of an exchange contract can be delayed, and goods not owned/possessed cannot be sold; conventional finance allows both items to be deferred and even options resold without ownership","There is no meaningful difference","Islamic finance requires all exchanges to be barter-based"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-22","type":"scenario","prompt":"A trader wants to exchange 1000 Pakistani Rupees for 100 Saudi Riyals, with the Rupees delivered today but the Riyals to be delivered in 30 days. Is this permissible under Bai' al Sarf rules?","explanation":"Per the exchange rules (Imam Nawavi/Bai' al Sarf), when commodities are heterogeneous but share the same 'Illah (as different currencies do, both serving as monetary units), excess/deficiency in amount is permitted, but delay in exchange is prohibited — settlement must occur hand to hand.","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.5","topicLabel":"Exchange Rules in Islamic Finance: Currencies, Commodities, and Deferred Settlement Limits","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"State the Shari'ah exchange rules for currencies and commodities, and explain the concept of time value of money in Islamic finance as distinct from conventional opportunity cost/interest","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[88]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, since the currencies are different, delay is always fine","No — even for different currencies (heterogeneous but same 'Illah as monetary units), excess/deficiency in amount is allowed, but delay in exchange is not allowed; settlement must be hand to hand","Yes, since the amounts are unequal","No, because Rupees and Riyals cannot ever be exchanged"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-23","type":"short","prompt":"Why can't Salam be used for currency-for-currency exchange, per Chapter 4?","explanation":"This is why the forward sale/purchase of currencies via Salam is not a valid contract, despite some scholars' historical suggestions of using Salam in Fulus (inferior-metal coins).","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"section":"4.5","topicLabel":"Exchange Rules in Islamic Finance: Currencies, Commodities, and Deferred Settlement Limits","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the Shari'ah exchange rules for currencies and commodities, and explain the concept of time value of money in Islamic finance as distinct from conventional opportunity cost/interest","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[89]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Because paper money can only serve as the price (Thaman) in a Salam contract, not as the deferred commodity. Salam requires one item (price) immediate and one item (commodity) deferred; deferring both currencies would create a prohibited exchange of debt against debt."},{"id":"q-ch4-24","type":"mcq","prompt":"Under what condition does Shari'ah accept that a commodity's credit price may exceed its cash price?","explanation":"There is near-consensus that a credit price may genuinely exceed a cash price, provided one price is settled/fixed before the parties separate; what remains prohibited is any addition to that agreed price after the debt is created.","topicId":"t4.6","chapter":4,"section":"4.6","topicLabel":"Time Value of Money in Islamic Finance: Pricing, Not Interest","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the Shari'ah exchange rules for currencies and commodities, and explain the concept of time value of money in Islamic finance as distinct from conventional opportunity cost/interest","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[89]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Never — any price difference based on payment timing is prohibited","Provided one fixed price is settled before the contracting parties separate, and no further addition is made once the debt is created","Only if the credit period is less than 30 days","Only for gold and silver transactions"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-25","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does the Islamic concept of 'time value of money' fundamentally differ from the conventional 'opportunity cost'/interest-based concept?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly distinguishes valuation of the credit period for pricing goods/usufructs from conventional 'opportunity cost'/'time value' — Islamic finance accepts time value only within genuine trade/lease pricing, never as a direct charge on a loan or debt.","topicId":"t4.6","chapter":4,"section":"4.6","topicLabel":"Time Value of Money in Islamic Finance: Pricing, Not Interest","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"State the Shari'ah exchange rules for currencies and commodities, and explain the concept of time value of money in Islamic finance as distinct from conventional opportunity cost/interest","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[90]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["There is no difference; they are the same concept with different names","The Islamic concept recognizes time value only through the pricing of a genuine trade/lease of goods (e.g. credit vs cash price); it is never a direct charge on money/a loan itself, unlike conventional interest","The Islamic concept applies interest at a lower rate","The conventional concept only applies to gold and silver"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-26","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Because paper money has no intrinsic value, Shari'ah rules on Riba and Zakat do not fully apply to it.","explanation":"False. Per Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench and the OIC Fiqh Council (1986), paper money is treated as 'real money' with all characteristics of value, fully subject to the same Shari'ah rules (Riba, Zakat, Salam, etc.) that applied to gold and silver.","topicId":"t4.7.1","chapter":4,"section":"4.7.1","topicLabel":"Money and Monetary Policy from the Islamic Perspective: Paper Money, Currency Trading, Money Creation, and Inflation","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain the Islamic perspective on the status of paper money, money creation, and inflation-linked debt settlement","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.1","pages":[91]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch4-27","type":"mcq","prompt":"What defining feature distinguishes Islamic finance's approach to money/credit creation from the conventional system?","explanation":"The chapter identifies real-asset-backing as a defining feature: unlike conventional finance, Islamic banks cannot create money out of nothing, and cannot finance government deficits absent sufficient real government assets or Shari'ah-compliant securitized debt.","topicId":"t4.7.3","chapter":4,"section":"4.7.3","topicLabel":"Money and Monetary Policy from the Islamic Perspective: Paper Money, Currency Trading, Money Creation, and Inflation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the Islamic perspective on the status of paper money, money creation, and inflation-linked debt settlement","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.3","pages":[92]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Islamic finance allows unlimited money creation via ledger entries","Financial instruments must be real-asset-based; Islamic banks cannot create money out of nothing or without real-asset backing","Islamic finance has no monetary policy at all","Islamic banks can freely finance any government budget deficit"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-28","type":"short","prompt":"What do all Islamic economists agree on, per Chapter 4, regarding interest versus credit creation as the cause of harmful trade cycles?","explanation":"Despite disagreement between 100%-reserve advocates and credit-creation defenders among Islamic economists, all agree interest's role within a credit system (not credit creation itself) is the real problem behind trade cycles.","topicId":"t4.7.3","chapter":4,"section":"4.7.3","topicLabel":"Money and Monetary Policy from the Islamic Perspective: Paper Money, Currency Trading, Money Creation, and Inflation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the Islamic perspective on the status of paper money, money creation, and inflation-linked debt settlement","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.3","pages":[93,94]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"That interest itself is the real villain, not credit creation as such — measured, interest-free credit/money creation may not harm the financial/payment system, and abolishing interest would substantially curtail the harmful features of credit creation."},{"id":"q-ch4-29","type":"scenario","prompt":"A debt of 10 Saudi Riyals is created on 1 July, payable 31 December. By 31 December, the Riyal has depreciated significantly relative to other currencies. How much must the debtor pay, per Chapter 4's rules?","explanation":"Per Qur'an 2:279 (Nass) and the chapter's discussion, a debt must be repaid in exactly the same currency and quantity originally owed, regardless of inflation/depreciation — indexation to compensate for currency value changes is rejected.","topicId":"t4.7.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","topicLabel":"Money and Monetary Policy from the Islamic Perspective: Paper Money, Currency Trading, Money Creation, and Inflation","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the Islamic perspective on the status of paper money, money creation, and inflation-linked debt settlement","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","pages":[94,95]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["An inflation-adjusted amount to compensate the creditor for the Riyal's depreciation","Exactly 10 Saudi Riyals — the debt must be repaid in the exact original currency and amount, regardless of any change in its value","Nothing, since the debt is voided by currency depreciation","An amount determined by the exchange rate on 1 July, the original contract date"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch4-30","type":"short","prompt":"What is Justice Taqi Usmani's practical (not purely textual) objection to using debt indexation as a substitute for interest in banking?","explanation":"This is a distinctly practical, banking-operations argument, separate from the purely textual (Nass-based) objection that indexation contradicts Qur'an 2:279's principal-only rule.","topicId":"t4.7.4","chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","topicLabel":"Money and Monetary Policy from the Islamic Perspective: Paper Money, Currency Trading, Money Creation, and Inflation","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the Islamic perspective on the status of paper money, money creation, and inflation-linked debt settlement","source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","pages":[95,96]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Indexation would give a bank's borrowers and depositors the identical inflation-based rate on both sides of its balance sheet (since both are measured by the same inflation index), leaving nothing for the bank itself — and no bank can be run without a profit."}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f4.1","front":"Loan vs investment (Islamic context)","back":"Loan: monetary transaction with full repayment and no return — not investment. Investment: funds become part of real activity; return permissible.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t4.2","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2","pages":[73,74]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.2","front":"Table 4.1 — which modes are liquid?","back":"Ijarah and sharing modes (Musharakah, Mudarabah); credit sales, Salam and Istisna‘a are non-liquid (receivables cannot be sold).","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,78]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.3","front":"Rate of return in Salam and Istisna‘a","back":"Basically unknown (sale price on delivery uncertain), but can be partly known via a parallel contract or promise to purchase.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,78]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.4","front":"Are all pre-fixed returns Riba?","back":"No. Fixed returns are permissible in trade (credit price) and Ijarah (rent) if Shari’ah rules are met; any increase on loans/debts is Riba.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,81]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.5","front":"Two benchmarks needed in Islamic finance","back":"A price (mark-up/rent) reference scale and a sharing-ratio reference scale (e.g. inter-bank or central bank Mudarabah ratio).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,81]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.6","front":"Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman / Al Ghunm bil Ghurm","back":"Entitlement to profit goes with liability for loss — the criterion of legality of any return on capital.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t4.2.6","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[81,82]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.7","front":"Additional risks of Islamic banks","back":"Asset, market and Shari’ah non-compliance risks; greater rate-of-return, fiduciary, legal and withdrawal risks.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[84,85]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.8","front":"Shart-e-Jazai","back":"Price-reduction clause for delay in delivery — allowed in Istisna‘a (not in Murabaha/Salam, where penalties go to charity).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[84,85]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.9","front":"Al Ujrah wal Dhaman La Tajtami‘an","back":"Rent/wage and liability do not combine: a lessee paying rent cannot also bear the asset’s ownership risk.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[84,85]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.10","front":"Key exchange rule in Islamic vs conventional finance","back":"Islamic: only one counter-value may be deferred; no sale of goods not owned/possessed. Conventional: both can be deferred.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87,89]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.11","front":"Forward FX cover in Islamic banking","back":"Only as a promise to exchange at an agreed rate for real trade needs; actual exchange of both currencies simultaneous. Salam in currencies is invalid.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87,89]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.12","front":"Time value of money in Islamic finance","back":"Recognised only in pricing goods/usufruct (credit price > cash price if fixed before separation; Salam price < spot). No addition to a debt once created.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t4.6","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[89,90]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.13","front":"OIC Fiqh Council 1986 on paper money","back":"Paper money is real money, possessing all characteristics of value, subject to rules governing gold and silver (Riba, Zakat, Salam, etc.).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.7.1","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.1","pages":[91,91]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.14","front":"OIC Fiqh Council 1988 on currency fluctuation","back":"A fixed debt is repaid in its own currency; it is not permitted to attach fixed debts to currency fluctuation.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.7.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","pages":[94,96]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.15","front":"Business vs benevolence","back":"Banks hold deposits in trust; they must do business (trade, Ijarah, services). Free loans only from own equity/charity fund without harming depositors.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t4.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.4","pages":[86,86]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.16","front":"Debt vs equity — the author’s view","back":"Not a prohibition of debt modes but a preference for equity; aim for a healthy balance; debt must not carry interest.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t4.3","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.3","pages":[85,86]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.17","front":"Loan vs investment — the government bond test","back":"Buying a bond or placing a conventional deposit is a financial transaction, not investment; investment requires real activity such as buying goods to sell at a profit.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t4.2","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2","pages":[73,74]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.18","front":"Security in PLS modes","back":"Can be taken only against non-performance, misconduct or negligence — not enforced for honest business loss.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,78]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.19","front":"Hind bint Utbah precedent","back":"Borrowed 4,000 from the treasury to trade, lost it; Umar refused to waive the loan — debts are repaid regardless of business outcome.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,78]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.20","front":"Wakalatul Istismar","back":"Investment agency: bank manages clients’ funds for a fixed fee; all profit/loss belongs to the clients.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t4.2.7","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[82,83]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.21","front":"Parallel Salam — independence rule","back":"Must not be linked to or conditional on the first Salam; it must be honoured even if the first fails.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t4.2.7","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[82,83]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.22","front":"Khan and Habib (2001) survey","back":"17 IFIs: PLS deposit risks seen as more serious; fear of withdrawal if returns lag; PLS and Salam/Istisna seen as riskier than Murabaha/Ijarah.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[84,85]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.23","front":"Currency transfers (OIC 1995)","back":"A customary delay in crediting another currency is allowed, but the beneficiary cannot deal in it until fully credited.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87,89]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.24","front":"Why currency Salam is invalid","back":"Paper money is price, not a commodity; deferring the “commodity” currency means a deferred currency exchange, which requires spot settlement on both sides.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87,89]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.25","front":"Time preference in an Islamic economy","back":"Positive, reflected in pricing of goods (credit and Salam prices); no basis for assuming zero time preference.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t4.6","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[89,90]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.26","front":"Three sources of monetary expansion","back":"Central-bank financing of deficits (major); secondary credit creation by banks; exogenous factors (e.g. capital inflows).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t4.7.3","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.3","pages":[92,94]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f4.27","front":"Debt in a currency that becomes unavailable","back":"Pay its counter value at the rate on the due date (e.g. SR 10 due 31 December paid in Rupees at that date’s rate).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.7.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","pages":[94,96]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-1","front":"Distinguish 'interest-free banking' from 'Islamic banking'.","back":"'Interest-free banking' (1980s term) is narrower — describing instruments that avoid interest. 'Islamic banking' is broader: it must also avoid Gharar and other unethical practices, and actively participate in achieving an Islamic economy's goals.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t4.1","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.1","pages":[73]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-2","front":"Why is a loan NOT considered 'investment' in the Islamic framework, per Chapter 4?","back":"A loan is a purely monetary/financial transaction — funds change hands with a guarantee of full repayment and no return to the creditor. Investment requires becoming part of real activity or being itself real activity (e.g. buying real goods/services and reselling at a profit).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t4.1","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.1","pages":[73,74]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-3","front":"Name three specific banking practices Islamic banks must avoid under the interest prohibition, per Chapter 4.","back":"Charging fees on lending as 'cost of funds'; charging rent on money; selling debt instruments at a premium or discount.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t4.2.1","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.1","pages":[74,75]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-4","front":"Why are conventional futures/options markets deemed un-Islamic, and what would make ordinary stock market trading acceptable?","back":"Conventional futures/options markets combine Gharar, interest and gambling elements. Ordinary stock market transactions would be Islamic 'if cleansed of' these elements — the prohibition targets specific practices (speculation, undisclosed uncertainty), not equity markets as such.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t4.2.2","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.2","pages":[75,76]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-5","front":"Define Mudarabah and state how profit and loss are allocated.","back":"A partnership where one party provides capital and the other provides entrepreneurial skill/management. Loss is borne solely by the financier (capital provider); profit is shared per a pre-agreed ratio.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,77]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-6","front":"In the Hind bint-e Utbah precedent (Caliph Umar), why couldn't her trading loan be waived after she suffered a loss?","back":"Because it was a loan from the public exchequer, and a debt obligation from a credit/loan transaction is independent of the borrower's subsequent business outcome — the debt must be repaid regardless of profit or loss.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[76,77]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-7","front":"Per Table 4.1, classify Murabaha, Ijarah and Musharakah by liquidity and return predictability.","back":"Murabaha (debt-creating): non-liquid, collateral, known return. Ijarah (semi-debt): liquid, collateral, known return. Musharakah (sharing mode): liquid, penalty-for-misconduct guarantee, unknown return.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t4.2.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.4","pages":[77]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-8","front":"Explain the 'All Pre-fixed Returns are not Riba' principle.","back":"The Shari'ah test for whether a return is permissible is the nature of the underlying transaction (sale/lease vs loan/debt), not whether the return is fixed. A fixed trade profit margin or fixed Ijarah rent is valid; any addition on a loan/debt (fixed or not) is Riba.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,79]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-9","front":"Why is the analogy 'if fixed Ijarah rent is allowed, fixed loan interest should be too' rejected?","back":"Ijarah rent reflects an asset's inherently uncertain usufruct-generating capacity over its economic life (uncertain until the lease ends), and only non-consumable assets (not money) can be leased — the lessor must be able to bear ownership-risk on an asset that continues to exist.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[79]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-10","front":"How many benchmark/reference-rate types does Islamic finance need, versus conventional finance, and why?","back":"Islamic finance needs TWO: a price (markup/rent) reference scale for debt/semi-debt contracts, and a sharing-ratio reference scale for equity contracts. Conventional finance needs only ONE (the interest rate), since all its financing is debt-based.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[80,81]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-11","front":"State the maxim 'Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman' / 'Al Ghunm bil Ghurm' and its role in Islamic finance.","back":"The Shari'ah maxim that entitlement to profit/gain (Ghunm) must be matched by liability for loss/responsibility (Ghurm/Daman) — this is the universal criterion for the legality of any return on capital.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t4.2.6","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[81]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-12","front":"In a trade (Bai') transaction, when does risk transfer from seller to buyer, and what obligation does the buyer retain regardless?","back":"Risk transfers to the buyer once the sale is executed. The buyer must then pay the settled price at the agreed time even if the asset is later destroyed (though he may separately insure via Takaful — this doesn't affect his payment obligation).","category":"Risk","topicId":"t4.2.6","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[82]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-13","front":"Contrast how conventional banks and Islamic banks are described in terms of what they 'deal in'.","back":"Conventional banks 'deal in documents, not goods' — pure financiers with no ownership/risk in underlying assets. Islamic banks 'deal in goods and documents, not money' — they must take genuine ownership and bear real asset risk before earning profit.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t4.2.7","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[82]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-14","front":"Define Wakalatul Istismar.","back":"A fund-management agency arrangement where the bank manages a client's investment for a fixed management fee; all profit/loss belongs to the client, and the bank earns only its fixed service fee — never a fee purely for lending.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t4.2.7","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[83]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-15","front":"List the distinctive additional risks Islamic banks face beyond conventional banking risks.","back":"Asset risk, market risk, Shari'ah non-compliance risk, greater rate-of-return risk, greater fiduciary risk, greater legal risk, and greater withdrawal risk.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[83,84]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-16","front":"State the juristic rule 'Al Ujrah wal Dhaman La Tajtami'an' and its application in Ijarah.","back":"'Wage/rent and liability/responsibility do not combine' — an Ijarah lessor who bears asset-related liability (risk of loss) cannot also shift that same risk onto the lessee while still collecting rent.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[85]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-17","front":"How does default-risk mitigation in Istisna'a differ from other modes?","back":"Most modes use a penalty clause (proceeds to charity) as a deterrent. Istisna'a instead uses a price-decrease clause for delivery delay ('Shart-e-Jazai'), because Istisna'a depends heavily on the manufacturer's personal effort/commitment across potentially concurrent contracts.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t4.2.9","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.9","pages":[85]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-18","front":"Is Islamic finance 'equity-only'? What does Chapter 4 say about debt?","back":"No — this is a myth. Debt-creating modes (Murabaha, Salam, Ijarah) remain fully legitimate; the real issue is a preference for equity where suitable, plus ensuring debt (once created) can never increase like conventional interest-bearing debt. Debt has always existed, including for the Prophet himself.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t4.3","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.3","pages":[85,86]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-19","front":"Why does Chapter 4 use the example of a risk-averse widow?","back":"To illustrate that Shirkah-based (equity) modes are unsuitable for some investors — she cannot bear loss risk, so the bank, as trustee, must invest her funds in trade/Ijarah-based activities, which inherently generate debt, showing debt-creating modes are necessary, not just tolerated.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.3","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.3","pages":[85,86]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-20","front":"What myth about Islamic banks does Chapter 4's 'Business versus Benevolence' section dispel?","back":"That Islamic banks should function as charities/social-security centres providing mainly free loans. In reality, business and benevolence are separate — banks hold depositors' money as a trust and cannot dole it out at will; charity must come from the bank's own equity or a dedicated Charity Fund.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t4.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.4","pages":[86,87]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-21","front":"What is the core Islamic exchange rule that differs fundamentally from conventional finance?","back":"In Islamic finance, only ONE side of an exchange contract may be delayed (unlike conventional finance, where both sides can be deferred); goods not owned or possessed cannot be sold.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-22","front":"What did the OIC Fiqh Council's 1998 (11th session) resolution say about currency sales?","back":"It is not permissible in Shari'ah to sell currencies by deferred sale, nor to fix a future date for exchanging them — evidenced by Qur'an, Sunnah and Ijma'. It also recommended Muslim governments regulate currency markets per Shari'ah as a safeguard against financial crises.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[87,88]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-23","front":"Distinguish the permitted vs forbidden forms of currency futures, per Chapter 4.","back":"Forbidden: one currency delivered on the spot, the other delayed. Permitted: a non-speculative 'promise' to exchange both currencies simultaneously at a previously agreed rate for genuine trade needs — the actual exchange remains simultaneous when executed.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t4.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.5","pages":[88,89]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-24","front":"Explain the Islamic concept of 'time value of money' and its scope limit.","back":"A commodity's credit price may legitimately exceed its cash price, provided one fixed price is settled before the parties separate (accepted, per near-consensus and clear Shari'ah text). But this applies ONLY to genuine trade/lease pricing of goods — NEVER to loans, debts, or monetary exchange, where money has no time value.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t4.6","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[89,90]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-25","front":"In Salam, does the advance-paid price exceed or fall below the equivalent cash-and-carry price? Why?","back":"It falls below (is genuinely LESS than) the cash-and-carry price at contract execution — a discount for prepayment, illustrating time value manifesting inversely compared to a credit sale.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.6","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[90]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-26","front":"Per the OIC Fiqh Council (1986) and Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench, how is paper/fiat money treated under Shari'ah?","back":"Exactly like gold and silver were treated historically — as 'real money' possessing all characteristics of value, fully subject to Shari'ah rules on Riba, Zakat, Salam and other transactions, despite having no intrinsic value.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.7.1","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.1","pages":[91]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-27","front":"Why can't Islamic banks finance government budget deficits, per Chapter 4?","back":"Because Islamic finance requires financial instruments to be real-asset-based — Islamic banks cannot create money 'out of nothing'. They cannot finance deficits until governments have sufficient real assets to raise funds in a Shari'ah-compliant manner, or convert debt stock into Shari'ah-compliant securities.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t4.7.3","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.3","pages":[92]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch4-28","front":"Why does Chapter 4 reject indexing debts to inflation, and what practical objection does Justice Taqi Usmani add?","back":"The clear Qur'anic text (Nass, 2:279) permits recovery of only the principal — Ijtihad cannot override clear Nass. Usmani adds: indexation would give a bank's borrowers and depositors the identical inflation-based rate, leaving no profit margin for the bank itself — 'no bank can be run without a profit.'","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t4.7.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","pages":[95,96]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e4.1","kind":"long","prompt":"“All pre-fixed returns are not Riba.” Discuss with reference to sale, Hibah, loan and Ijarah.","expectedStructure":["Introduce the nature-of-transaction test","Bai‘: ownership, risk transfer; credit price; no re-pricing","Hibah: free permanent transfer","Loan/Riba al-Nasiah vs Qard al Hasan","Ijarah: rent with ownership risk; why the rent–interest analogy fails","Conclusion: fixed profit in trade/leasing; nothing over principal in loans/debts"],"keyConcepts":["Bai‘","Ijarah","Qard","time value","ownership risk"],"essentialPoints":["Credit price vs cash price","No Murabaha rollover","Money cannot be leased","Usufruct uncertain"],"commonMistakes":["Saying Islamic finance requires all returns to be variable"],"topicId":"t4.2.5","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.5","pages":[78,81]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e4.2","kind":"short","prompt":"Explain the maxim Al Kharaj bi-al-Daman and its implications for loans, sale and Ijarah.","expectedStructure":["State the maxim","Loans: no profit","Trade: risk transfer on sale","Ijarah: lessor bears ownership risk","Risk mitigation vs elimination"],"keyConcepts":["Al Ghunm bil Ghurm","ownership risk"],"essentialPoints":["Cannot transfer risk without reward"],"commonMistakes":["Suggesting risk can be fully eliminated"],"topicId":"t4.2.6","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.6","pages":[81,82]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e4.3","kind":"difference","prompt":"Differentiate between conventional banks and Islamic banks in terms of what they deal in and the risks they bear.","expectedStructure":["Conventional: money and documents","Islamic: goods and assets","Mode-by-mode risk","Additional risks"],"keyConcepts":["asset risk","Parallel Salam","Wakalatul Istismar"],"essentialPoints":["Banks deal in documents not goods (conventional)","Islamic banks take ownership"],"commonMistakes":["Ignoring fee-based income rules"],"topicId":"t4.2.7","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.2.7","pages":[82,83]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e4.4","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"Does Islamic finance recognise the time value of money? Explain.","expectedStructure":["Credit vs cash price consensus","Salam price","Spot exchange of money","No time value on loans/debts","Positive time preference"],"keyConcepts":["time value","mark-up","opportunity cost"],"essentialPoints":["One price fixed before separation","No addition after debt created"],"commonMistakes":["Claiming Islam denies time value entirely"],"topicId":"t4.6","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[89,90]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e4.5","kind":"viva","prompt":"Can an Islamic bank index a Murabaha receivable to inflation?","expectedStructure":["No — Nass 2:279","OIC 1988","Floating only for future periods in Ijarah"],"keyConcepts":["indexation"],"essentialPoints":["Due-date exchange rule"],"commonMistakes":["Confusing floating Ijarah rent for future periods with indexation of accrued debt"],"topicId":"t4.7.4","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.4","pages":[94,96]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e4.6","kind":"long","prompt":"Explain the status of money in Islamic finance. How does it affect currency trading, money creation and the settlement of debts?","expectedStructure":["Money as Thaman: medium of exchange and measure of value","Paper money rulings (SAB; OIC 1986)","Currency trading: Bai‘ al Sarf; OIC 1998; forward cover by promise","Money creation: real-asset basis; sources of expansion; credit debate","Debt settlement: no indexation; OIC 1988; unavailable currency rule"],"keyConcepts":["Thaman","Bai‘ al Sarf","derivative deposits","indexation","OIC Fiqh Council"],"essentialPoints":["Same currency: equal and spot","Different currencies: spot, any rate","Money supply neither excessive nor inadequate","Debts repaid in the same currency and amount"],"commonMistakes":["Treating currency as a Salam commodity","Allowing indexation of accrued debt","Confusing floating rentals with indexation"],"topicId":"t4.7.1","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.7.1","pages":[91,91]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-17","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain how Islamic finance treats the time value of money, and how this differs from the conventional treatment.","expectedStructure":["State that time value is not recognized for pricing money/debt itself","State where it is recognized (pricing goods/usufruct in trade or lease)","Contrast with conventional interest, which prices money directly over time"],"keyConcepts":["Time value of money","Riba","Pricing goods vs pricing money"],"essentialPoints":["Islamic finance does not accept charging extra for the mere passage of time on a loan or debt — that would be Riba.","Time is, however, a legitimate factor in pricing goods or usufruct: a trader may charge a higher credit price than a cash price, and a lessor may price rentals over a lease term, because these are trade/lease transactions, not loans of money.","Once a price or receivable is fixed under a valid contract (e.g. a Murabaha sale price), it cannot be increased later merely because payment is delayed."],"commonMistakes":["Claiming Islamic finance rejects the time value of money altogether — the book distinguishes pricing goods/usufruct (permitted) from pricing money/debt (prohibited).","Confusing a legitimate higher credit price at the point of sale with a prohibited increase for late payment after the price is already fixed."],"topicId":"t4.6","chapter":4,"source":{"chapter":4,"section":"4.6","pages":[89]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
